      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0003" n="art0003">
        <p>Vhvineeday, Oct. 5, 2.30 p.m.</p>
        <p>To be OPENED by The Hon, MRS. J. EMMOTT BARLOW. Chairman, Sir. J. T. BRUNNER, Bart, M.P. Supported by 3. EMMOTT BARLOW, Esq. M.P., A. H. CROSFIELD, Esq., and others.</p>
        <p>Thursday, Oct. 6, (Ladies' Day) .30 p.m.</p>
        <p>To be OPENRD by MRS. CHAS. N. NICHOLSON. THE CHLIZ WILL BE TAKEN BY MRS, ZIBA. ARMITAGE, Supported by CHAS. N. iSICHOLSON, Esq., LIBERAL CANDIDATE FOR DONCASTER DIVISION.</p>
        <p>Friday, Oct. 7, 2.30 p.m.</p>
        <p>To be OPENED by W. H. LEVER. Esq.; Chairman, CHAS. B. PARKER, ESQ., Supported by A. J. KING, ESQ., LIBERAL CANDIDATE FOR KNIITSFORD DIVIAION.</p>
        <p>UNIQUE ATTRACTIONS AND ENTERTAINMENTS, Side-shows, Concerts. Glees, Instrumental music, and Organ Recitals. THE GREAT SID MACAIRE, Society Mystical merry-maker, Ventriloquist and Court Conjuror. KHAN TELMA!, The Oriental Impressionist and delineator of !character.</p>
        <p>H.M. THE QUERN OF SHEBA, Iliac will receive visitors in the gorgeous Treasure Temple.</p>
        <p>"FLORA'S HOLIDAY," Llharnaing Musical sketth, Entirely new to Warrington. „q)ecially prepared for the Bazaar by Mr. W. H. Payton Niue. 'me., assisted by the Misses Arline Harrison, M. W. Collin arid Messrs. A. Griffiths and E. Pownall. VENTRILOQUIAL AND COLLOQUIAL CSRIOS. MARIONETTES EXHIBITION °I Lilliputian artistes, including Jugglers, Equilibrists, and Humorists. Fairy Fish Pond, Shooting Gallery, and KUMKIZMI, THE TOKIO MYSTERY. Musical Programme Constantly Changiag. SOLO ARTISTES, the Misses M. 'leash%ll, Woodland, Houghton, Bouchier, Birchall, Thompson, Mrs. Holmes, Mrs. Willett. Messrs. Pownall. George,Grifilths,Hassall, H. M. Ashton, H. Mosely, and Whittaker. QUARTETTES: The Lyric Prize Quartette, the Warring- ton Male Quartette and the Apollo Male Quartette. Instrumental Music each day by /tIASTER TOM BARTON'S JUVENILE ORCHESTRA and MISS B. TURNER'S MANDOLINE BAND. ROAN RECITAL each day by Mr. W. H. Payton,Mus. Bac.</p>
        <p>PRICES OF ADMISSION : Season Tickets (not transferable) 3s. 6d. Wednesday, up to 6 o'clock 2s. 84. after 6 o'clock is. ,Thursday. all day \Friday, up to 6 o'clock C. JACKSON HOLMICS, Treasurer. EDMUND OSBORNE E. V. WALKER. ' t Hon. Secretaries</p>
        <p>AWONDERFUL DISCOVERY.—This is the acre of research and experiment, when all nature, so t'to speak, is ransacked by the scientific for the oomfoet and happiness of man. Science has indeed made giant strides during the past century, and among the—by no means least important—discoveries in medicine comes that of • THHICAPION. This preparation is unquestionably one of the most genuine and reliable Patent Medicines ever introduced, and has, we understand, been used in the Continental Hospital+ by Ricord, Rostan, Jobert, Velpeau, 14faisonneuve, th e well- known Chassaignac, and indeed by all who are regarded as authorities in such matters, including the celebrated Lallemand, and Roux, by whom it was some time since uniformly adopted, and that it is worthy the attention of those who require such a remedy we think there is no doubt. From the time of Aristotle downwards, a potent agent in the remo7al of these diseases has (like the famed philosopher's stone) been the object of search of some hopeful generous minds; and far beyond the mere power—if such could ever have been discovered—of transmuting the baser metals into gold is timely the discovery of a remedy so potent as to replenish the failing energies in the one case, and in the other so effectually, speedily and safely to expel from the system the poisons of acquired or inherited disease in all their protean forms as to leave no taint or trace behind. Such is the new French Remedy THRRAPION, which may certainly rank with, if not take precellence of, manv of the discoveries of our day, about which no little ostentation and noise have been made, and the extensive and ever-increasing demand that has been created for this medicine wherever introduced appears to prove that it is destined to cast into oblivion all those questionable remedies that were formerly he sole reliance of medical men. Therapion may be obtained tf the principal chemists and merchants throughout the world.—" Dr- amond Fzeldt A dvertiTer, ," Kintebrrl ey. . —For particulars of Therapion,send stamped, addressed envelope to MR. R. JOHNSON, 43, HOLFORD SQ., LONDON. W.O</p>
        <p>OLLESTER.i S I'REE I:, LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>INVOICES for Grocers, Shoemakers, Cl ogger Butchers. Bakers. Plumbers, Publicans, ac.</p>
        <p>EXAMINRR OFFICB, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>ittarbintra, etc.</p>
