      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>BENEFITS OF SCIENCE.</head>
        <p>THE BENEFITS OF SCIENCE.</p>
        <p>It is seldom that any discovery of science is such as -will benefit all classes of people. Some of the greatest discoveries of the age, while they have proved directly beneficial to thousands, have also, at least for a time, injured other thousands. Even some of the best medi- cinal preparations, however much they may do to remove one class of disease, or suit some constitutions, at the same time either deepen the hold of another disease, or leave it entirely untonch?d, or if they benefit one class of patients, they may inpre. another. Now the great need of the age is some scivtifically arranged preparation which will cope effectually with the pre- valent diseases of this country, which will be certain to do good when fairly tried, which will be equally adopted to the needs of the merchant and the workman, the professional man, and he who wins his bread by the sweat of his brow, the student, the clerk, the factory hand, the miner, and the roadside labourer. It should be, too, such a pre paration as contains.no injurious ingredients, and which may be taken with Impunity by the weakly child, or the delicate lady, as well as by the stronger constituted man. Such a discovery would deserve to be called The Perfection of Medicinal Preparations, and would indeed be an Invaluable Boon to Suffering Humanity. Now this much needed boon has been found, has been tried, has been proved, and wherever proved, it has been found satisfactory. We refer to Gwilym' Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic, which when once tried, has been always recommended, and has proved successful when all other medicine has failed to give ielief, and we may say further, that it has proved per- Thanently beneficial, when other preparations at best gave only temporary relief. It is strongly recommended as The Best Remedy of the Age for Indigestion •in its different forms, such as Sick Headache, Pains in the Side, Giddiness, Loss of Appetite, also for Nervousness and KsTervous Disorders, Sleeplessness, Neuralgia, Low Spirits, and all kinds of Weakness. It has often proved vory beneficial to persons suffering from great Weakness, either after an illness, long con llnement to ill-ventilated rooms or any othe cause. it strikes at the source of the Disease, removing the cause of the illness, and strengthens those parts of the system which have been weakened by it, and therefore most liable to colds and other ailments. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters being purely vegetable, it is a Natural Tonic, and suited to all agos at all seasons of the year. It is everywhere acknowledged to be a powerful aid in restoring the body to a healthy state when deranged by disease. If you suspect that your health is beginning to fail, brace up your nerves, and fortify your constitution by taking now a course of this excellent Tonic, which is sold everywhere in bottles 2s. 9d. and 4s. 6d. each, but should any difficulty be experienced in procuring it, the Proprietors will forward same for the above prices, carriage free. Avoid Imitations. When purchasing,see the name, " Gwilym Evans," on Label, Stamp, and Bottle: Sole Proprietors: Quinine Bitters Manufac- turing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.—Before Mr. Birchen in the chair), Alderman Bennett,Mesars. R. T. Fairclough and A. J.</p>
        <p>Jolley. A TICKETLESS PASSENGER..—Joseph Bates, last from Manchester, was charged with travelling on the London and North-Western Railway from Manchester to War- rington without having paid his fare and with intent to defraud the company.—Prisoner pleaded guilty.—On being asked for his ticket, prisoner seplied that he had not got one, neither had he any money with which to pay his fare. When asked where hp lived he said he had no settled addre,ss.—Superintendent Monaghan said Bates was charged with a similar offence in June last, and had been three times convicted at Manchester and once at Bury and Ratcliffe.—The magistrates imposed a fine of 20s. and costs or a month's imprisonmnt in de- fault of payment. - -</p>
        <p>- MONDA.Y.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman! Smethurst, Councillors Wilkinson, Thornton, and Tinnion Messrs. T. Birchall, J. W. Hayward, S. Wright, W. Roberts, and G. Pilling. THE BEGGING NUISANCE.—Thomas Mitchell was charged with begging in Bridge-street at 8.30 on Satur- day evening.—The Chief Constable said the proceedings were the result of complaints made to the police by prominent tradesmen in Bridge-street about men and women of a certain character standing about the end of Sbip-yard,following respectable foot-passengers,begging for coppers for drink, and otherwise annoying them. He (Mr. Talbot) had personally suffered annoyance of that kind. Some people bitterly resented it and the police had been paying special attention toithe complaints.—Detective]Clough said prisoner was patrolling from the end of Ship-yard to the entry adjoin- ing the Lion Hotel. In that short distance witness saw him stop about twelve persons. He asked one young man to give him coppers for a pint of beer as well as some tobacco. He told other passers-by that he was a valiant soldier and had fought in South Africa.— Prisoner, who made his seventeenth appearance, was sentenced to a month's hard labour. A MONTH FOR VAGRANCY.—WiIIiam Mulligan was sentenced to a month's hard labour for having been found sleeping in a drying shed at Messrs. Waring's tannery, Winwick-road, on Sunday morning.—P.C. 3luskett proved the case, and a foreman named Higham said he had been instructed to press the case, as the offence might have had serious consequences. The con- tents of the shed were of a most inflammable nature. AN ADJOURNED CASE.—Mr. Browne called the atten- tion of the Bench to the case of Bridget Nevin, Winwick*oad, which was adjourned to enable her to go to Ireland or to a home. Mrs. Nevin, he said, had made no attempt to carry out her promise, and she was as bad as ever on Saturday. The husband now stated that she had left the house, and had probably gone to a son who was living irt Wales.—A further adjournment for a week was granted. OLD OFFENDER.—Mary Broderick, 4, Dallam-lane, was charged with having been drunk and disorderly in Foundry-street on September 19th.—Defendant com- plained that she had been annoyed by neighbours, whereupon the Chief Constable remarked that she was a scourge and curse in any neighbourhood. That was her 37th appearance, and she had been fined so recently as September 9th. She had been before the Court five times within eighteen months for drunkenness and similar offences. She really ought to be in an inebri- ates' home, as she was not responsible for her actions when she got drink.—Prisoner was sent to gaol for a month. DISORDER DI OLD ROAD.—William Hill and Mary Hill, Welch's Yard, were summoned for disorderly behaviour in Old Road on September 17th. Evidence was given by Mr. Burdett to the effect that the defend- ants quarrelled, and used very filthy language.—They were bound over to keep the peace, and ordered to pay the costs (9s. 6d. each). INDECENT LINGUAGE.—Patrick Burke, School Brow, was fined ss. and costs for making use of indecent language within hearing of School Brew on September. 18th: He made his 52nd appearance. CHIMNEY ON FIRE.—For allowing the chimney of his house to be on fire, James Cosgrove, 4, Wharf street, as lined Is. - - - -</p>
        <p>TUESDAY.—Before Dr. Fox (in the chair), Alderman Hutchinson, Councillor Thornton, Messrs. W. Roberts, J. Rymer Young, and W. Richardson. ARREARS OF MAINTENA.NCE.—Thomas Brown was charged on a warrant with arrears of maintenance, due to his wife, amounting, with the costs, to .£2. ss. 6d.— He was ordered to pay the money or go to gaol for a month.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.—Before Messrs. J. R. Young (in the chair),</p>
        <p>W. Roberts and F. W. Monis: Bairey and Charles Wilmot were charged with sleeping out at Froghall sidings at 9.50 on Wednesday night.—George Davies, a detective in the employ of the London and North-Western Rail- way, Company, said he found the prisoners sleeping in a brake van. He roused them and they stated that they wanted a rest.—They were sent to goal for seven days. FRlDAY.—Befere the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman Smethurst, Councillors 'Roberts, Thornton, and Tinnion, Messrs. A. J. Jolley, G. Pilling, C. S.Bnrgess. AssAn-vr CASES.—James Ridgway, 6 Heaton Square, was summoned for assaulting Sarah Annie Lythgoe, 5, Pilkington's-yard, on September 24th.—Complainant said defendant struck her a violent blow in the mouth with his flst.—A fine of ss. and costs was imposed.— John Little, Winwick-road, was charged with assaulting his wife, Annie Little, on September 27th.—Complain- ant said her husband came home drunk and because the dinner =Snot to his liking he kicked her and used abusive language.—Afterwards he seized her by the hair, and holding her head on the table with one hand he thumped her with the other. He then threw her on the floor, placed his foot on her stomach, and threatened to kill her.—Defendant was lined 10s. and costs and as his wife stated that he had continually illused her for the past twenty years, a separation order was granted with 7s. 6d. per week alimony. UNHAPPY MARRIED LlFE.—Edward William Robin- son, Hoyle-street, was summoned for persistent cruelty</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>AGAIE, AND AGAIN, AND AGAIN.</head>
        <p>AGAIE, AND AGAIN, AND AGAIN.</p>
        <p>Again we ask you to drink Vi-Cocoa. Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa is neither a medicine nor a mere thirst-assuager. It is a food at the same time that it is a beverage, and thus answers a double purpose in the building up of the human constitution. You can try it free of expense. Merit alone is what is claimed for Dr. Tibbles Vi-Cocoa, and the Proprie- tors are prepared to send to any reader who names this journal a dainty sample tin of. Dr. Tibbles' Vi-Cocoa, free and post paid, upon receipt of a postcard to the Head office, 60, Bunhill Row, London, E.C. ; or you can purchase a 6d. packet, or 9d. or is. 6d. tin, from any grocer or stores. Vi-Cocoa is the cheapest and best food beverage in the world.</p>
        <p>to his wife, Ann Robinson whereby he had caused her to leave him.—Mr. Browne appeared for the com- plainant, and Mr. Steel defended.—Mr. Browne said the case had previously been adjourned but no satisfactory settlement could be arrived at. Complainant was twenty- seven years older than her husband, and as there was such a variance in ages, a separation had been agreed to. The only question was that of means.—After hearing evidence on this point, the magistrates granted a separation order with 7s. 6d. per week. A MONTH FOR A VIOLENT ASSAULT.—Richard Clancy, Dial Court, was summoned for assaulting Catherine Higgins, 18, Gandy street, on September 24th.—Com- plainant said that when she returned home on the date named defendant (who was her son-in-law) and a num- ber of friends were holding a spree there. She objected and subsequently an altercation took place between her son and the defendant. Clancy threw a bottle at her son but it missed him and struck her on the forehead inflicting a severe wound which had to be stitched at the Infirmary.—P. C. Burton spoke to arriving at the house and finding Mrs. Higgins covered with blood. She fainted, and after witness had rendered first aid she was taken to the Infirmary.—Defendant denied throwing the bottle and called his wife to support this statement —The Chief Constable said defendant had a very bad record and bad been imprisoned several times for assaulting the police. He was &amp; notorious fighter,and a dangerqus and treacherous man.—Defendant was sen- tenced:to a month's hard labour. ASSAULT WITH A JUG.—Maria Nevin, 16, Cockhedge- lane, was summoned for assaulting Mary Ann Brown, 22, Cockhedge-lane, on September 27th.—Mr. Browne defended.—Complainant said she was passing the defen- dant's house when defendant came out and struck her on the forehead with a jug.—For the defence Mr. Browne said that some years ago defendant lost her eye whilst working at Cocahedge factory. She obtained some insurance money, and complainant had persisted in calling her a "one-eyed cadger." Unfortunately she was so provoked on the date in qujestion that she assaulted her.—A fine of 10s. and costs was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>i EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>i EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>NEWTON DIVISION REVISION COURT. Mr. J. T. Foard, revising barrister, concluded the revision of the voters' lists for the Newton Division on Saturday, at Haydock, and remarked upon the lightness of the business, consequent to a great degree on the amicable working of the Conservative and Labour parties' representatives. EA RLISTOWN BOOKMAKER'S GRIEVANCES. At Newton-le-Willows, on Thursday, Matthew Seton, bookmaker, Earlestown, appeared for the' sixteenth time for street betting,at Eailestown. The evidence showed that on September 16th a constable, recently transferred to the district, and attired in tattered clothing and clogs, sat on the pavement across the road in Earle- street, presumably reading a newspaper, but as he said, he kept an eye on Seton, who took 55 bets from men, women and youths. Defendant contested the state- ment about taking bets off women and boys, and then contended that he was already taxed 'as a bookmaker in having to pay income tax lon his earnings as a bookmaker, and asked whether it was right that he should, under those cir- cumstances, be brought to court and fined the full penalty. The income tax authorities treated book- making as being legal, and said it was no crime to put half-a-crown on a horse. He also found fault with the bench allowing a guinea and a half advocate's fee in each case. Defendant was fined the full penalty, £5 and costs, and, a guinea and a half advocate's fee. IDefendant pleaded guilty to a second offence on the following day, the 17th September, and was fined a similar amount. He disputed the police evidenceabout taking bets on a third occasion, the 19th September,but was mulcted in the full penalty again. Frank Dale, another Earlestown bookmaker, pleaded guilty to three cases on same the dates as the previous ones, and was fined £5 and costs in each case and advocate's fee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>LIVER RULES US! _— -- Bile Beans Rule the Liver. – – LIVER COMPLAINT AND HEADACHE CURED.</head>
        <p>THE LIVER RULES US! _— -- Bile Beans Rule the Liver. - - LIVER COMPLAINT AND HEADACHE CURED.</p>
        <p>Every sufferer from headache, indigestion, and liver complaint, should know that Chas. Forde's Bile Beans are a certain cure for these ailments, even though such may be of long-standing. The liver is the most im- portant organ in the body. If it is disordered the digestion is thrown wrong, and various complications result. _ _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. William Scott, of Catherine street, He reford says :—" On Xmas day, 1902, I was taken bad with liver complaint and indigestion. At times I felt cold, and these feelings alternated with flushes of heat, great thirst, and aching in the stomach. My head ached, andi I bad a general feeling of langour and depression. I I had pain after food, wind, palpitation, and what little food I took would often repeat. A local medical man was called in, and pronounced my case so grave as to require a consultation with another doctor. This was held, and I was under treatment for some time. My condition became worse from day to day,and week to week, and for some time I hovered between life and death. I could not sleep for several weeks, and. as I lay awake hour after hour, suffering great pain my state may easily be imagined. The liver com- plaint had seriously affected my sight, specks seemed to be continuAlly floating before my eyes, the giddiness i and the wind increased, and the pains n the loins and back was most agonising. I lost all desire for food,and all sense of taste and smell. Despite all the doctor's attentions, I did not get any better, and my friends began to feta it with only a matter of days with me. A friend who came to see me urged me to try Chas. Forde's Bile Beads. I .commenced a course, and had been taking the Beans for several days when I felt my spirits begin to rise a little, and I became able to enjoy a little sleep. I persevered with the remedy, and a desire for food revived within me. Strangely enough, the food taken did not cause pain as before. This was most gratifying, and from that time my progress was rapid. The indigestion with all its symptoms, and the liver complaint were gradually subdued, and my whole organism seemed to be strengthened and invigorated. In the end Bile Beans restored me to perfect health and I became as you now see me, a living testimonial to their wonderful power." Bile Beans are a cure for indigestion, debility, bilious- ness, constipation, piles, nervousness, armmia. female ailments, weakness, colds, chills, neuralgia, rheumatism, sleeplessness, loss of appetite, liver complaint, headache, , flatulence, pimples, and skin eruptions. Of all chemists or post free from the Bile Beau Co Red Cross-street London, E.C., for is. lid. or 2s. 9d. per box (2s. 9d. box contains three times is. 1-W. size).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>AUCTIONEER ON PASSIVE RESISTERS.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday afternoon, at Runcorn, Mr. Alfred Grounds (of Messrs. Grounds and Co.) offered for sale the goods of the passive resisters distrained upon in Runcorn,Helsby,and Frodsham districts. The auctioneer commented upon the diminished attendance since the "last performance," and said distraining for these goods was one of the pleasantest occupations he had ever had. He wished there were two or three hundred passive resisters, instewl of only a dozen. The goods were all bought in. Mn. CARNIIGIE'S GIFT TO WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The RevT:fo.sl;;;Yealr, pastor of the Milton-road Congregational Church, has just been the recipient of a letter from the secretary of Mr. Andrew Carnegie, in which he states his willingness to contribute £2OO towards the fund of £4OO to provide the church with a new organ. Although he does not stipulate that the cost should not exceed £4OO he intimates that if the congregation feel disposed to increase the value of the purchase, his contribution will still remain at £2OO. SUPPOSED SUICIDE AT WIDNES.—A DISTRESSING CASE.</p>
        <p>Henry Wilcock, of Heath-road, Widnes, Ditton, had some words with his wife at noon on Thursday, and left the house. He returned shortly afterwards, without a coat and hat, and they were !afterwards found on the edge of a pit close by.. Dr. Eatock was summoned to attend to some injuries the wife had sustained, and shortly afterwards he heard a gurgling sound upstairs, and went up and found Mr. Wilcock lying on his side with his throat cut, and a pocket-knife lying by his side. Every assistance was given by the medical man, but he died shortly afterwards.—He was summoned to appear at the Widnes Police Court on Thursday for non- payment of the poor rate, and two distress warrants were granted. A DANGEROUS OCCUPATIOS.—PROSECUTION AT WIDNES. _</p>
        <p>At Widnes on Thursday, before Messrs. T. S. Timmis and E. K. Muspratt, Arthur Cooper, mineral water manu- facturer, Terrace-road, Widnes. and John O'Neil, bottler, were charged with breaches of the Act of 1891, which schedules the mineral water trade as dangerous.—Mr. Graves, factory inspector, prosecuted, and elicited from Mr. Cooper that on the 6th of August he saw O'Neil at his work without his mask or gauntlets. O'Neil was left by himself,and when Mr. Cooper returned he found O'Neil had cut his hand.—Mr. Poole defended Cooper, and contended that he had provided all the materials for the men according to the rules, but they would not wear them because they were cumbersome: and the masks ruined the eyes.—The Bench were of opinion that Cooper should have enforced the rules. As the inspector did not wish to press the charge, they had decided to deal leniently.—Cooper was fined 10s. and costs, and O'Neil 2s. 6d. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>PTOMAINE POISONLNG AT ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>PTOMAINE POISONLNG AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Edward Swift, aged four years,.a fine healthy boy, living with his parents at 2, Blacklull-street, St. Helens, was taken suddenly ill on Saturday night, and•on Sun- day he was found to be very much swollen about the face and stomach, and on Dr. Bellow being called in on Monday he found him suffering from ptomaine poison- ing. The boy said he had eaten some pears on Saturday night. After considerable suffering he died late on Tuesday night. The facts have been reported to the coroner, who has decided to give a certificate in accord- ance with the medical opinion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, October 1, 1904.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 1, 1904.</p>
        <p>ling.llll</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>WARRINGTON RESISTERS</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RESISTERS</p>
        <p>AGAIN APPEAR BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>What is the Poor Rate ?</p>
        <p>For the third time the Warrington magistrates on Tuesday heard summonses against passive resisters- for non-payment of the rate devoted to educational pur- poses. On this occasion, however, the Rev. J. F. Matthews, minister of Latchford Baptist Church, was not included. He has made over his goods to his wife, and it is evident that the authorities do not intend to proceed against him further. The rev, gentleman was present in Court, and during the hearing took the oppor- tunity of making a suggestion to the justices. Dr. Fox was the presiding magistrate, and he was accompanied on the bench by Alderman Hutchinson, Councillor Thornton, Mr. J. Rymer Young, and Mr. W. Roberts. The following is a list of the resisters with the amounts of the rate due :—William Clatworthy, 21, Lovely-lane, 7s. 4d.; Rev. W. J. Roberts (Wersh Calvinistic Methodist Church, Crosfield-street), 30, Parker-street, 6s. id.; Norman Balmford, 15, Museum-street, 10s. 6d. ; Emma J. Fieldhouse, Bridge-street, us. 6d.; Edward Howard 192, Padgate-lane, ss. 6d. ; Rev. J. L. Milne (St. John's Presbyterian Church, Winwick-street), 33, Orford-road, 7s. 6d,; William Gallop, 69, Buttermarket-street, 10s. ; William Mayer, 33, Froghall-lane, ss. 6d. ; Thos. Dale, 76, Priestley-street, Is. 4d. Mr. W.-H. Woodcock (assis- tant overseer) appeared for the authorities. The Rev. J. F. Matthews said he was present to answer the summons fog Mr. Clatworthy who could not appear,but he was advised that he had no title to lodge a.protest on his behalf. Therhairman : He does not make any defence. (To Mr. Woodcock): You ask for an order. Mr. Woodcock: Yes. , The usual order was made.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. J. Roberts said he would like to know when he refused to pay the poor rate. Mr. Woodcock: You refused to pay the portion we summon you for. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Roberts: The portion of what? Mr. Woodcock: Of the poor rate, on April 18th, 1904.</p>
        <p>The Magistrates' Clerk (Mr. G. F. F. White) : You don't dispute that you awe the rate? Mr. Roberts: I refuse to pay the money,but not the poor rate. The Chairman: Do you admit that it is a legal claim? Mr. Roberts: I don't know. The Chairman: I suppose that is the only ground on which we can hear you. Mr. Roberts: We don't consider the summons— The Chairman: Answer the question please. Do you admit it is a legal claim? Mr. Roberts: What do you mean by a legal claim? The Chairman: It is obvious what I mean. Mr. Roberts: If you mean a legal claim in connec- tion with the poor rate, I should like to know what you mean by poor rate. Would you mind giving me a definition of it? Mr. Woodcock: We have nothing to do with the definition. There are several rates included in the term poor rate. They are all collected in the same way as a poor rate. The Clerk: You do not dispute owing the money? Mr. Roberts: No sir, not at all. I never refused to I pay the poor rate, but am summoned here to show cause why I have not paid it. The Clerk: You dispute that it is part of the poor, rate. Mr. Roberts: I refuse to pay a certain amount, but not the poor rate. Mr. Woodcock said his claim was for the balance of the rate levied as a poor rate. Mr. Roberts: Then the Council of Warrington has a right to raise money under false pretences, has it? The Chairman: We have no option in the matter but to make an order. Mr. Norman Balmford admitted owing 10s. 6d. He had nothing to say except that he refused to pay the rate willingly. The Chairman: It is a legal claim, and the order isygranted. Mr. Balmford explained that Miss Fieldhouse was unable to be present and he had been asked to answer for her. Her position was just the same.—The order was made. _ . .</p>
        <p>The magistrates granted an order in the case of Mr. Howard, who did not appear. The Rev. J. L. Milne said he declined to pay the education rate. Councillor Thornton: In this form, of course. Mr. Mime: In any form, ,I decline to pay it. The order was made. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Roberts: I think these ladies and gentlemen quite understand that the Bench have no option in this matter. However much we may sympathise with them in this protest still, as magistrates, we have no option but to grant the order. The Chairman: That is so.</p>
        <p>The Rev. J. F. Matthews: Could not your worships help us by making a representation to the Govern ment to relieve us from this ignominous position? The Chairman: It is for you to do that. Mr. Matthews: We are doing it. We are obliged to you for your courtesy, but we are doing all we can and we intend to do it.</p>
        <p>.• Messrs. William Gallop and William Mayer offered the same objection as the other resisters, and the orders were made. - -</p>
        <p>.• Mr. Thomas Dale said he had a conscientious objec- tion to paying the education rate. He had paid the poor rate. The Chairman: We have no alternative but to make the order.</p>
        <p>The magistrates agreed to issue one distress warran to sover the whole of the cases.</p>
        <p>Distraint warrants for the education rate were issued at Sandbach Police Court on Wednesday against the Rev. R. Hughes (Congregationalist) and Mr. lA. B. Thorne, Betchton, the magistrates being the Rev. J. H. Armitstead, of Holmes Chapel (in the chair), Mr. Ezar Harthan, and Mr. T. Marriott. The last-named said that personally he might agree with the defendants, but the bench had no legal alternative but to issue warrants. Mr. Harthan, on the other hand, speaking with much earnestness, emphatically dissociated himself from his brother magistrates. He characterised the Education Act as amost unjust one so far as Nonconformists are concerned, and declared himself in full sympathy with the passive resistance movement. Yet until the law is repealed or amended the bench must discharge its mostunpleasant duty. THE REV. W. BRACECAMP'S VIEW OF RESISTANCE.</p>
        <p>Priaching at St. Paul's Church on Sunday evening at a service on behalf of the day schools, the 'Vicar (Rev. W. Bracecamp) chose for his text Prov. xxii. 6: "Train up a child in the way he should go, and when he is old he will not depart from it." After dealing with the education of children generally, he said it would seem strange to some of them after what was being so often said by their opponents, that all education, both religious and secular, was paid for out of the rates, that he should appeal to them for help for their day schools. But those who knew anything about the Education Act knew perfectly well that what was being said about the Church schools was most untrue and very uncharitable. In fact, he said, when he read some of the bitter and cruel remarks made by the so-called Chris- tian leaders of the people,he began to wonder if there was such a virtue as the charity of the Bible existing amongst them. He thought if the preachers of religion would only practice a little more of it among themselves and preach less about it, what a grand thing it would be ! What would have been said by their opponents if they had refused to pay the rates when the Act of 1870 was passed, when board schools were built in nearly every town, and Churchmen were compelled to pay the School Board rate,and support:their;own denominational schools at the same time? Their position at St. Paul's was this : Since the passing of the Act they, the managers of the schools, had spent £l,lOO, and were now in debt. What had the money been paid for ?—in order that they might be able to teach the religion of their forefathers. And what was more, they would have continually to find money for the part upkeep and for the use of. the school for all meetings held on the premises. It was very hard lines, but he felt they would all do their best for the sake of the Church they loved so dearly. As far as he personally was concerned, he was prepared to give his last penny, rather than that the schools should be handed over to any authority. A great many of the • passive resisters had received a great portion of their education in denominational schools, and now for political purposes and" bluff "nothing was too unkind, too uncharitable to be said against the Church.</p>
        <p>MIME'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>Bold-street Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>- _ Large congregations attended this Church on Sunday when the anniversary services were held. The members of the church had been fortunate in securing the ser- vices of the Rev. Marshall Hartley(ex-president of the conference)as'preacher for the day. Special attention had been given to the musical portion of the services,and the choir is to be complimented upon the efficiency attained In the morning the anthem "The wilderness and the solitary place shall be glad," was admirably rendered,the solo being taken by Mr. W. H. Wallington. The quartet was sung by the Misses Taylor and D9nn, and Mtssrs. Warburton and Page. Later Mr. Levi Richardson con- tributed "The King of Love." The preacher took a his text "All to the glory of God" (Psalm ixxii. 3), and urged his hearers to aspire higher and higher in their services for God. The church was well filled in the evening, when the choir was augmented by vocalists from other choirs and an orchestra. The choruses "And the Glory," "Worthy is the Lamb "and the "Hallelujah Chorus" were rendered in highlz creditable style, the overture from the "Messiah" being also played by the orchestra. Basing his remarks on the words "What seek ye? Come and see," the preacher made a powerful appeal to the young people to live a God-like life. Mr. Frank Hickman presided at the organ and to him and to Mr. Page, the choir secretary, special praise must be accorded for the success of the musical features.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>MUNICIPAL _ELECTIONS. Meetings of Ward Committees.</head>
        <p>THE MUNICIPAL _ELECTIONS. Meetings of Ward Committees.</p>
        <p>Preparations—are already being ma-de for the approach- ing municipal elections. The two political parties have held several ward committee meetings this week with the view of selecting candidates, and at a meeting of the Labour Representation Committee held on Tuesday, we understand that the names of two possible labour candidates were brought forward for the Whitecross and Bewsey wards respectively. In addition to the usual contests, there is a further vacancy in Bewsey Ward to be filled up, owing to the retirement of Mr. Samuel Gough from the Council. A rumour has been circulated that Mr. Henry Roberts, J.P., intends to 'seek re-election and will contest either Bewsey or Whitecrom Wards in the approaching elections. The councillors who retire in November next are as follow : Ward. Representative. Town Hall W. J. Forshaw (C) Whitecross Brough (L)</p>
        <p>Bewsey W. Rogi;ls -(r) Orford J. Wilkinson (L) St. John's H, Thornton (N T) Fairfield D. S. Dungan (L)</p>
        <p>Howley St. Austin's Latchford</p>
        <p>S. Davies (C) T. Burton (L) L. Ellison (14</p>
        <p>It will be seen that five of the retiring Councillors are Liberals, two Conservatives, one a Non-Trader and one an Independent. LIBERAL WARD MEETINGS. ST. AUSTIN'S WARD.</p>
        <p>A meeting in connection with St. Austin's Ward was held in the Thewlis-street schoolroom on Monday night the chief business being the seledion of a candidate for the forthcoming election. On the motion of Mr. ELWELL, seconded by Mr. W. CROSSKILL, and supported by Mr. D UNBOBBIN, Councillor Burton was heartily thanked for the good work he had done in the past as representative of the ward. It was then unanimously decided that Councillor Burton be asked to stand again as candidate. The meeting was presided over by Councillor Tinnion, and Messrs. J. Howes, jun., and F. Plumpton were also present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>FAIRFIELD WARD.-</head>
        <p>FAIRFIELD WARD.-</p>
        <p>An enthusiastic meeting was held in the Fairfield Schools on Tuesday evening. Mr. John White occupied the chair. It was proposed by Mr. Woods that Coun- cillor Duncan be again asked to contest the ward. This was seconded by Mr. W. Habberley and carried. Mr. Duncan consented to accept the nomination. He was thanked for his past services, and a resolution was after- wards passed pledging the meeting to do all in its power to secure his return at the election on November Ist. The resignation of Mr. John Willett as chairman of the ward, owing to pressure of business, was received with .deep regret. It was proposed by Mr. J. Willett, and seconded by Mr. Woods, that Mr. George Walker be appointed in his stead, and this wasYcarried. WHITECROSS WARD.</p>
        <p>A largely-attended meeting of the Whitecross Ward Committee was held in Thewlis-street schools on Wed- nesday evening. Councillor James Evans, the chairman of the committee, presided. After some preliminary work in connection with registration had been disposed of, the Chairman said the business of the evening was to select a candidate for the forthcoming election. Mr. Councillor Brough was the retiring representative of the ward, and he (the Chairman) had received a letter from him, which he would ask the Assistant Secretary to read.—Mr. Frank Plumpton then read the letter. Mr. Brough wrote that he desired to convey to the meeting his sincere thanks for the honour they had done him in sending him to the Town Council. He had endeavoured to do his duty faithfully and conscientiously by sup- porting whatever he thought to be in the best interests of the town, but he desired to be relieved from the posi- tion and did not seek re-election.—Mr. -Brough was thanked for his services, and it was unanimously decided on the proposition of Mr. Osborne, that a deputation wait on Councillor Brough, asking him to allow himself to be nominated again for the position.—The deputa- tion will consist of the Chairman, Mr. Osborne and Mr. Ashworth.—Several names were added to the com- mittee, and the proceedings terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>CONSERVATIVE WARD MEETINGS. TOWN HALL WARD.</head>
        <p>CONSERVATIVE WARD MEETINGS. TOWN HALL WARD.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Conservative Ward Committee was held at the Conservative Club on Wednesday evening, Councillor W. J. Forshaw presiding. The attendance included Messrs, A. J. Jolley, J. Strettle, and other members. On the motion of Mr. Jolley, seconded by Mr. John Hayes, it was decided to ask Councillor Forshaw to allow himself to be nominated again for the ward. Councillor Forshaw returned thanks for the invitation, and in promising to stand at the forthcom- ing election, said he would do his utmost for the welfare of the ward and of the town in general. On the proposition of Mr. J. Strettle seconded by Mr. W. Hayes, 'Mr. A. J. Jolley was appointed chairman of the election committee. A vote of thanks was accorded to Councillor •Forshaw for his past services and also for presiding, on the motion of Mr. Jolley seconded by Mr. T. Cowdell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>MOTOR NOTES.</head>
        <p>MOTOR NOTES.</p>
        <p>We are pleased to notice that that eminently practica and sensible paper, the" Antocar " comments upon the foolishness of decorating the ears with flowers and parading them through the streets at the close of the recent Trials. The proceeding was silly, it served no useful end, and it merely served to introduce the spirit of the irresponsible gymkhana into what had been a very serious event.</p>
        <p>We do not want gymkhanas. We never did. They helped to spoil cycling, both as a sport and a pastime, and they will do the same for motoring. There are kindergarten schools for little children who want to play games whilst they are learning, their lessons • and there are institutions and asylums for grown-up children who still want to play when they should be learning. But a motor car is not a suitable toy for the mentally deficient, a woolly bear or a rag doll is better, and the adults who want to play games with motors will be better off in a nice comfortable building where there are good strong bars to the windows to keep them from falling out and nice soft cushions to the wallsj to pre- vent them hurting themselves when they gettoo excited with their toys.</p>
        <p>It is a great pity that more attention is not paid to the most important matter of circulation. The high- powered and high-priced cars are usually well-designed in this respect; but upon many of the voiturettes the circulation gives the owner more trouble than any other part of the motor. It is certainly best when arranged in connection with a gear-driven pump; but there is no reason why the Thermo-syphon system should not prove perfectly satisfactory upon a light car. The usual fault seems to lie with making the pipes and glands too small, for, in nearly all cases where the gravity has been tried ani found wanting, the error may usually be traced to this source.</p>
        <p>At the time of writing an attempt is being made to beat the existing record for a non-stop run—in the Automobile Club's acceptance of the phrase—which is, that the engine must run continuously, even though the road,wheels be stopped. Mr. Archibald Ford, a North of England motorist, is attempting to cover 3,600 miles on his 15 h.p. Darracq under these conditions, the road he is following being from London to Perth, where he turns, returns to London and thence to Brighton,turning here again and going back over the same distance. This route will be. repeated until the coveted mileage has been covered. Although Mr. Ford has an engineer with him who takes turns at the wheel, the test is almost a more severe one for the man than for the motor, and, should he succeed in his self-imposed task, one will be inclined to count the reliability of the engine second to the pluck and endurance of the driver.</p>
        <p>Of course, the longest real non-stop run, which is the world's record, stands to the credit of the 5 h.p. Decauville, that, four years ago, ran one thousand miles on the Crystal Palace track without stopping either road, wheel or engine. It is rather an extraordinary thing that, although this run was made so far back as 1990, it has never yet been beaten, for one would natur- ally suppose that, with the immense improvement in motors and theggenerally increased h.p. car now, the record would have fallen long ago. That it has not done so only goes to show that the performance was perhaps a more remarkable•one than was at first sup- posed. Indeed, considering the hysterical delight that has been expressed over a few cars which managed to cover 12 runs of 50 miles non-stops each in six conse- cutive days, each of these cars being of greater h.p. than the Decauville voiturette, it almost seems as if, with increased efficiency, we expett less of our engines than was formerly the case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>SOUTH LANCASHIRE TRAMWAYS.</head>
        <p>THE SOUTH LANCASHIRE TRAMWAYS.</p>
        <p>Heavy Debit Balance.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED RECONSTRUCTION OF THE COMPANY.</p>
        <p>The shareholders of the South Lancashire Electric I Traction and Power Company have received notice of an extraordinary general meeting, to be held on October 7th, "for the purpose of considering the advisability of winding up the company, in view of its contemplated reconstruction, and, if thought fit, rpassingia resolution for voluntary winding up, and the appointment of a liquidator." The accounts of the company for the year ended June 30th show a profit of £2,573, making, with the balance brought forward, £3,351, but liabilities to contractors for works not certified by consulting engineers, and the accrued dividends from date of allotment to June 30, ' 1904,0 n the preference 'shares have not been provided for There is also a debit in the accounts of £58,702, being j interest on debenture stock and calls paid in advance for the twb years ended June 30, 1904, less credit inter- est of £12,347. The accounts show an issued share and ' debenture capital of nearly £913,000, plus £47,000 of stock issued as security for advances; also a debit of £22,128 in respect of preliminary and formation expenses. The directors report that the whole of the tramways and light railway constituting the first constructional scheme of the company have been in operation since Easter, 1903 (portions of ithe scheme being in operation earlier), but the receipts on the working of these lines have not quite come up to expectations. It must be I remembered, however, that the districts served have passed through a crisis in the cotton industry, and that trade generally has been extremely depressed. More- over, it must be borne in mind that the system still remains to be completed, and there can be no doubt that the sections remaining to be built will act as feeders, and will not only earn money themselves, but will also largely increase the earning power of the exist- ing lines. The South Lancashire Tramways Company • inaugurated an extensive system of dealing with parcels lin conjunction with the Great Central Railway and various other tramway authorities, but this service did not prove remunerative and absorbed some of.the profits ,of the company. The service was, therefore, discon- tinued in June.</p>
        <p>The arrangements for raising further capital, which were sanctioned by the Debenture stockholders and authorised by the shareholders it the latter end of last year, were actively proceeded with by the directors,who were, however, frustrated partly by reason of the finan- cial depression which made itself felt so acutely during the year, and partly by the feeling of ',insecurity arising from the legal proceedings brought against the company and some of the directors by a very small minority of the Debenture Stockholders. In order to afford more time for negotiations for fresh capital, which appeared likely to yield good results,arrangements had been made for the payment of the debenture interest due on June 30, but the directors were prevented from carrying them out by the attitude adopted by the owners of some of the Ordinary Shares, which were to be transferred as one of the conditions on which the resolution authoris. ing the creation of fresh capital was passed last year. The Debenture interest not being paid,the Court, on the application of a Debenture Stockholder, appointed Mr. J. M. Henderson and Mr. R. J. Salter joint receivers and managers. Negotiations are pending, and there is reason to hope that a reconstruction of capital may be possible,and that the additional capital, required to proceed with the construction of the lines may be obtained at a satisfac- tory rate. Sir John A. Wilcox, has, through continued ill- health, retired from the /directorate, and Mr. Joseph Beecham and Mr. H. R. Hogg have joined the board. The Hon. Arthur Stanley, M.P., and Mr. C.- E. Maples offer themselves for re-election.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>CHILDREN'S COUGHS.</head>
        <p>CHILDREN'S COUGHS.</p>
        <p>MRS. BALLIN, THE GREAT AUTHORITY ON C4ILDREN'S DISEASES, STRONGLY RECOMMENDS</p>
        <p>VENO'S L.IGHTNING COUGH CURE.</p>
        <p>Mrs. ADA S. BALLIN, 5, Agar-street, London' editor of "Womanhood," writes :—"VENo's LIGHTNING COUGH CURE is an exceedingly successful remedy. It is very pleasant to take, and the relief it gives is very rapid.. The preparation is perfectly safe for children." Mrs. HAGUE, Ratcliffe Crescent, Loekwood, near Huddersfield, writes :—" My little boy suffered from hoarseness and bronchitis. We all thought he never would be cured or speak plain again. After taking VERO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE he was able to speak plainly. I have never seen anything to act so Quickly." Ask for VmsTo's LIGHTNING COUGH CURE at Chemists and Drug Stores. Price 90., is. INd. and 2.5. 9d. Sold everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>NORTHERN RUGBT,'',LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN RUGBT,'',LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Batley Ground Suspended.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Northern Rugby League was held at the George Hotel, Huddersfield, on Tuesday, Mr. J. Platt (Oldham) presiding. The best thanks of the Committee were voted to Mr. J. H. Smith for his services in the arrangement of the League fixtures.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>REGISTRATIONS.</head>
        <p>REGISTRATIONS.</p>
        <p>The following registrations were made : Put on.—Batley, H. Taylor .(Slaithwaite) ; Bradford, W. H. Bartle (Shipley Victoria) ; Brighouse Rangers, T. Priestley (Batley), G. Saunders (local), J. Maude (Leeds) and J. Battyre (Huddersfield) ; Broughton Rangers, J. Milman (Salford St. Bartholomew's) ; Castleford,J. Cannon (Bushtown Rangers); Hull,A. Land (local) and J. W. Burchell (Neath) ; Hunslet, C. Mellish (Headingley) and R. F. W. Nelson (Hudders- field Old Collegians) ; Lancaster, G. Kilburn (local) ; Leeds, A. Lunn (Hunslet), I. Leonard (Holbeck), and F. E. Wasling (Cbmmercial College); Normanton, G. Dixon (Roscoe White Rose); Runcorn, T. Jones (Runcorn Recreation). Salford, J. Elliott (Egerton) ; St. Helens, D. H. Edmunds (Hull) and D. Traynor (Swinton) ; Swinton, V. Fearnley (Stockport) ; Wakefield Trinity, E. Richardson (East-. moor Clarence) and H. Holbrook (Wakefield Rugby) ; Warrington,.H. Baines (Latchford) and H. Berry (War- rington Albions) ; and Widnes, H. J. Murray and T. Harrison (both local). Struck off.—Broughton Rangers,A. Anderson, J. Berry F, Dawson G. Duffern, H. Eastwood, A. Garside, P. Morgan, H. J. Selby, B. Smith, and A. B. Thomas; Huddersfield; J. Maude; Hull, B. H. Edmunds and F. Smith; Hunslet, T. Pyrah ; Leeds, W. Storey and T. Hanson; Salford, W. McQuinn ; Swinton, D. Traynor; and Widnes, F. W. Sampson and W. Morris.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>FINES.</head>
        <p>FINES.</p>
        <p>The following are fines for late starts :—Bradford 305., at Widnes; Pontefract 205., at Bramley ; Batley and Birkenhead 10s. each, at Oldham and Lancaster respectively.</p>
