      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>SUSPENSIONS.</head>
        <p>SUSPENSIONS.</p>
        <p>The following players were suspended up to October 8 :—J. Dixon (Runcorn), J. Wagstaffe (Normanton), E. Fallowfield (Barrow), and Littlnwood (Leeds), all for kicking; T. Morrison (Warrington) and Pennington (Birkenhead), for striking; E. Dawson (Keighley), for striking and using abusive language; and E. Burnett (llwaslet) and Butterworth (Runcorn), sending off the ground was considered sufficient punishment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>PON'TEFRACT FAIL TO puLrfi, AN AGREEMENT,</head>
        <p>PON'TEFRACT FAIL TO puLrfi, AN AGREEMENT,</p>
        <p>Wakefield Trinity complained that Pontefract had not kept their agreement re the transfer of G. W. Milsom, and the Committee ordered the agreement to be kept, the payment of the transfer fee to be made on or before October 10, through the League 'Secretary, failing which the Pontefract ground will be suspended.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>ASSAULT ON A REFEREE AT BATLEY.</head>
        <p>THE ASSAULT ON A REFEREE AT BATLEY.</p>
        <p>Mr: J. H. Smith (Widnes), the-refe-ree—iniTie.match Batley v. Wigan, laid a charge of assault by stone- throwing against the Batley spectators. The Com- mittee decided to suspend the rtatley ground up to and including October 29, and ordered that the match, Batley V. St. Helens on October 8, and the match Batley v. Hunslet on October 29, be played on grounds to be arranged, the expenses of the referees and touch judges to be paid by the Batley F.C.</p>
        <p>A cannon played with check side with the nap, and with running- side against the nap..</p>
        <p>Diagram, 14 shows the three balls in such a posi- tion that the angle for the canon is exactly the same playing with either ball. The angle is intended to besuch that a half ball stroke would miss the cannon, and to make the cannon either plenty of side must be used or a rather• thin stroke or a partial run-through must be played. In such a position any player playing from the baulk end would, if playing the cannon with side, use check side (left hand side in the diagram), but most players would also use check side for a similar stroke when playing from the top end of the table. Instead, running side should be used (left hand side in the diagram), for running side against the nap of the cloth acts in the same way as check side with the nap of the cloth. Of course, the cannon-could be made with check side instead of running side, but it would be made, not through any assistance gained by the check side, but in spite of the check side, aud the contact would have to be finer than if .he stroke were played with running side.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>DIAGRAM 15.</head>
        <p>DIAGRAM 15.</p>
        <p>A thin in-off played against the nap. Red ball 26i inches from the side cushion and 49i inches from the top cushion. Cue. ball 35 inches from the side cushion an.cl_ 17 .inches from the top cushion Running side will make the cue ball pull to the pocket.</p>
        <p>Diagram 15 shows a thin in-off into a centre pocket. Again here most players would use check side for such an in-off, but as the cue ball has to ran against the nap of the cloth running side (right hand side in the diagram) should be used to assist in the making of the stroke. If this stroke be played quite slowly, thus allowing the greatest effect to be got from the side working against the nap of the cloth, the cue hal will immediately after contact often appear (when the stroke is ultimately successful) as though it were going to strike the cushion above the Pocket at the point marked x on the diag.ram. The running side on the cue ball, liowever, working against the nap of the cloth, 'pulls the ball away from the line it was 'making towards the cushion and allows it to enter the pocket. Should the ball happen to strike the lower angleof the pocket that is the angle nearer to baulk, the running side on the ball will be pocket side off this opposing angle, and the tendency of the side will therefore be to take the ball into the pocket. (To be Continued.)</p>
        <p>Mr. Riso Levi will be pleased fo answer in these rolumns all questions relative to the article appearing weekly, and as far as possible any questions on other points in connection with the tameg of Billiards. Envelopes should be marked Billiards."</p>
        <p>been written on this branch of the game. Nursery cannon play, now that the push stroke has been abolished, is so very difficult that the instruction given will be of very little use to any except really good players. Pigue and Mass4,strokes are most ably dealt within Chapter 11., and the hints given should be of great assistance to the aspiring player. The chapters on "Break-making "and •' Controlling the Red Ball " are so masterly and Yet withal so lucid that any player who studies them cannot fail to improve his knowledge of the game' The final chapter treats of the top-of-the-table genie' and those who hope to improve at this branch of the game will find it most carefully analysed. Mannockt however, all the way through his book,strongly advises amateurs to stick to the in-off game, and quite rightlY too, for it is safe to say that there is not a single amateur in the 'country who does not lose far more than he ever gains by essaying to play the top of the table game. "Good to look upon, but deep as un- fathomableawaters," says the writer, and only those virbn have attained just a slight proficiency at this fascin- ating but elusive branch of the game know how true this is, and how many are the pitfalls. Mannock admits that when playing at the top-of-the-table he feels like treading on the edge of a precipice. "Billiards Expounded" is a well-written book printed 1 on good paper and should be in the hands of all billiard I players, and those who think 7s. 6d. too much for a l book on billiards would do well to abstain from plal- ing for a week or two and buy the book with the money saved.</p>
        <p>ANSWERS TO CORRESPONDENTS. A. Thomas (Taunton).—You should get "The World of Billiards," weekly, price 2d. A. Gibson (Droitwich).—Diggle's break of 791 was not passed as a record, owing to the table not having been a standard one. Amateur (Stafford.)—When a ball is nearly on thf:_ baulk line, but is more out than in, it is playable,and can be hit on that part which is in baulk. Answers to several correspondents are unavoidably held over till next week. RISO LEVI.</p>
        <p>: . ____ [..___lf_l_ Q ( i s. §</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>A ST. HELINS CHILD'S DEATH. Mrs. Cotton, wife of a glassmaker, of 95, St. Pall: street, St. Helens, retired to rest on 'Saturday nig"' with her two little boys, and at 3.30 on Sunday morning she awoke to find the baby, John, aged eight weeks' dead, on her arm, with his brother's head close up t 0 his face. The elder child, George, is two and a half years old. THE MUNICIPAL ELECTION'S. It has been stated in various quarters that the Labour party intend bringing forward a large number of condi; dates at the forthcoming elections at St. Helens, Wl' inquiries point to the fact that only in North Eccl.,at.: ton and Sutton Wards is there any probability of za": happening. The difficulty is to get candidates who ar- able to attend Council meetings held during the III; time, instead of the evening. In North Ecelestoo Conservatives are bringing forward Mr. Eyers, a loca tradesman, and there will also be a contest in Sutton Ward.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS NEW RESERVOIR OPENED. The new covered reservoir which has been constructed at a cost of about 230,000, to ' increase the storage capacity for St. Helens water, was on Thursday opened by the Ma,yor. St. Helens has an excellent water supplT: from five pumping stations, but the hitherto smal l storage capacity had occasioned the gravest anxiety to the officials lest by a breakdown at any of the station the constancy of the water supply should be interfereu with. The new reservoir has a capacity of 1%0000 gallons. PRESCOT BLICKLISTER AT ST. HELENS. Thomas M'Eran a blacklister, of 7, Hill-street, Pres- cot, was charged al St. Helens on Tuesday with attenlPt- ting to obtain drink at the Griffin Inn, Eccleston, 011 the 10th inst.—Sergeant Notman said M'Egan was Put on the blacklist by the Prescot magistrates on Ist last. He had forty-four convictions, and since Mara had been sent to goal for breach of the peace.—grs' Briers, the landlady, gave evidence.—M`Egan said 11,1; Alarcl2 thought the blacklist only applied to the county. 101' that he could get a drink in the borough of St. Helens' —Alderman J. C. Gamble (chairman) : But the Griffin is not in the borough.—M`Egan was fined 10s. and 60' costs or seven days. •</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS OFFICE-BOYS' WALK. d This walking contest, which was last year insti te tu_ by Mr. J. A. Gamble, of St. Helens, took place ti Saturday over a seven-mile course. About twenty between the ages of twelve and sixteen years entered. The start took place from the top of Dente°,.. green, at 3.70 p.m., and the course was by way of Cre3,7,, hill, Billinge, and Moss Bank. The Rev. T. W. lietewrll vicar of St. Mark's Church, and Mr. M. J. Hareri, l" were the starters and judges. The result was as follows: —l, H. G. Lyon (15 years), time 1 hour 18 minutes ;2, W. Hunter (13), 1 hour 19. minutes, 5 seconds;_ 3, Farrington (14), 1 hour 20 minutes, 5 seconds ,• estt (15), 1 hour 20 minutes, 10 seconds. Thec9s petitors were afterwards entertained to tea at bY Y.51.C.A. Gymnasium, the arrangements being made Mr. C. Carey. The prizes were distributed by Mrs. -v W. Tinker.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>PENKETH,</head>
        <p>PENKETH,</p>
        <p>THE WESLEYAN DAY SCHOOL.--On Monday evolirogi a meeting of duly qualified subscribers and paren,f,i,.. children on the school register, for the purpose of 8,11'4. leg two representative managers,was held in the seu-°",, room, under the presidency of the Rev. G. H. Carnse.'-' The following facts had been previously made knovvibe (a) The term of office will be three years ,• (b) il person who has the actual custody of any children W_„l be regarded as their parent for the purpose of this ele`l: tidn ; (c) husbands may allow their wives to vote orii their behalf; (d) electors may give their vote eith:a personally at the meeting, or by writing deliver before the meeting to Mr. Watson, the head teacb,.,eri; each elector has one vote only in respect of vacancy; (e) one of the managers chosen must be_., member of the Wesleyan Methodist Church. 7." names having been put before the meeting, finallY, John Midwood and Mr. Alfred Seed (members of t Wesleyan Church) were unanimously elected.</p>
        <p>The London and North-Western Railway Corn has increased the number of men employed at Holywetit, in retarding the advance of the sea, the encroachine of which threatens the safety of the Company's lines•so</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
