      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH.</p>
        <p>A new departure was adopted in the celebration of the harvest festival at the Parish Church, the services commencing on Sunday and being continued on Thurs- day evening. The decorations were on a lavish and artistic scale, the altar, pulpit, clergy stalls and font having been treated in a tasteful and effective way which called forth general praise. The following is a list of the decorators :—Altar, the' Misses White (Sankey-street); chancel rails, Miss F. Price and Miss Brooks; clergy stalls. Mrs. Farrell, Mrs. William Fair- clough and Miss Willis; pulpit, Miss Lilian tßogers and Miss Mabel Sankey; font, Miss Lena Neild and Miss Millington; gas standards, Mrs. Craik, Miss Taylor and Miss Farrell; windows, Mrs. Willis; and altar in the Boteler chapel, the Misses White. On Sunday morn- ing the service took the form of Matirs and a choral celebration of the Holy Communion, the preacher being the Rev. F. Howard Pitt, of St. Agnes', Liverpool, who has several relatives in Warrington. The choir rendered the musical portions of the service very, creditably. Mr. F. M. Darby, Mus. Bac., presiding at the organ. A service for the young was held in the after- noon. At Evensong the church was crowded, even the seats in the chancel being occupied. The prayers were intoned by the Rector (Rev. Canon Willis), and the Rev. E. J. Willcocks, headmaster of the Grammar School, read the lessons. The choir gave an effective interpretation of the anthem "Lo, summer comes again." The preacher was the Rev. J. W. Isher- wood, St. Chad's, Shrewsbury. a former scholar of the Grammar School,and a son of Mr. Robert Isherwood, of Warrington. The words of the text were: "And God said, 'Let the earth bring forth grass; the 'herb yielding seed, and the fruit tree yielding fruit after his kind, whose seed is in itself upon the earth,' and it was so and " The Lord is faithful." The preacher pointed out that there were those who reckoned on the faithfulness of God, and yet all the time basely neglected His com- mands. The farmer, who, perhaps, never bowed his knees to God, and never uttered the name of God except to flavour his brutal oaths, never- theless ploughed his land, dropped in the seed, and then waited .confidently for the harvest. Men whose lives were spent in utter forgetfulness of God took it as a matter of course that spring would follow winter, and that the harvest would spring from the earth enriched by alternating rain and sunshine. The man of science, again, calculated all the various phenomena of what he called the "laws of nature," and placed so much confidence in the exactness of their working that he could foretell with a precision which simply astounded what would happen the exact moment thousands of Oars hence it might be when, for instance, two planets would meet in space. Everything in oar lives instinctively proclaimed the faithfulness of God, and it was that very faithfulness that caused man to forget God. The very fidelity left them cold and indifferent and served as an excuse for ingratitude. At least those present did not allow the university of God's laws to make them forget the divine order of things ; and behind all nature's laws they, recognized the love and wisdom of God or he took it they would not be present that night. They were not present to see the decorations or for the luxury of a merely popular service or singing popular hymns,but simply as children of God to offer their thanks for the many blessings He had showered upon them. The collections were in aid of the clergy fund for which £5O is required. The total sum realized throughout the day was £23 13s. 7d. There was another large congregation on Thursday evening when the preacher was the Lord Bishop of Liverpool (Right Rev. Dr. Chavasse.) Basing his sermon on the words "The reapers are the angels" his Lordship pointed out that as that was the feast of St. Michael and All Angels, they were celebrating a double festival. The great thoughts which the day suggested were wrapped up in his text. There was no occasion for him to prove to them the great truth of the existence and ministry of angels. Our Lord Jesus Christ, from whose word for Christians there was no appeal, taught it emphatically. At the last day the angels would be the reapers, and the three offices deputed to them would be to separate the good from the evil, to bind the tares into bundles, and to gather the wheat into God's barn. In the church of God to the very end the evil would ever be mingled with the good. Tares and wheat would grow side by side. At present we could not attempt to separate them. If we did we should root up the wheat with the tares. The plausible hypocrite would be taken to be the saint, and many a quiet child of God whose secret life was hidden from the world would be classed as outside the pale of God's harvest. The great work of separation was reserved for the last day, and the angels were to do it. They were super-human and they would make no mistake. That separation would be final. Death did not change bad people into good, or good people into bad. Death only stamped upon them for ever the character which they had made for themselves in their earthly life. In conclusion, his Lordship drew a picture of the eternal happiness which would fall to the blessed, and urged his hearers to seek so to live, and so to trust and follow Christ that at the last they might be gathered into His barn.—The choir rendered the anthem" 0 come let us sing unto the Lord" in a manner which did them great credit, Mr. F. M. Darby, Mus. Bac., presiding a the organ. BAPTIST CHURCH, GOLBORNE-STREET.</p>
        <p>The harvest thahksgi 'ring services in connection with this church were held on Sunday, when the Pastor (Rev Herbert Dannico) preached morning and evening. In the afternoon, under the able leadership of Mr. T. Brougk (conductor) and • Mr. A.grow (organist), the choir rendered a service of song entitled "The Gathered Sheaves," solos being sung by Miss Keene and Miss Emily Bates. The connective readings were given by Miss Venda Taylor. Mr. Dunnico preached in the even- ing from the text, "And the residue thereof he maketh a god" (Isaiah chap. 44 first clause of verse 17). Although the picture represented in this and the preceding Terse he said was a crude one, yet with our 20th century civilization and all the increased facilities and advan- tages of the present day, many people were living. on no higher plane, first satisfying all the demands of their physical nature, and afterwards giving the " residue " to 'God. "First things first" was the lesson to be learned, and.) the, preacher pointed out that men were apt to confuse the materials of life with the principle which ought to govern life. There was no such distinc- tion as sacred and secular; all duty rightly performed could become sacred and righteousness should be the governing principle of life. Anthems were sung by the choir morning and evening, and the services throughout the day were well attended, the church in the evening being full some time before the commencement of the service. The church was, as iniprevious years, artistic- ally decorated with tokens of harvest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>ST. BARNABAS' CHURCH.</head>
        <p>ST. BARNABAS' CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The harvest thanksgiving services were continued on Sunday and were largely attended. The preacher for the day was the Rev. G. R. Oakley, vicar of Dearnley, near Rochdale, who at the choral celebration of Holy Com- munion in the morning, based his discourse on the words "I am the Bread of Life." In the afternoon he addressed a children's service. For evensong the church was filled, and the music was of a festal character. There were processional and recessional hymns, and the choir rendered the anthem" Let the people praise Thee," Mr. T. Evans presiding at the organ. The preacher took for his text the words "The harvest is past, the summer is ended, and we are not saved" and emphasised many lessons to be learnt from the harvest.—The offertories were on behalf of the choir fund. CROFT.</p>
        <p>The harvest thanksgiving services were held in Christ Church on Sunday. The weather was all that could be desired, and there were large congregations at each ser- vice. The following ladies were responsible for the decorations :—Pulpit, Mrs. Wadeson ; chancel, Miss Carr; font, Mrs. Lowe and Miss Brimelow ; windows, lectern and lamps, Mrs. Ingham and Miss Bate. In the morning the Rev. A. S. Cameron, Warrington, preached, in the afternoon the Rector (Rev. H. I. Wadeson), and in the evening the Rev. J. Preston, vicar of Hindesford. Special hymns and the anthems "Like as a father" (Hatton) and "Fear not, 0 land" (Simper) were effectively rendered by the choir, Mr. Herdis presiding at the organ. The offertories, which were in aid of the renovation fund, amounted to .232 2s. 21d. CAIRO-STREET CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>- • Services of thanksgiving for the ingathering of the harvest were held on Sunday, the preacher both morn- ing and evening being the Pastor, the Rev. Charles Harvey Cook. The building had been tastefully decora- ted for the occasion with flowers, fruit, vegetables, &amp;c., by ladies of the congregation. Special anthems wer) creditably rendered by the choir, and at the evening ser- yice an anthem composed by the organist (Mr. Muire was sung. The preacher made special reference to the harvest, and also spoke of the excellent work done by the District Nursing Society. The collections, which realized a good sum, as well as the fruit and vegetables were given to the District Nursing Association. PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHAPEL, FRODSHAM. At this chapel on Sunday,thanksgiving services were herd, and sermons were preached by the Rev. H. R. Didcook in the morning, and by the Rev. T. H. Kynas- ton in the evening, to very good congregations. in the afternoon a service of song entitled " Meadow Field" was rendered, Mrs. Didcock supplying the necessary readings. Tho interior of the chapel was tastefully aJorned with green foliage, fruit, &amp;c.</p>
