      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <p>tne ume ouvrzeres iron' me Rue .Vivienne, looking excited and bewildered.</p>
        <p>"Mon Dieu, Justine ! what has happened ? " cried Suse.</p>
        <p>"Oh, mademoiselle, mademoiselle! " was all the girl could say, and the tears began to stream down her cheeks.</p>
        <p>"Speak, Justine, for mercy's sake! What is it ? " "M. Paul—an accident ! " the girl gasped. Suse uttered a cry as of intense pain, and fell but into Mathilde's arms. She was not, however, the sort of girl to allow her feelings for long to take precedence of active measures. She heard Mathilde say somewhat angrily: " Allons, fillette, tell us quickly what has happened. Is M. Paul alive ? " ies, yes. tie is terribly hurt, but he is alive. An automobile overtook him as he was crossing the boulevard—a passer-by knew him, knew that lewss.mactennask,ie'slathce, and came to tell ils."</p>
        <p>Sue had by this time pulled herself together, and, raising her head from 3.lathilde's shoulder, expressed her intention of going at once to Paul. Poor old Pere Andre, he and his, ailments seemed to be entirely forgotten in the general consternatiom caused by the announcement of Paul's accident. Yet no one..there, not even Suse herself, felt more keenly sorrowful for what had occurred, than did. Pere Andre. For years past he had been devoting' his life to the bringing about of a happy union between these two young people, for Paul was the son of an old friend of Andre's, and from their cradle these two youngsters had been destined for each other.</p>
        <p>"Go at once to Paul—of course, she Must. r have been made most comfortable, and want for nothing. lam all right." So said the unselfish old man, in as cheery a voice as he could -command.</p>
        <p>Mathilde offered to stay with him, but on no account would, he keep her away from her husband and children. If use would send a messenger along to give him full particulars of the state in which she found Paul, that was all he asked.</p>
        <p>This, of course, she promised, and in less than half-an-hour from the time they had hung up the old man's stock-pot with so much interest, he was once more alone—alone, too, with thoughts that were far more sad and sorrowful than he had permitted those about him to perceive. Pere Andre felt that the hand of time had already been laid so heavily•upon him that. he would never live to reach the much-talked-of cottage at Asnieres; and now that Paul had been stricken, that dream of happi- ness seemed further off than ever.</p>
        <p>'What would become of his little Suse if they were both taken from her, and she left with her genallesse and her pretty ways to battle with a cold, wicked world. True, she had a good position as premiere . in one of the most renowned houses in Paris, but that position would not protect her from the troubled adventures that were constantly arising in such a city as Paris." They were dreary, depressing, ominous thoughts that crowded in the old man's brain as he lay there; and at last, weak and weary though he was, he crawled out of bed, took a key from a Corner where it was hidden, and opened a cupboard. From this eurth,.ara he prrtltuled an spo;:tless teapot which, %;41en be ti,4,k toff the lid, appeared to be crammed with papers. Bank-notes !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <p>Pere Andre was not Ilicn the pauper his neigh- bours believed him to be, and•yet lus was no iniser. This was Suse's dot, and nos: an inconsiderable ,lot either. For years at Pere Andre had been- steward—"strrintend:ita," as lies was called—to rich nobleman, e-110;" when he died, left his faithful servant an annuity. •</p>
        <p>'Scarcely a penny of this money had Andre touched. He had worked as long ..as he was able, and gained enough to pay his rent and meet his. small requirements; of late he had dipped sparsely into his own savings, most of which, hOwever, itha been used for Suse's eduction in the art of dress- making. He could not leave her his income since it died 'with him, but he would save it for her—and this he did, and yearly invested it for her benefit. None of this did Snse know, nothing even of the hoard of bank-notes in the teapot.. If she had, she would have been very angry with thoold man for stinting himself to enrich her—for -Susowas indeed his child. She had inherited his selflessness and his generosity of iemperament. Having possessed himself of the notes. Old Andre sat down by the fire to count them. The amount was satisfactory— a thousand francs: forty pounds in English money. It was his yearly allowance, and he had intended to have invested it ere this, but illness had over- taken him. He returned thirty pounds to the teapot, locked it carefully .away, tied up the remainder, placed the. sum inside his pillow, and finally, utterly exhausted and chilled, crept. back to bed. He had scarcely accomplished this when he once' more heard a heavy footfall on the stairs. Mathilde had returned. Good, worthy Mathilde. Old Andre's surprise was great, ,it was only equalled by his pleasure at seeing her, for he 'was very weak and ill.</p>
        <p>"I could not go home, and leave you like this, she said. "I sent the charrette by a good-natured girl, who has promised to see to the children. My man is away, working on Merrimont Farm for a few days. Dieu Pere Andre, you do look white, and the soup won't be ready for hours. Coffee and cognac, that will keep you up !" "But Paul !" he asked. Is there any news?" "News! Of course," said Mathilde, busying herself the while making some coffee from a tin she found on the Mantelshelf. "IYo you think I should have been away all this time except to inquire about M. Paul ? ' - "Boa! And the news of him? "</p>
        <p>"Not encouraging, mom vieux. His thigh is broken. It is an affair of weeks. Pauvre diable !" "But he will recover ? " "Yes, yes, only may be lame for life." "Poor Paul! , Poor little Suse And I can do so little !" "Mademoiselle Suse does not know about the lameness. it is of no use to tell her all the malheurs One time. Come, M. Andre, here is the coffee; unless you have some cognac here, I Must fetch some."</p>
        <p>"On the little shelf above your head a tiny bottle--"</p>
        <p>It was only just in time, for Andre, from weak- ness and emotion, was nearly fainting. Mathilde's mixture revived him, but he was totally incapable of eating the huge slice of bread which she pressed him to do. Once again he asked for Suse. "She is coming soon, ".was the answer. "They won't let her stop at the hospital ; and the Pat ronne at the Rue Vivienne says that she is to come to you. When she comes, I will go home, so rest content, mom trieux; all will turn out for the best. Even if M. Paul is lame, he can print." When at last Suse arrived and good, motherly Mathilde etarted• for her home, the notes front under Andre's pillow were given to "the child." Suse cried her heart out Over the old grand- pere's munificence, thinking the while how he must have deprived himself for her sake, and wondering how he had managed to get so much 7noney. Of the pension left him by his late master, even now Suse beard no word, nor how, since she was only eight years old when the Comte died, it had accumulated into a bic, sum. The days that followed ° were for the most part very sad and drear, gladdened though they were by kindliness and a total want of self-considemtion. Mathilde was a constant visitor, and, thanks to her care, old Andre was beginning to pull round; her efforts, too, were aided by the old man's keen desire to live so that Suse might be the richer. At last, after many weary months, Paul left the hospital, and not until then did Suse learn the terrible truth that he would never walk again save with the assistance of a crutch, or at the best, a stick.</p>
        <p>"'She would marry him at once, marry him and nurse him," was Suse's decision, and it was echoed by Andre, only Paul thought that since he could not work, it was wrong to marry Suse ; but he was overruled. 0 _ . _</p>
        <p>The cure made every arrangement for him to be carried into church, and married in the early morning before curious idlers would be about. Mathilde and old Andre, who could now manage to get 'about a little, were the only witnesses. All thoughts of the cottage at Asnieres must •be given up for a while; but Sum said they would all three live together, end be very happy for all that. She, of course, retained her: position as premiere in the Maison Fournie ; and although it was some months before Paul was able to go back to work, and he was always delicate and ailing, yet; thanks to Suse's s-dary and the savings of the little old man of the Datignolles, the pot au was never unfilled, and a hearty welcome and a good meal were always awaiting commere Math ilde. [IDE, END. 1</p>
        <p>A steady improvement in the coal trade is reported, and those with long experience say that this is a much better sign than a sudden jump up. An average of four or four and a half days per week is now being worked Lancashire collieries. • at</p>
        <p>_ _ _ _ _ _ _ - - - `--,,rs*Prorwir, - , • "</p>
        <p>lOW</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
