      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>AN OLD MAN'S DARLING.</head>
        <p>AN OLD MAN'S DARLING.</p>
        <p>BY JEAN DI IDDLEM A SS,</p>
        <p>Author of "In S David Lisle, Lord of All,</p>
        <p>"The Case of "Love is</p>
        <p>Everybody knew him, and yet no one knew any- thing about him. He was a mystery.</p>
        <p>Though he was a . little old man, with eagle's eyes, there was a tender expression about his lips that never failed to touch those who came ill contact with him • while they regretted the generally hungry looic of his face. He went by the appellation of "Pere Andre," and lived quite alone in a tiny, half-furnished apart- rnent in the Batignolles. He did his own little hit of cooking—such as it was—and there was not one among the commeres who sold provisions in the market close to his house, who did not treat Pere Andre more generously than the rest of their customers. Thus the few pence he expended on his weekly pot au fpu gave him, thanks to their generosity, several good meals.</p>
        <p>For several days the kindly market folk had seen nothing of the little old man, who for several years had been a constant visitor to their stalls. Even when he had not been on purchases intent the cheery voices of the vendors did him good and inspired him with fresh life; for it was dull and still enough in his dreary apartment where there were none to communicate with him save the silent voices which came from the books which lay littering all over the floor, or the bubbling of the cremailliere when he hung it up to warm the soup which was his sole sustenance.</p>
        <p>" Tiens, where can Pere Andre be? Some mis- chief must have befallen. I don't go back to my man and my children this blessed day without going to inquire about him," said buxom Mathilde, as she was loading a little hand-cart with the vegetables she had not sold, preparatory tO a five- mile walk to her country home.</p>
        <p>"Take care, Mathilde," answered a lean, care- ful woman ; "if there's much of an illness you may be let in." -</p>
        <p>"Saints above! And shall I refrain from help- ing on that account? Take care lest you find no one to give you a hand when you are in want, Mere Frugalite," and, the last carrot tossed on the cart, the tarpaulin cover well tied down, off trotted Mathilde to the side street, where she believed that the old man dwelt. The concierge or caretaker was 4 cobbler. Black and grimy he was working at his window, awl in hand.</p>
        <p>"Le Pere Andre, does he live here?" asked ISLEathilde, who knew the concierge from his visits to the market, knew, also, that he was morose and disagreeable.</p>
        <p>"Path floor—door to the right," was the surq answer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <p>"Is he ill ? " asked the com;nere. A shrug and a grumble were the reply. "Too much to do to attend to the ailments of the locataires," "It is to be hoped that the loeataires will have too much to do to attend to you when you are ill," growled Mathilde, as she beckoned to a comrade to look after her hand-ca' ll in the street and then she trotted off up to the fifth storey in order to ascertain, if possible, what was the matter with Andre.</p>
        <p>She knocked at the door to the right, and some- what to her surprise it was almost immediately opened by a bright-faced young lady, whose appearance was scarcely in keeping, either with the poverty-stricken apartment or the -neighbourhood in which it was situated.</p>
        <p>Mere Mathilde dropped a clumsy curtsey. "Pardon, mademoiselle, I have made a mistake in the d00r... I came to inquire after Pere Andre." "No, no, it is all right. He lives here." "Is he ill, mademoiselle? He has not been in the market for •</p>
        <p>"Entrez, Mere ,Mathilde, entrez," came the old man's voice from the interior of the room. "Que vow Nes bonne, good, very good." - Thus invited Mathilde went in and her country- bred mind was shocked by the dirt and untidiness that greeted her. "If she had only known- she would have come and righted things up a bit," she thought. Whether Pere Andre Would have thanked her for this was, however, very doubtful.</p>
        <p>"Why didn't the girl do it ? " was .the next thought. Then she once more came to the conclu- sion-that she was too much of a lady. What little breath Mathilde had left after mounting the stairs and at the top falling in with such an unexpected apparition of grace and beauty, however, once more nearly deserted her when Andre said: "This.is_my granddaughter, la petite Suse."</p>
        <p>"Ah !" in a long gasp. - Then a goodly bunch of carrots and turnips and sweet herbs, which had (-:onsiderably contributed to the toil of mounting unaccustomed stairs, was laid upon the table. "Some legumes for the pot on .feu, a little offering .for old friendship's sake. I hope mademoiselle will not be offended?" "Offended, madame! Why should I be offended? It is so good of you to think of le pere' " and Suse's arms. were round good old Mathilde's neck.</p>
        <p>The girl was quite natural and simple-hearted in spite of her pretty face and dainty clothes. "I love everyone who is kind to grand-Tere," she said, 'noting the. comnu)re's surprise and pleasure.. "He Jae been so (-rood to me. He saved, and saved, and saved', until he had me taught thoroughly, and put me into, a large couturiere' s business in the. Rate Vivienne, and when I have made pots of money grand-pere is going to live with Paul and me in a chalet outside Paris. It is the dream of my life." Mathilde lookcd at the emaciated form of. the 'little white-faced old man who lay on the bed, and she doubted the realisation of the dream, but all she said was: "Ben.- So there is aM. Paul."</p>
        <p>"Myfittwee," and the girl blushed as she thought perhaps she had been too gushing to this stranger. The old man, however, set matters right by murmuring: -</p>
        <p>"Good -boy, Paul. My children are both very good. All I have left on earth—all—the rest have gone before." "We are going to make up for them, dear grand- father. You are not to think about a sad past, but of the bright future, when you are well and strong again, living witlt..its among the lime trees at Asnieres."</p>
        <p>"It's a long way otf,"' he murmured,</p>
        <p>"Not at an. Paul is already I)oking for a little chalet. He has got such a large rise lately in the printing-office, yent can't conceive how much the patron ' thinks of my Paul." - The latter part of the sentence was addressed as information to Mathilde, who was Wiping her eyes with the 'corner of her large checked apron, so touched was she at witnessing the affection that existed between the old man and the young 'girl. She Was thinking, t-o, of her own heme, where bickerings and strife were frequent, though they Were as wormwood to kindly Mathilde. But her man was not—altogether desirable or controllable. Would .the babies, for her children were still young, ever be to her as Suse WttS to old Andre? Mathilde was, however, too practical to be much of a dreamer. She had come there to be helpful, and she meant to help, eo she set to work with Suse's,assistance to tidy up, and between them the room soon presented a more comfortable appearance. Nor were the old man's Lares and Penates unduly disturbed, since he • gave directions as to the handling of these, to him sacred objects. Suse ran down to a boucherie on the opposite side of the street. and speedily returned with a larcrer piece of beef than had been in that stock-pot-since Andre was its possessor, and with Mathilde's vegetables the pot au feu was forthwith set a-going. Then came the time for Mathilde's departure since she had a lent, trudge home.' She-would look in on next market-day, she said. Just however, as she .was taking her leave there came a knock at the outer door. Suse opened it and uttered -an exclamation when she. saw 021 A a</p>
