      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>SMALL-PDX AT DEWS BURY.</head>
        <p>THE SMALL-PDX AT DEWS BURY.</p>
        <p>There is a good deal of uneasiness in the out-districts, as well as in Dewsbury, at the continued prevalence of small-pox. Fresh cases are occurring daily in every part of the affected area, and the present hospital is taxed to its utmost capacity. On Tuesday, there were two more deaths in the Dewsbury Hospital, and six dangerous and seven serious cases were reported. In the Thornhill district there were •four fresh cases and two deaths, one of the victims being Mr. Barstow, the son of Mr. J. J. Barstow, secretary of the Dewsbury Pioneer Industrial Society. In Ravensthorpe three fresh cases were reported, and until the new wing at the Dewsbury Hospital is completed some of the patients will have to be treated at, their own homes.</p>
        <p>The Town Clerk of Dewsbury (Mr. H. Ellis) has received a letter from Mr. Speight, the secretary of the local Tradesmen's Association, stating that his com- mittee were of opinion that a medical man should be appointed to deal with smallpox cases alone, and that he should have an inspector to act with him. The letter also complains of the absence of Dr. Clay, the Medical Officer, who is on a holiday, and expresses the opinion that all officers appointed to deal with small-pox cases ought not to ride in public conveyances or mix in public company.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wilfred Trub.shaw, assistant prosecuting solicitor to the Liverpool City Council, has accepted the post of assistant solicitor to the Lancashire County Council. His primary duties in the new appointment will be to attend to quarter sessions work.</p>
      </div>
