      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>EXAMINEE,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINEE,</p>
        <p>OCTOBER Ist, 1904.</p>
        <p>"We khow what, in Mr. THE COLONIAL CHAMBERLAIN'S scheme, we "OFFER." are to give," writes Lord</p>
        <p>ROSEBERY in the "Times." "What we are to obtain in return we know not. That he has never told us. And yet it is the essence of the matter." To this the "Times " replies that we cannot know until we have negoti- ated with the Colonies; and it taunts Lord ROSEBERY with a readiness to let "Imperial or national interests perish rather that he should have to revise his Cobdenite creed." From Aus- tralia it is reported that Mr. WATSON, the Leader of the Labour party, is about to institute an active propaganda on behalf of preferential trade with the United Kingdom, and the " Times " says that Mr. WATSON is the true representative of Liberal principles, while Lord ROSEBERY stands for the" rankest Toryism, the most obstinate obscurantism, masquerading in the robes of what was once, temporarily and by accident, comparative Liberalism." This is amusing when we consider that Mr. WATSON'S principles are by no means to the taste of the "Times," and have often been criticised by that organ in accents of displeasure. We do not know why any English Liberal should wince when he is told that his Liberalism is not of the Australian pattern. In the days when Mr. CHAMBERLAIN professed the Cobdenite creed he attached small weight to the argument, if it be an argument, that no English Liberal ought to be a Free Trader, seeing that Australian Liberalism is Protectionist. It would be just as irrational to contend that because the" Times" has turned Protetlionist it oueht to embrace all the doctrines of Mr. WATSON. Lord ROSEBERY stands on the perfectly sure ground that the national interests of Great Britain have been admirably served by Free Trade, and that Imperial interests cannot be benefited by schemes for reconciling this system with Colonial Protection.</p>
        <p>That is why the Liberal party in this country refuses to take the steps which the " Times" thinks so reasonable. " Why not negotiate," we are asked," and see what comes of it? You can- not find out what the Colonies propose to do until you negotiate. To refuse all negotiations is obstinate obscurantism." But what we do know is that Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S scheme is so disadvan- tageous to this country that even the most liberal concessions in Colonial markets would be no com- pensation. Let us take it for granted that Sir WILFRID LAURIER, Mr. REID, Mr. WATSON-, Mr. DEAKIN, and Mr. SEDDON would meet us in a most friendly spirit, and concede as much as they could. But they would still remain:Protectionists, and the bargain—if a bargain were made—would be all to their advantage, and not to ours. How could an arrangement so profoundly unsatisfactory to the , British taxpayer prove conducive to Imperial unity? For an 'Australian eye the policy of preferential trade has a natural attraction. " Australia writes a correspondent of the "Times," " wishes to expand the market for its foodstuffs. Neither Europe, America nor the East will take them. The policy of France and Germany has been to exclude them; and it is to the British markets of the United Kingdom and of South Africa that the people of the Commonwealth must look for their develop- ment. That there is scope greatly to increase the production of these commodities there can be no</p>
        <p>question." There can be no question that if we tax the same class of commodities imported ram foreign countries the Alstralian exporter will profit by the enhanced prices in our markets. But, as the "Times" correspondent goes on to remark, "the question of the return Australians will make has not been so much con- sidered. It seems desirable," he adds mildly, " that the Colonies should clearly understand that they are not to receive something :for nothing. Evid- ently this point of view is not prominent in Australia, and for the very simple reason that it does ,not bear examination. Tne Colonies know perfectly well that, in the very nature of things, it is impossible for them to make us any return which would be a real equivalent for what we should :surrender. This is the plain, business-like truth ; and to pretend that we can get rid of it by negotiation is the worst of blunders.</p>
        <p>The annual and usually thorny problem for Town Councils of settling the Mayoralty for another year has not given rise to very much anxious consideration in Warrington this time. The borough is in the unfortunate position of possessing very few men who are anxious for the honour of occupying the Mayoral chair,and at the same time willing to faithfully carry out the multi • farious duties of the office. Consequently the competition for the post is nil—a very undesirable state of things. It is poor testimony to the health-i ness of municipal administration locally that such a state of things should obtain. The cause is, perhaps, not very obscure. During the last few years the Corporation has been passing slowly and painfully through the flames of the purifying fire and can hardly yet be considered to have reached the last stage in the process. The representation of the varied interests affacting the ratepayers has not yet returned to the normal and it will be some time ere the Warrington Town Council reaches the standard which is both desirable and neces- sary. A year ago Alderman Bolton was with difficulty persuaded to undertake the duties of the Mayoralty, and in the ensuing period he has striven faithfully to honour the trust reposed in him. He himself would be the first to admit that he is not an ideal Mayor—the qualities for such a postare more special than general— but he has done his best for the interests of the town and on that account he deserves the warm. thanks of the com- munity and a measure of public gratitude which is redoubled by his acceptance of office for a second year. As he has himself remarked, the task is "no sinecure" and we hope he will have the cordial and sincere support of every townsman during the twelve months for which his lot is again cast in so high a place.</p>
        <p>A striking testimony to the wide-spread appre- ciation of Alderman Bolton's public spirit was evidenced by the representative charactet of the gathering at the Mayoral dinner on Thursday evening. The public men of Warrington and dis- trict assembled in full force, and there was an evident cordiality to his Worship which must have been very encouraging to him in his official capacity. After the dainty viands pre- pared for the occasion had been disposed of, there was the time-honoured toast-list, but we cannot truthfully say that the various speakers. imparted much that was original into their eulogies of the King and Royal Family, the Army and Navy, Clergy and Ministers, Town and Trade, etc. Perhaps it is too much to expect that ordinary human beings—and even Town Coun- cillors and Aldermen come under this head— should be able to invest the same old subjec with a new and living interest. They cudgel their brains manfully in the endeavour to do so, bu without any unkindness we must say that it is well their audience is, on such an occasion, in a tolerant frame of mind. Man is usually considered to be an amiable creature after dinner, and he is never loth to applaud post-prandial orations. Their inevitability may have some effect in reducing him to a pacific and not too critical disposition. The speech-making on Thursday was remarkable for the unanimity with which members of the Corporation found it necessary to pat themselves on the back as some recompense for the " censures and kicks ot jealous and weak-minded individuals" as Alderman Sutton delicately put it. Every man is" wiser in his own conceit than seven men that can render a reason,' says an Old Testament proverb. Verily so! But we would not deny our municipal governors a little self- glorification if it contents them. The reward of service at the Town Hall has been little more apparent than real for some time past. IR4CS Not much has transpired during the week as to probable happenings at the November elections. The L.R.C. have shown commendable activity and at least two of the wards are to be contested in their behalf. Granted that theCommitte eare able to secure men of intelligence and sincerity to champion their cause, we shall be pleased to wel- come their presence on the municipal battlefield. Rumour has been busy during the week with the name of Mr. Henry Roberts, J.P., who, it is freely stated intends to seek re-election to the Town Council for either Whitecross or Bewsey Wards. A meeting of the Whitecross Ward Committee has been held during the week, but there was no reference to the possible candi- dature of Mr. Roberts, and the retiring councillor, Mr. Brough, was asked to reconsider his decision not to seek re-election. The appear- ance of .11r. Roberts in the lists will naturally create considerable interest, as the . circumstances of his resignation are still fresh in the public memory. There is much surmise as to the attitude he will take towards questions which have agitated the minds of the ratepayers so greatly during the past two years. From what is definitely known, however,it seems clear that ths elections a month hence will be vigorously fought all round.</p>
        <p>It is somewhat unusual—and not altogether satisfactory—to find the speeches delivered at a maeting of shareholders of such an important trading concern as that of the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron Company Limited bearing a political complexion. It is one result of the tariff reform agitation and will become more and more evident as time goes on. In America the adoption of tariffs has made politics a business in which trading companies who wish to get a share of the pl'irns must participate. Nothing has tended moee to bring American politics into contempt than the methods whi2h , have been pursued by trusts and corners to shape ' legislation for their own advantage. Whatever faults may be laid at the door of Free Trade, it has at least kept our politics free from the degrading influences which we see at work in protectionist countries. Granting for argu- ment's sake all that Mr. W. H. Blecklv predicts with reference to the success of the " e strm, rdiohend " with his trump card of tariff f anyone believe for a moment that the reform, s proposed tariff of ten per cent. on imported manu- factured goods would be sufficient to exclude them ? Immediately,an agitation would be startEd for raising the tariff on this article and on that with the result that every trading company interested would be seeking some concession. The colonies, on the other hand", would ask for increased duties to be levied on foreign corn, or in turn threaten reprisals by taxing our manufactured goods. And so the game would go on. We con- fess we do not look forward to a reversal or change of our existing policy with the same confidence as the speakers at the meeting alluded to.</p>
        <p>Motoring has its dangers, as many in Warring- -1 ton can fully testify.. Yesterday, Dr. Markel was to be seen conducting a deputation from the Associated Chambers of Commerce over Messrs. Crosfield's works, with his arm in a sling. Inquiries revealed the fact that he was suffering from a serious injury to one of his fingers, a s the result of a motoringaccident. The cause of the accident shows the necessity of addressing a word in season on the abuse of the rule of the road. It appears that he was motoring in Norfolk afortnight ago in company with Mr. News", the chief engineer of the firm, when on rounding a sharp benl in the road both occupants were surprised to find a cart</p>
        <p>immediately in front of them on the wrong side of the road. In endeavouring to avoid a collision with the cart,the car was turned into the gutter and upset, both Dr. Markel and Mr. Newall narrowly escaping fatal injuries. In this 2ase the driver of the cart was unquestionably at fault. It is customary to lay all the blame for motoring acci- dents to the charge of the motorists, and there are some reckless motorists to whom little sympathy can be extended. These black sheep bring discredit upon motorists generally, and endanger the lives of all who use the highways. But in country dis- tricts the rule of Oa road is too loosely regarded by drivers of vehicles of all kinds, and many an acci- dent is attributable to the beglect to observe this simple rule. Our _highways are becoming more and more the avenues of swift locomotion, and a careless disregard of the rule of the road is criminal, •as it would be on the part of the locomotive driver or the mariner.</p>
        <p>Aftei an hour and a half of patient waiting at the Borough Police Court yesterday, a conscienti- ous objector to vaccination applied to Cue magis- trates for an exemption order. He was not even afforded an opportunity of stating his arguments, for immediately he made known the purport of his application, at least three magistrates, including the Mayor, withdrew from the Bench, leaving Councillor Thornton in sole pos- session. As the certificate requires the signatures of two justices, the applicant will be put to the trouble of again attending the court in the hope of gaining an hearing. We have heard a great deal of late about magistrates being bound "to administer the law." Presumably those who sat at the court yesterday came with that intention, and in common fairness they might have heard the application before declining to grant the order. Their action certainly savours too much of shirking an unpleasant duty.</p>
        <p>Cavaliere Pino says of his inventions for raising treasure from the sea that he holds "the simple key with which to open to man the mysterious virgin temples of the nymphs and of the syrens who, by their sweet singing, draw down men to see and to take those endless treasures." One is naturally curious about the key, and the descrip- tion in this month's "World's Work" almost sug- gests possibilities as romantic as the Cavaliere's own reference. The inventor is at present at work on twenty-one Spanish galleons sunk in 1702 off the North-west of Spain, containing treasure to the value of £28,000,000. The idea of the hydro- scope developed when Pino found that it was possible to read a newspaper in the sea at a depth of 100 yards. It consiats of a tube containing a ladder, capable of telescopic elongation, at the end of which is a room fitted with twelve great lenseE, through which subterranean objects may he as clearly seen as a summer landscape through field-glasses. The principle of the elevator is almost as simple. It consists of attaching flexible bags (,connected with a floating platform) to sunken objects, pumping compressed air into them and continuing to attach bags and to pump in air until the air overcomes the resistance of the water and the object bobs up like an indiarubber ball! Five cannon, chained together, are said to have shot to the surface like a cork.</p>
        <p>Index to Inside Pages. PAGE. 2. Women's °hatelid Fashions.</p>
        <p>"A Winter Pa ssion,"—§ll;VStory. Bowling. 3. Football. The St. Helen's Sensation. Events of the Week Illustrated. 6. The Municipal Elections. Passive Resisters in Court. South Lancashire Tramways. Police News. Billiards. 7. Harvest Festivals. Mr. A. J. Jolley on the Bible. Proposed Canal Amalgamation. "An Old Man's Darling." Short story.</p>
        <p>Temporary Authority. At the Borough Police Court yesterday John Looker was granted temporary authority to sell at the Hop Pole Inn, in lieu of Henry H., Follies. GREAT SALE OF BOOTS: AND SHOES now On UNDER THE CLOCK.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Miners and the Newton Division. The Miners' Labour Representation Committee, have declined to nominate a candidate for the Newton Division in place of Mr. Sam Woods, President of the, Lancashire Federation, who has retired owing to' ill health. They are concentrating their forces oh three' constituencies in the county, viz., Accrington, with Mr. T. Greenall as candidate, St. Helens, with Mr. T. Glover, and lace, with Mr. S. Walsh. The prospects are reported to be good in each division. Fire Alarms. At 4.50 on. Monday morning P.C. Gibbs was goilg. off duty in Bridge-street when he noticed some electric wires in flames at the shop of the Mersey Cycle Coal-- pany. He at once obtained buckets of water and the tire was extinguished in a short time—Several articles of clothing which were hanging on a rail in the kitchen of the King's Arms Hotel became ignited at 8.50 on Tuesday morning. As the flames were spreading: aC alarm was raised and the Fire Brigade turned out with an engine and hose. When they arrived the fire ha d been extinguished with buckets of water.</p>
        <p>H. M. ASHTON'S LIFT TONIC is the KING or TONICS. I 63. SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Daresbury Scholars' Treat. On Saturday afternoon the scholars attending: the Council Schools at Daresbury, Hatton, Moore; and Preston Brook had their annual treat. Over 300 children assembled early in the afternoon in a field kindly placed at their disposal by Mr. Joseph Billing ton, of the Village Farm, where sports of all kinds were indulged in. At four an excellent tea, prepared by many willing ladies in the parish, was partaken ef, after which a programme of sports for money prizeg was gone through until dusk. The children from Hatton School were kindly conveyed by lurry by gr. Richard Warburton, and those. from Preston Brook School by Mr Henry Whitlow,.of the Manor Parm.</p>
        <p>GREAT SALE OF BOOTS AND SHOES Row on UNDER TIIE CLOCK.—Advt. The Widening of Warrington Bridge.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the General- Purposes -Committee on September 26th a letter was read from Messrs Alexander Wilson and Cowie on behalf of Messrs- James Fairclough and Sons, Ltd., giving notice of the intention of that firm to take proeeedings by injunc- tion to prevent the alteration of Warrington Bridge by means of cantilevers and alleging that the proposed widening of the bridge would increase the risk and difficulty of navigating the river.—Resolved that the letter be referred to the Town Clerk. Conservative Labour Party Conference. In connection with the newly-formed Conservative Labour party, of which Mr. Frank Fearneley, of Salford, is the secretary, a conference of representa- tives from Lancashire, Cheshire, and West Yorkshire is to be held in Manchester to-day.—Resolutions are on the agenda asking the Government to adjust the legal position of trade unions, favouring the reforma- tion of the registration laws as applied to lodgers and those in receipt of outdoor relief, the introduction of 3 Redistribution Bill, the reintroduction of an Aliens - Bill, and the reconsideration of the fiscal policy. Councillor Holden, of Manchester, will preside, an d Mr. Fearneley will talk about the need of a Consery a tive Labour party.</p>
        <p>GREAT SALE OF BOOTS AND SHOES now on UNDER THE CLOCK.---AdVt.</p>
        <p>Warrington and the Church Congress. In connection with the Church Congress which meets at Liverpool next week, an exhibition Of ecclesiastical art is being held in the Gymnasium, Mrytle-street. The Rector (Canon Willis) and church- wardens of Warrington Parish Church have sent a silver-gilt chalice, said to be a " Hanap " cup (these were used for secular purposes,chiefly to hold treasure money, &amp;c.), its date is 1615 ; and a silver flagon u.f 1699, which stands 15in. high to the top of the lid and weighs 1450 z. 17cwt. From the Rev. L. J. T. Darwall of Walton comes a cope which was worn by the late' Rev. L. Darwall, of Criggion, Shrewsbury, at cele- brations of Holy Communion from 1852 onwards, the Rev. L. Darwall being " the first to restore a distiac- tive vestment at Holy Communion, beginning in 1848."</p>
        <p>SUGAR is DEARER.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Warrington Draughts League. The Warrington Draughts League is to be con- gratulated upon the acquisition of the new Champion- ship Cup, presented by Mr. R. A. Naylor,. for annual competition among the Draughts Clubs of Warrington and District. The Cup is of a remarkably bold design, stands over 18 inches high, and is surmounted by a figure of" Victory," holding with outstretched arm a wreath of laurels. Upon one side, is an oval panel, and:the cup bears the following inscription :-- Warrington and District Draughts Clubs Champion- ship Cup. Presented by R. A. Naylor, Esq., 19034." Upoil. the other side a draughts board is neatly engraved. The artistic embellishments are .of a most ornate description, and taken altogether the Cup is el exceedingly fine specimen yf the silversmith's art, salu' must be seen to be appreciated. It is at present on view in the shop window of Messrs. Dolan, clothiers, Horsemarket-street, along with the League medals, won by Cairo-street Draughts Club, the first holders. It has been supplied by a local firm, Mr. J. FinlaY- son, Horsemarket-street, and reflects the highest credit upon the designers.</p>
        <p>GREAT SALE OF BOOTS AND SHOES now on</p>
        <p>lUND-FTR—;HT: • " To Prevent Cruelty to Children. With the commendable object of assisting the funds of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children, a sale of cakes, confectionery, &amp;were and dairy produce was held in the crushroom at the Parr Hall on Tuesday; The sale had been. promoted by the members of the local branch of the League d Pity, which is the children's section of the N.S.P.C.C• The young ladies connected with it had invited their friends .to contribute some gift to the stalls, and the large number of articles offered for sale on TuesdaY was pleasing evidence of the hearty response. 011ie stall was devoted to flowers and dairy produce an another to cakes and confectionery. In addition iiiera was a cafe chantant where tea and toothsome morsels were served to the pleasing strains of a ladies'orchestra. Vocal items were also contributed by gig°, Birchall, Miss Polly Houghton and instruraentaL trios by Mrs. Willett, Mrs. C. Jackson Holinl and Miss Bouchier.—There was a large attenu- ance of ladies at the opening ceremony, which was performed by Miss Binadbent, who has taken such. a deep interest in the work of the society, since the in- auguration of the Warrington branch. In a few well- chosen remarks she observed that it would be hardl9; necessary for her to press the claims of the National Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children - She was sure it was a society whose work must appeal to everyone, for the gotid which it did in preventing cruelty was even greater than the improvement it affected in those cases which happened to come uncle their notice. They had in Warrington a most adtnir able inspector, whose whole heart was in the work' and she thought the society deserved more generou and whole-hearted support than it received. Th League of Pity was the children's branch, and th idea of it was to enlist the sympathies of the haPPY children of the land on behalf of the unhappy ones- The members were expected to collect net less than ss. .. a year from their friends ; or, better still, give up some of their Olr„12 pocket money for the good cause; or, best of 9-"' arrange some little sale or entertainment like that one, which had the double advantage of making inert.° money and interesting more people in the wurl' When the members reached the age of 10 they beegraieci honorary members, and then it was hoped they wow extend their work on behalf of the society; hat' unfortunately, when they reached that age their interest seemed to flag, and they wrote to say the)" wished to retire. She would have thought that, on the contrary, as they got older and got to know some; thing of the saddet side of life— which they conceale" from little children—they would do all they could to alleviate it. Miss Madeley, the hon. secretary of the branch, who had been ably supported by Miss Hilda Leigh and Miss Elsie Taylor, had worked with th,e greatest possible energy, enthusiasm, and judgruell" and she (Miss Broadbent) hoped all would do their best to make the sale a great success by buying everr thing they saw before them. (Applause.) The youog. ladies in charge of the stalls then commenced busineg,' in earnest, and as a result of the sale the societzig. funds will benefit to the extent of IL?. The star holders were Miss 31adeley -(who also ettleitietth discharged the dutie3 of hon. secretary), Miss s Leigh, Miss Elsie Taylor, Miss Irene Roberts, Daisy Hesketh, Miss Perris, and Miss Marie Rand'</p>
      </div>
