      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <p>New Clock at the Police Court. A large new clock has this week been fixed above the gallery at the Borough Police Court. Its intro- duction supplies a long-felt want. Local Will. Mrs. Sarah Robinson, of Latchford, left estate valued at £2,305 gross. Probate of her will has been granted to her nephew, Mr. Frederick William Dalby, farmer, of Compton, near Collingham, York. Personal. The Rev. D. R. Davies, BA., St. John's College, Cambridge, also of St. Michael's College, Aberdare, has been licensed to the curacy of St. James', Latch- ford. Mr. Davies commences his duties this week. Mr. Charles E. Parker has been nominated by the Association of Nonconformist Elementary Schools for the vacancy on the Education Committee caused by the resignation of Mr. E, J. Guest. BASSMETTES AND MAIL CARTS in latest art shades.—J. S. G ARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. The Rate of Mortality. The Registrar-General reports that the annual rate of mortality last week in seventy-six great towns ol England and Wales averaged 16 per 1000. The rate in London was 13, Liverpool 21, Birkenhead 20, Wallasey 13, Bootle 16, St. Helens 17,Wigan .16, War- rington 21, Preston 17, and Barrow-in-Furness 10. The Warrington and District Electric Light and Power Co. Ltd. The "London Gazette" of Tuesday contains the following :—Notice of Dividend.—The Warrington and District Electric Light and Power Company Limited, Warrington: first and final dividend, 4d. payable October 3rd, at 4, Egypt-street, Warrington FOR CLASS AND CHINA. of every description try J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street. Warrington.—Advt. Girl Shockinglypnjured. A girl named Nellie Helsby, aged between four and five, while crossing or playing in Church-street, Frod- sham, on Tuesday morning, stumbled in front of a horse and vehicle. She was knocked down by the horse, which trod on her hand and kicked her in the stomach, and the wheel of the cart passed over her upper arm, badly damaging it. The Anthrax Scourge in Cheshire. The anthrax scourge in Cheshire has now spread to sheep on the farm of Mr. Robert Wade, Aston Grange, Sutton. Four sheep died suddenly and mysteriously. Mr. Ferguson, veterinary surgeon, Warrington, made an ,examination, and certified that death was due to anthrax. The carcases were cremated and the farm premises thoroughly disinfected. It is unusual for the deadly disease to attack sheep. Fire at a Wireworks. About four o'clock on Thursday afternoon a fire broke out in the dipping department at the works of Messrs. Rylands Bros., wire manufacturers, Church- street. The fire brigade were promptly on the scene, but in the meantime the flames had been got under control by the employees with the aid of sand, It was found unnecessary to use the fire engine, but a hose pipe was applied and in less than an hour the fire was quite extinguished. The damage was slight. ' Music in the Park. On Sunday next at 3.15 the Legh-street Brass Band will occupy the stand, and will render the following programme, Mr. Fred Podmore conducting :—Hymn, "0 Lord of Heaven," (Dykes); march, "Austria," (Nowstry); selection, "Hymn of Praise," (Nlendels- *John); concert piece, " War March of the Priests," (Mendelssohn) ; anthem, "The Mighty Lord," (Beet- hoven) ; solo, "Comfort ye," (solo Trombone, Mr. J. Forster), chorus, "And the Glory," (Handel) ; march, "The Fighting Fusilier," (Hawkins); "God save the King." 1</p>
        <p>As for CONNIN'GHA.M'S HIGH-CLASS MINERA WATERS.—Adyt. Financial Result of the Lancashire Show. The Council of the Royal Lancashire Agricultural Society, which met on Thursday in Manchester, under the presidency of Mr. John White, of Warrington, advanced the salary of Mr. Edward Bohane, the secre- tary, to £5OO a year, with an allowance of £250, if necessary, for clerical assistance.—A report on the financial results of the Southport show showed that the receipts amounted to £9,700, a deficit, as com- pared with the Manchester show, of 11,890. The local committee's contribution was less by £1,150 than at Manchester. A saving, however, had been effected in the expenditure which would enable them to show a Profit of probably £5OO. • Death of Mr. Mel. B. Spurr. Many Warrington concert-goers will regret to hear that news was received in London on Tuesday that Mr. Mel B. Spurr, the well-known entertainer, author, and composer, died at Melbourne on Sunday, while on tour in Australia. The deceased artiste had appeared at Parr Hall, Warrington, and his refined wit and versatility hadzained for him a wide popularity. Mr. Spurr was a familiar figure at ;several of the best- known hydropathic.establishments in this country,and there he picked up the material for many of his funny sketches in the George Grossmith manner. A great many. of his recitations and songs werehis own com- position, and some of them, such as "When I was a boy at school," had a great vogde. He was nearly always his own accompanist, either on the piano or Zither. Mr. Spurr was a Freemason, and always wel- come at gatherings of the craft. ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Adyt. A Frodsham Merchant's Affairs. The public examination was held on Wednesday, at -the London Bankruptcy Court, of Henry George Linaker, lately carrying on business at Frodsham, Cheshire, as a coal and lime merchant and carting contractor. The statement of affairs showed total liabilities £5,725 2s. Bd. (of which M,478 15s. 11d, is unsecured), and assets £312 16s. 3d. In the course of his evidence, the debtor stated that after having been employed as a clerk to district authorities in Cheshire, he in January, 1892' with £250 capital, commenced the above-mentioned business. For a time he traded With success, but latterly at a loss; and in October last he sold the business, exclusive of the book debts for £l5O. He then' removed to London, and had traded as a house and estate agrnt at 14, North Audley- street, W. under the name of Biscoe and Biscoe. That business proved unsuccessful, and was abandoned last June. Since then he had been without occupation. The examination was concluded. Loyal Order of Ancient Shepherds—Warring- ton Branch. ~, At the fortnightly meeting of the Morning Star -Lodge No. 1043 of the above District, on Saturday a very interesting and pleasing ceremony took place, viz., the initiation of Mr. John Riley, of the Big Horse Srnhoe Inn, Horsemarket-street,as an honorary member. The Lodge was crowded to its utmost capacity by ' )zembers and visiting brethren, amongst the visitors heing the officers of the District, Pro. C. S. Bro. Joseph Ashfon, D. Pro. C.S. Bro. Joseph Mather, Dis. Sec. Bro. Peter Edgar, Dis. Treasurer Bro. James Parr , and P.P.C.S. Bros. Joseph Adams, John Haley S. Massey, T. C. Duckworth, James Gresty, , William Hall, James Lowe and many other 'Prominent members of the Lodge. The core- was very impressively and effectively per- formed by the District Officers. A Smoking Concert , Was afterwards held. With songs,toasts and sentiments , happy evening was spent, and brought to a -close by the singing of the National Anthem. The harmony f the lodge was well sustained by Bros. Ratcliffe,' Lawler, Hall, Bibby, Williams, and Adams. Every 4!redit is due to Bro. Thomas Miller for his efforts in eEtrrying out the proceedings so successfully. H. 31. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC, is the KING OF TONICS.-63. SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. A Bold Colliery Fatality. 31r. Brighouse the county corner, held an inquest at the Griffin Inn, Bold, on Tuesday, on James Jones, Reci fifteen,a pony-driver at the Bold Golliery,belong- gto the Collins Green Company. Jones lived at 11,1 zrederick-street, Sutton, St.Helens, and was described 43 a very smart and' intelligent youth. On Friday l'aorning last, when he was driving a pony along the Dit with four full boxes of coal they broke loose, ran 4„way, and were overturned. Jones was found under, I.l'ae first one, quite dead.—James Bedson' of Reginald- :ad, Sutton, and William Stubbs who had instructed 'le boy in bis work, said they told him distinctly to tit two scotches under any waggons going down this but he said they had been able to manage with e. There was only one scotch under the box which lsed the accident.—Mr. Matthews, Government in- asector, said that the road was a very good one, and he °115;• nt the boy had been trying to manage with one e°t —The jury returned a verdict of" Accidental dea th." and the coroner, addressing Mr. Thompson, the Zallager, said he should saddle these boys with Zowledge when they went to these pits. He should it :re them a book in which an order to use these atelies was written, and then they would not be to escape responsibility if they did not carry out -"eau instructions.</p>
      </div>
