      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>• '"-vf ,</head>
        <p>• '"-vf ,</p>
        <p>MAYORAL DINNER.</p>
        <p>His worship the Mayor (Alderman Bolton) enter- tained a large number of his fellow-townsmen and other guests to dinner at the Parr Hall on Thursday • evening. There were about 130 gentlemen present in- • cluding the members of the Corporation and Borough magistrates, and the affair was one of the largest and most representative of its kind ever held under the auspices of the Chief Magistrate of the Borough. The Hall had been handsomeiy decorated for the occasion by Mr. Parker, head gardener, whose choice array of plants and shrubs gave an effect exceedingly pleasing to the eye. The catering was, as usual,in the capable hands of Mr. Powys, who served an excellent repast at 6.45 p.m. Excellent music was provided by Mr. R. H. Pearson's orchestra wh) submitted a mist attractive programme of popular airs and dances. His Worship the Mayor presided over the gather- ing . The following accepted invitations:— Aldermen Fairclough (deputy-mayor), Bennett, Evans, Francomb, Hutchinson, . Pierpoint, Smethurst and Sutton; Councillors Ashcroft, Barton, Brough, Burton, Cinik, Davies, Duncan. Ellison, Evans Forshaw, Hulse, Lord, Maginn, A: Peacock, P. Peacock, Pemberton, Roberts, Rogers, Sankey, Thornton, Tinnion, Wilkinson and Wilson; Dr. Fox,Messrs. T. Birchall, R. T. Fairclough, J. W. Hayward, J. Hepherd, F. Monks, F. W. Monks, H. Roberts, S. Wright, C. S. Burgess, C. J. Holmes, G. Pilling, W Richardson and W. W. Roberts, (justices of the peace) ; Rev. M. Stevenson, Messrs. W. Bibby, A. J. Jolley, T. May, E. Plinston, J. Fulton Smith, J. Upson, G. Walker, and C. H. Unsworth, (membel s of the Education Committee), Messrs. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), T. Loagdin • (borough surveyor), J. Fairhurst, (borough treasurer), G. F. F. White, magistrates' clerk), Dr. G-ornall (medical officer of health), L. Talbot (chief constable), Mr. W. S. Haddock (gas engineer), Charles Madeley (librarian and curator), F. V. L. Mathias (electrical. engineer), .T. Teller (tram- ways manager), J. M. Murray (director of education), S. Wilson (deputy town clerk), W. H. Woodcock collector), A. M. Ker (assistant borough surveyor), E. Place (markets inspector), F. G. Ruddock (public analyst), C. B. Aylward (art master), E. S. A. Robson (headmaster of the Technical School), J. Lister (post master) S. Brighouse (coroner), Revs. E. C. E: Carleton and B. Percival, Sir. Gilbert Greenall, Bart.; Colonel Richardson, Major Appleton, Captain Crosfield, Dr. Markel, and Messrs. A. H. Crosfield, R. G. Henshall, H. Greeaall, C.E Parker, W. Skinner,C. W. Needham, J. Bolton,J. Longland, J. Molar°, Vere B. Davies, R. H. Meldrum., W. B. Roberts, E. Parry, I. T. Williams,W. Fairhurst H. Madden, A. Browne, E. Bradshaw, W. P. Rylands, W. D. Jeans, W. H. J. Jenkins, T. W. Picktnere, H. Ferguson, T. Locker, W. Milner, Alderman Burgess (Liverpool), T. Birtles, C. Bunch, T. Cooper, C. D. Parkinson, George S. Pendlebury, W. Shirlaw W. Fletcher, H. Chambers, and tha Mayors of Widnes and Leigh. There were a number of absentees and among those who sent apologies for non-attendance were :—The Mayor of Wigan, the Rev. J. P. Whittle, Colonel Edelsten, Mr. John White, Mr. Henry Fairclough, Mr. Charles E. Bunch, Mr. A. H. Crosfield, Dr. Gornall. The menu was as follows :—Hors d'Oeuvr4s : olives, saltedalmonds. Soup: turtle, oyster. Fish; turbot, lobster; sauce, red mullet, tartar sauce. Entrees: sweetbreads with mushrooms, compote of pigeons, sorbet of rum. Roasts; saddle of mutton, goslings. Game; grouse, partridges. Sweets; Croiltes 6 la Italienne, iced fruit salad, maraschino jelly, coffee eclairs; ice pudding, vanilla sauce. Dessert ; coffee. THE LOYAL TOASTS. _</p>
        <p>The Mayor briefly gave the loyal toasts, referring in suitable terms to the good qualities of his Majesty the King, and the virtue of her Majesty Queen Alexandra. He also spoke of it being a matter for satisfaction that the Prince of Wales was following in the footsteps of his father. Both toasts were received with musical honours.</p>
        <p>BISHOPS AND CLERGY AND ALL NIINISTERS OF RELIGION.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. SAWKINS BURGESS proposed this toast. Speaking of the bishops he said it was their constant aim and desire to rule the Church wisely and promote whatever tended to further the moral and spiritual welfare of the people. The clergy and ministers of other denominations were men animated by the highest and purest motives, ever ready to sacrifice themselves to advance whatever they considered to be for the good of their fellowmen. (Hear, hear.) The Rev. E C. E. CARLETON responded. THE ARMY, NAVY, AND AUXILIARY FORCES. Mr. HENRY ROBERTS proposed "The Army and Navy, and Auxiliary Forces." On rising,,he met with ahearty reception. He said with regard to the army they could say that in regard to personal bravery, devotion to duty, loyalty and everything else which made a soldier, the "Tommy Atkins" of to-day had no cause to be ashamed of his uosition in any respect. (Applause.) He wished he could speak equally well and in words of equal ap- preciation of the administration of thatgreat force also. Speaking in the light of recent events, they were bound to admit that the revelations of the Commission ap- pointed were such as brought at any rate a considerable amount of shame to the cheeks of very honest man. John Bull was a very liberal paymaster, and he (the speaker) was afraid he did not always get value for his money. In regard to that great branch of the public service, it was one of those things which ought not to be made the sport of parties,—(hear,hear.)—seeing that the interests of the whole empire were to some extent dependent upon the manner in which the army was ad- ministered. That branch at any rate should be left outside all party political consideration and dealt with upon its merits in regard to the duties it had to perform. There were one or two points in respect to it, speaking of course in the ignorance of a layman, which he would -like to emphasise. One was this, that no mere social considera- tion nor any personal influence even of the kind of support which was supposed to come from the fair sex should in the slightest degree have any influence in the selection of men who were responsible for the carry- ing out of the duties of the army. (Applause.) Every responsible appointment should be open to every man alike. Let the best man come to the top. (Applause.) In regard to the navy, he thought he might say that so far as he knew, they retained their confidence in the men they had at the head and that if necessary the navy when called upon would serve its part in warfare— (applause)--and that they would not be found wanting in any respect whatever. He now came to that branch of the service—the despised and neglected volunteer and militia. He felt strongly in regard to that branch of the service because from reasons which he need not enter into that night, largely of a financial cnaracter, there was no doubt whatever that, that branch of the service had been seriously neglected. (Hear, bear.) At one time of course, many people thought the volunteers would never be of any use except as men who went about rather fancying themselves and with a handsome costume. Time had proved differseitly. If it had not been for a number of men whom they were able to send out—and their little town had done its share in that respect—(applause)—to guard the communications and , who did the not less important but perhaps the less I honourable work in the fact that it was not brought so I frequently before the public notice, he was sure that the regular army would not have been able to do their work so thoroughly. (Applause.) The question of the individual reponsibility for the protection of hearth and home should be brought before everyone. He thought that with the man who enjoyed the privileges of the safety which characterised their home business for so many years the least he could do in return was to make himself efficient to take his part if ever that safety should become endangered. (Applause). Mr. Roberts proceeded to refer to the memorial fund to the South Lancashire Regiment and appealed to his hearers— though he supposed none of them had failed to sub- scribe—to do their utmost to raise theiamount required in order that Warrington very soon might have added to its very few statues, the important one which should commemorate the high esteem and regard in which they held those who had done their duty four years ago. (Applause.) Colonel RICHARDSON replied and thanked the com- pany for the manner in which they had received the toast. He remarked that he had been in the South Lancashire Regiment for 32 years. (Applause.) He hoped that the next time he came to Warrington the memorial could be in a more tangible form than it was at present. Sir GILBERT GREENALL, Bart., proposed the health of the Corporation of Warrington. The gentlemen who so willingly gave their services to the town were de- serving of their warmest thanks for the able way in which they filled their very difficult positions. They were well aware that no man could please everybody— (laughter)—but he thought they might give them credit for doing the best theyicould for the town. The Cor- poration had been most ably assisted in all their work by his Worship the Mayor. (Applause.) The higkest honour they could pay to him for the manner in which he had performed his duties was the fact that they had unanimously elected him to fill the office tot" another year. He coupled the toast with the names of two old and respected townsmen,Alderman Sutton and Alderman' Fmncomb. (Applause). Alderman SUTTON, in reply, said he thanked Sir Gilbert Greenall for the kind terms he had used in reference to the Corporation. They were so unaccustomed to be praised—(laughter)— that he wondered whether it was the good dinner, the good wine or the good company or the kindness of heart of Sir Gilbert which had caused him to use such kind expressions. (Laughter.) However it might be, he con- gratulated him for the very sensible way in which they had drunk to their really valuable services to the borough. (Laughter.) He thanked Sir Gilbert for the generous way he;had acknowledged the manner in which many members of the Corporation had given the best part of their lives to the service of their town. (Hear, hear.) Let him assure them that notwithstanding all the censures and all the kicks accorded to them by</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, October 1, 1904.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 1, 1904.</p>
        <p>jealous and weal: m i nded individuals—(laughter)—in return for the number of hours and weeks and months they gave to the service of the town, they deserved the best thanks of everybody. (Laughter.) Alderman FRANCOMB also replied, and remarked that after the many abusive names they had received during the part few years, it was refreshing to find that there were a few who did not mistrust them altogether. For his part during the past 24 years, he had done his duty faithfully and well to the best of his ability. As they knew, he was chairman of the Water Committee. In 1890 they acquired the Water Works from the old Water Works Company, and it had been a very great success, and at Winwick they had one of the best supplies in the county of Lancashire. Within a few weeks there would be water there endugh to supply three such towns as Warrington with water of an excellent quality for domestic parposes. At Appleton, however, there was a reservoir built 60 years ago, and at the present moment it was very much below the deep level. If the drought continued there would be absolutely no water for manu- facturing purposes. Last year they sold of Appleton water 155 million gallons for £25,000,025. This water was very soft and very suitable for manufacturing pur- poses, and they must remember that Warrington was strictly a manufacturing town. (Hear, hear.) If they did their duty to the manufacturers and the working classes, it was necessary to get cheap water. There was another scheme to make a new reservoir at the Bird's Wood, Daresbury, which he believed would hold 180 million gallons, and cost £lBO,OOO besides buying the land. This would come up for consideration of the Water committee, and he believed in looking to the futureand not standing still. It would be for the Council to con- sider the matter, and it would have to be considered very seriously. (Hear, hear). Alderman BURGESS of Liverpool, said he had to pro- pose a toast which was not in the programme and he was quite sure everyone would drink it most enthusias- tically and it was the health of his Worship the Mayor. (Applause). There were few in that room who were colleagues of his (the speaker) when he entered the Council, but Alderman Sutton and he believed Alder- man Francomb were. While asking them to drink the health of the Mayor he wished to mention the Mayoress —(applause)—who had performed her duties and ren- dered valuable services to the town.</p>
        <p>The IVlavoa replied and said he had always tried to do his duty to the town. He was glad the Mayoress had been mentioned and it was owing. to her foresight that they were there that night. (Applause.) He was glad to see that they had enjoyed themselves. The Mayoress had done all she possibly could to assist him during his year of office, and they ktiewhe was some- times a difficult man to manage. (Laughter.) There had also been some serious business during the past year. There was the Ship Canal action and also the Ship Canal Bill, and he thought that Warrington had not come out badly in that respect. Alderman Francomb had mentioned the Waterworks and he he thought the least said the better. (Laughter.) If they had taken his advice twelve years ago they would not have needed an Act•of Parliament to do it. In conclusion he thanked all for the manner in which they, had drunk his health, as he had always tried to do his duty to the town. (Applause.) Mr. W. P. RYLANDS proposed "The County Borough Magistrates," and said the position of magistrate was one of great peril. Sometimes it meant that the magistrate ran great risk of being shot by the prisoner at the bar, and there was no doubt that he ran very good risk of being shot by Mr. Labouchere in the pages of "Truth," (Laughter.) When they criticised the deceisions of magistrates they were like " Truth " ignorant of the general circumstances of the case. He believed that the magistrates of England were a shining light and an example to the other countries of the world as they did their duty conscientiously and without any reward. He was glad to be able to express his appreciation or the way in which the magis- trates performed their duties. (Applause). Mr. F. MONKS said that on behalf of the older magis- trates he thanked the company present for the manner in which the toast had been drunk. He thought that it showed that the general decisions of the Warrington Bench gave satisfaction as there had not been many appeals. They simply ilitt their duty and considered a case on its merits. He would ask Mr. Roberts to reply for the younger branch of the magistrates. Mr. H. ROBERTS expressed his relief that he was associated by Mr. Monks with the younger magistrates. He could say of the Bench that he did his duty without fear or favour, and he thought he could say the same for all his colleagues on the Bench. (Applause). • Alderman BENNETT proposed "The town and t of Warrington," and said he could not possibly have a more agreeable toast to propose. As a Warringtonian, he was almost, if he might say so, absurdly proud of Warrington, and was as patriotic a citizen as it was possible to be. There were many points about the town worth mentioning and when they had the centre of the town at Market Gate 130 feet in width, it would be a fine town indeed. Much had been said about the high rates, but if they had improvements, they must pay for them, and he did not believe that the rates would be permanently at their preseut level. (Hear, hear.) Councillor TvNroN replied to this toast and said that when he came into the town 35 years ago, his first im- pressions were not favourable, but now he did not think that in the whole of Warrington there was one more enthusiastic for the town. He would like to say that the progress of Warrington did not lie altogether with Warrington's sons. It was very greatly due to her adopted sons. Although trade was not very brisk at the present time he thought that there were signs that Warrington would be able to look forward to a period of prosperity. (Hear, hear.) The proceedings were brought to a close by the sing- ing of Auld Lang Syne " and the National Anthem.</p>
        <p>that time one of our important assets was a more or less nebulous item of £315,559 17s. 9d., representing the price paid to the vendors for the then estimated value of colliery leases and the goodwill of he business. The properties, as the original ' shareholders were soon painfully made aware, were acquired during a period of unusual inflation, and the asset I refer to consequently began, even in the early stages of the existence of the company, to be an incubus on the business, from the fact that a large proportion of it had in reality disappeared. It was early recog- nized, therefore, to be an absolute necessity to get rid of this item as rapidly as possible, notwithstanding the fact that such a policy would inevitably entail a diminu- tion of dividends. Fortunately, the directors have always held the largest proportion of the capital of this company, so that being the principal sufferers by such a policy, they did not hesitate to adopt it. When Mr. Harmood Banner, our present chairman, joined the Board in 1884, he promptly pronounced himself as being strongly in favour of this policy, which had already been initiated. It has been persistently carried out all these years, and the result is that from the accumulated funds the item of £315,500 in round figures will at last be entirely, and finally extinguished from our balance sheets after to-day, and there will then remain a substantial reserve fund of £llO,OOO. I trust you will forgive me for taking up more of your time than usual. My excuse is that to-day, from my point of view, is a red-letter day in the career of this eom- pany, to the great satisfaction of every member of your Board. I may say, however, it is more especially so to ray brother and myself, who have not only controlled the manufacturing departments of this company from the beginning, in 1874, but also those of the original manufacturing firms which were then absorbed. .(Hear, hear.) At present, we enjoy the strongest financial position we have ever held, and I trust with a reason- ably good demand for coal and an enlightened defensive commercial policy on the part of the Government of this Empire, that we have a prosperous future before us. (Hear, hear.) As regards the approval of the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</head>
        <p>HARVEST FESTIVALS.</p>
        <p>(Continued front Page 7). LEGH-STREET PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHURCH.</p>
        <p>The sorvices were held on Sunday and were largely attended,the church being crowded in the evening. The preacher for the day was the Rev. J. F. Matthews. In the afternoon a harvest cantata entitled "A daughter of Moab" was admirably rendered by the choir and friends, Mr. William Hickman presiding. At the even- ing service the anthem "Sing unto the Lord" was sang in a very creditable manner. Mr. A. Spruce conducted and Mr. F. Podmore, presided at the organ with his accustomed ability. The church had been beautifully decorated by the ladies of the congregation. The collections were- in aid of the trust fund and realized over £l3. On Monday evening asocial gather- ing was held. ST. JAMES'S, LATCH.FORD. The celebration of the harvest festival in connection with this church commenced on Thursday evening when there was a good congregation. The church had been artistically decorated by the following ladies :—altar, Miss Burgess and Mrs. Collins; font, Miss E. Gibbs; pulpit, Mrs. Carruthers and the Misses Parry; lectern, Mrs. Hallwood ; chancel screen, Mrs. Fargher and Miss Chester ; choir stalls Mrs Calvert and the Misses Jefferson, Peers and Hatton; windows, Misses Gibson, Hushes' Dillon, Booth, Brindley, and Livingstone; choir standards, Mrs. Mee,Mrs. Hilson,Misses Eckersley and Bradshaw. The preacher was the Rev. Canon Maitland Wood, vicar of Runcorn Parish Church, who based his discourse on the words "The earth, 0 Lord, is full of Thy mercy; teach me Thy statutes." Psalm caix. 64. The choir rendered the anthem" Fear n0t,.0 land," the soloists being Mr. Hill and Mr. H. Pevit. Mr. Davies presided at the organ. _ . _ ST. ANN'S.</p>
        <p>A fairly large congregation assembled in St. Ann's church on Thursday evening, when the harvest thanks- giving services were commenced. The church had been prettily decorated for the occasion by a number of willing workers including :—Miss M. Rigbey, Miss R Rigbey, Mr. and Mrs. Savage,Mr. and Mrs, Thomas, M r and Mrs. Colquhoun, Mrs. Downey, Mrs. Daniels, sen. Miss Peake, Miss Bunch. Miss Randall, Miss Foster and members of the Christian Endeavour Society. The preacher for the occasion was the Rev. G. Peters, vicar of St. Thomas's, Liverpool, and he was assisted by the Rev. J. Davies (vicar), and the Rev. D. Kerrin (curate), The preacher based his discourse on the text For the earth bringeth forth fruit of herself; first the blade, then the ear, after that the full corn in the ear" (St. Mark lye. 28v.). Nature he pointed out did nothing spasmodically or abruptly So it was in the spiritual life. If they were God's children, the work of Christ must go on—must go on continually.—The choir sang the anthem "While the earth remaineth " (Maunder). Miss Annie Hardman effectively took the solo part. Mr. Padmore presided at the organ with his usual ability.—The offertory was ou behalf of the Curate and Lady Reader's fund.</p>
        <p>ENIMANUEL CaURCH, BOLD-STREET. The harvest thanksgiving services in connection with the above were commenced last Friday, and were con- tinued on Sunday, In accordance with custom the church had not been decorated but the nlembers of the newly-formed Men's Union had undertaken to renovate the interior in their ow 3 time, and at their owncharges, and well had they done their work. The first part of the work having been completed by Thursday night the harvest thanksgiving services were all that could be desired. On Friday evening there was a fair attendance, the preacher being the Rev. G. H. Carnson. The ser- mons on Sunday morning and evening were preached by the curate-in-charge, Rev. F. Vaughan. A united ser- vice was heldtin the afternoon, when the Rev. J. F. Matthews preached to a large and representative gather- ing. At that service the Sunday schoolscholars brought large offerings of frilit and flowers for distribution to the sick of the congregation and the hospitals. The choir sang special anthems in a very creditable manner. The congregations on each occasion were large, and the collections, which realized £6,were devoted to the Sun- day School Building Fund. „</p>
        <p>Election of Deacons at Wycliffe. More than ordinary interest was taken this year in the annual election of deacons for Wycliffe Congrega- tional Church. A large number of members assembled on Thursday night in the Memorial Buildings, and proceeded to elect five deacons by ballot. Twenty persons had been nominated. The result of the voting was the election of Mr. Ziba Armitage,. Mr. Alfred Corney, Mr. Benjamin Farrar, Mr. Thomas Wallington and Mr. E. S. Garland.</p>
        <p>HAMS, BACON &amp; CHEESE A.RE DEARER.--Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>PEARSON &amp; KNOWLES COAL AND IRON Co., LTD.</head>
        <p>PEARSON &amp; KNOWLES COAL AND IRON Co., LTD.</p>
        <p>• 31st ANNUAL GENERAL MEETING.</p>
        <p>Foreign Competition.</p>
        <p>A PLEA FOR PREFERENTIAL TARIFFS.</p>
        <p>The 31st annual general meeting of the above com- pany was held at Warrington, on Thursday, the chair being occupied by Mr. J. -S. Harmood Banner, chairman of the Board of Directors. There were also present : Directors: Messrs. W. H. Bleckly, John J. Bleckly, George F. Pearson, Ed. Beck, Richard Brancker, and W. Peter Rylands ; James Dodds (secretary), W. Blease (auditor); shareholders: Messrs. John Knowles, J. Paul Rylands, I. Helliwell, H. S. Bleckly, J. Bleckly, jun., A. H. Burgess, W. J. Bleckly, William Richardson, A. S. Bleckly, and Charles E. Bunch. There were also present by ptoxy 205 shareholders whose holdings together with those of the shareholders present in person represented 109,994 shares, 0r£549,970 of share capital. The report and balance sheet, a summary of which has already appeared in the "Examiner," were taken as read. THE CHAIRMAN ON THE POSITION OF THE COMPANY.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. S. HA.RMOOD BANNER said: In submitting our annual report and statement of accounts to 30th June last at this our 31st meeting,whilst wedo so with some regret that ourprofits for the year are not larger and that we are unable to pay you more dividend, we also have some pride in pointing out that the balance sheet after adopting the recommendations contained in the report, is in a most satisfactory condition and that ex- cept by a reference in a side note which we may keep as a memorial stone of its previous existence, the item of good-will is dead and buried away from off the asset side of our accounts. The amount as you know, £315,559, was a large one to deal with. We may average our 31 years of existence by saying that we have written off £lO,OOO a year. As a matter of fact the burden has been heavier, for in the first years •of the company's existence it was not necessary to look at all our assets with the vision of a scrap heap before us as now all prudent men are bound to do. The intense foreign competition to which at least the iron trade is subject, and the enlarged powers of production both in coal and iron arising from wider pits, mechanical haulage, improved machinery,and electrical appliances are the features of the present day It was only in 1888 that we really began to tackle this business seriously, so that we may claim to have accom- plished the task in the last 16 years. It has been of the greatest advantage to us in financial matters that we have had the funds so retained to deal with, and, as'youl shareholders know, it required a large amount of un- selfishness on your part. We have had to pay smaller dividends, and from 1893 to 1896 no dividends at all,but the advantage is that wehave been able to meet the calls of our managing directors for additions and im- provements to.our works and collieries without incur- ring either a debenture debt, or a debt to our bankers. We stand therefore</p>
        <p>IN A VERY STRONG POSITION for what the future may bring forth. Let me give you one illustration. The t oppull Colliery, wh:ch is at Pre- sent the most profitable part of our colliery property, has been entirely paid for out of the moneys retained to write off goodwill and carried to reserve. Now, as regards the accounts themselves, there are only two items to which, I think, I need refer. The first is the amount due to creditors, which is £48,752. This is the only amount we owe, and as you will see from the other side of the accounts, irrespeotive of stock and book debts, we have £82,346 cash and bills in hand with which to meet it. You will, therefore, feel some confidence in our stability. The other item is the freehold and lease- hold land, machinery, plant, 8:c„ which stands this year at £565,798 ; againstlast year £560,811 ; or an increase of £4,987 and this sum we propose to write off out of profits. Now, as regards the profits, I think I may say that I was</p>
        <p>FAVOURABLY DISAPPOINTED with both the coal and iron results. They are not goo d,and compare :-1904, £46,747; with 1903, £53,426; and 1902, £62,864; but looking at the poor demand and lowering prices of coal and iron du:ing the last yeti., I did not anticipate that we should do so well as we have done, thanks to the energy of our management. (Hear,hear.) That was the reason why in our interim dividend in March we paid at a rate which must have been a disap- poktment to you. In the second half-year we have paid at the rate of 4 per cent. per annum, as against 5 per cent. in the corresponding period of last year. You could easily see by the newspapers how bad the coal trade has been, inasmuch as there have been two applications to the Conciliation Board for reduction of wages paid to colliers, both of which have been granted, viz., 5 per cent. in January, and 5 per cent. in August, and the evidence of reduction in price there shown is not the only measure of loss we sustain, as the poor demand entails working short time, which adds greatly to our cost, and reduces any profit there may be on sales. • You hear a great deal about the high price of coal, and how it ought to be further reduced, but when I tell you that the average net profit on each ton of coal works out this year at 6•27 d., you will see how narrow a margin there is for any complaint of an excess of profit paid to the coalowner, and how necessary it is to be up to a high standard of efficiency to maintain a profit at all. In the iron trade we have fortunately certain specialities on which we have been fairly busy, but in our general trade, particularly the export trade, we have been not only slack,but working at:lower prices in consequence of foreign competition. We have had to dismantle one branch of our works where we have waited for a long time for a revival in trade, and use the ground for other purposes. Fortunately, we can, in this instance, use the ground advantageously in the extension of other works, but it is unpleasant to see an old trade not only des- troyed, but to have to bear the cost of removal, and the scrap-heaping of what was formerly valuable and expensive machinery. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>AS REGARDS THE FUTURE we stand, as I have explained, on a very sound financial basis, but not on very hopeful prospects of trade. We have done so much in the last few years in increasing the efficiency and productive power of our collieries, that now poor times have arrived, we may hold our hands except where advisable, and still keep our initial cost of coal at a fairly low price, but we want a better demand, especially for slack, and we shall not have that until the cotton mills re-obtain their full supplies of cotton, and until the iron trade improves. In that trade we did expect that good trade in the Argentine would help us, but Germany gets it all. Germany, too, takes a large portion of the iron trade in India, 'where we formerly made large sales, and we shall have diffi- culty, owing to German competition, in retaining our export trade in other markets, where we have been prominent hitherto. Undoubtedly</p>
        <p>THE PREFERENTIAL -TARIFF given by Canada, South Africa and New Zealand helps ius a little and n an increasing way. Why cannot India follow the example so well set by those Colonies, and give a helping hand to British trade? Last year I made certain remarks about giving you a better divi- dend with higher wages to the men if we could get this tariff reform carved, and whilst there was a good deal of criticism about it, I am pleased to say that that view has been adopted by far the larger majority of iron manufacturers in this country. We have not got it yet, but undoubtedly the agitation for tariff reforms has done some good, and in the preferential tariffs which Canada, South Africa and New Zealand have already given us we see some. benefits already to our trade. One thing we cannot understand is why those. three Colonies having given us a benefit, it should not be eiven us aleo by India. It would certainly be a great boon to the iron trade generally if India would give it.and would exhibit that same community of interest between herself and Great Britainlwhich exists, or ought to exist, quite as strongly there'? as in Canada, South Africa and New Zealand. In this company we are harassed on both sides for our export coal pays duty, and I suppose even if the American imports which are threatened become a reality, that the principle of Free Trade will prevent our receiving any assistance either by the release of the home export duty or the imposition of an equal foreign duty on coal impofts, and that we shall be penalized on all sides. I appeal to shareholders to use their influence to get this export duty removed. The Chairman concluded by moving : "That the report and accounts now sub- mitted be approved and adopted."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>"THE UNFAIRNESS OF FOREIGN TARIFF."</head>
        <p>"THE UNFAIRNESS OF FOREIGN TARIFF."</p>
        <p>Mr. W. H. BLECKLY, in seconding, said: It has been my privilege for many years to follow our worthy chairman at these yearly meetings, and to second a similar resolution to that which he has just proposed. I cannot help saying a few words to-day at the risk of being a little tedious, as supplementing his able remarks which we have just listened to. I wish to call the attention of my fellow-shareholders to the fact that this is a day which some of us have been looking forward to ever since the formation of the company in 1874. At</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S 1/- TINS</p>
        <p>ASSORTED BISCUITS.</p>
        <p>CONTAINING 12 KINDS:</p>
        <p>OVAL RICH TEA POLO KIEL FINGERS ALEXANDRA HANO'VER RAGLAN THIN ARROWROOT CREAM CRACKERS MEXICAN MILK OATWI' WAFERS CHEESE WAFERS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>BRILLIANT FISCAL POLICY</head>
        <p>BRILLIANT FISCAL POLICY</p>
        <p>which has been usnered in since our last yearly meeting , by one of the greatest and wisest statesmen of the day, %hose commercial acumen and experience is of the highest order, I am glad to say your Board is absolutely unanimous. You will recollect that in season and out of season I have consistently advocated at our meetings some enlightened measure calcalated to counteract the present gross unfairness of foreign tariff-nurtured trade competition, which has had, and continues to have, such a disastrous effect upon our business. This ques- tion, I should like to impress upon our working men with whom I have been in personal contact since I was a boy, is of as muchiimportance to them as it is to their employers. Every ton of iron and steel, which in the natural course of things should be made here but by reason of protective tariffs abroad goes to foreign manufacturers, means less work for our men and less profit and often an absolute loss to our shareholders.</p>
        <p>'Fortunately, the question has been taken up by a master hand, and we may now safely leave the interests of our trade and the interests of our workmen in his care, and when he has won the day, as he most certainly will do sooner or later, we may confidently look forward to more regular employment, and consequently better wages for our workmen, and more adequate dividends to our shareholders. (Hear, hear.) The motion was then put to the meeting, and carried unanimously. FALLING MARKIITS, SLACKNESS OF TRADE.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN moved: "That the usual dividend at the rate of 6 per cent, per annum on the preference shares for the half-year ending 30th-June last, and a dividend at the rate of 4 par cent, per annum, free of income tag, on the ordinary shares, be paid on and after this date."</p>
        <p>Mr. J. J. BLECKLY, in seconding, said: It is quite true that we might pay a much handsomer dividend if we looked merely to the accounts before us, which would warrant the payment of a better dividend, but I think that all who are acquainted with the position of the iron and coal trades will commend the prudence of the Board in retaining in their own hands a larr pro- portion of the earnings. We cannot afford in this trade in these times to be handicapped for want of fonds We have large calls upon us for new machinery and new developments of all kinds and there is one thing we always look upon as the snare of the deceiver, we are not going to issue new capital or borrow from the public on de- bentures for those extensions, or for any alterations of our systems. The policy I have named has guided us in times past, and so long as the present members of the Board have the honour of occupy- ing their position—a somewhat onerous position—we don't intend to have any debentures. We were once very nearly driven to them, but a little resolution and streneth of mind enabled us to take out of our shareholders' pockets what we required instead of going to the public. In regard to the operations of the year there is very little to add to what appears in the report. Falling markets and slackness of trade sum up the whole position. We are still assailed abroad by foreign competition, and at home—as the Chairman has said—we, unfortunately, have been very much affected by the trouble in the cotton trade, upon the prosperity of which both our coal and onr iron very largely depend. As regards the future I am as little inclined to prophesy as the Chairman. If the improved outlook in the cotton trade is an indication as to the immediate future of the general home trade, we shall get our share; if it does not we shall be disappointed. In regard to the foreign trade, unless and until INir. Chamberlain's prin- ciples are exploited into acts in India and other places, we shall not be able to contend with this foreign com- petition as successfully as we used to. %rear, hear). That brings me to another question; and it, perhaps, may startle some of you if I expound another view and give you another explanation of the•difficulties we are surrounded with in our trade, particularly in the iron trade ; and that is the very heavy and</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>ABNORMAL COST OF PRODUCTION</head>
        <p>ABNORMAL COST OF PRODUCTION</p>
        <p>with which we are saddled at this day. As a matter of illustration I will take the bar trade, which has been our staple trade ever since we commenced work. I will take as the low water mark of the cost of production and the selling price the years 1887-8. I do it fora set purpose, and not for the purpose of garbling figures. In 1887-8 our average cost of pig iron for making bars was £1 16s. 7d. per ton ; last year for the same purpose it was £2 us. 2d., or an advance of 40 per cent. Fuel in 1887-8 cost us on the average for our works 6s. 41d. a ton ; in 1904 it cost us 7s. 9d.—an advance of 25 per cent. in spite of the adoption of economical processes which had reduced the cost another 25 per cent.; other- wise the cost would have been 50 per cent. more than it was. In regard to wages the cost of a ton of bar iron from beginning to end in our works was 20s. 10N. in 1887.8; last year it was 295. 3id., or an increase of 40 per cent. To be absolutely fair I must say that in some respects short time has contributed to that ad- vance; but mainly it is accounted for by advances in rates of wages under our sliding scale and so forth. Our average selling price of a ton of bar iron in 1887-8 was £4 175.10 d. f.0.b., Liverpool ; for the two years it did not vary a penny. Last year it was £6 6s. 10d.—an advance of 30 per cent. These are very serious figures—(hear, hear)—and they point to the fact that for some reason or other we are at a disadvantage of 40 per cent., or something like that in our cost of is as against what we were in 1887-8. Why s that? We are persistently told that technical education, modern metlwds, and so on are the things we are to look to for salvatTon, but here we go back to what might be called the dark ages, when we had not practically any technical education, when our methods were admittedly antiquated, and yet in those times we prodnced 40 per cent. cheaper than we are doing to-day. That clearly is not the explanation. If at this moment we could sell at the prices we sold at in 1887, and we did better then than we do now, we could sweep the boards and defy foreign competition. What is the explanation then? I think we need not go very far to account for it. If we look to the minimum rate and</p>
        <p>THE LIVING WAGE PRINCIPLE that has been so persistently advocated by political demagogues, if we look to that which affects everything from the bottom to the top of our trade, that is mainly responsible for it. And what is the effect upon our men? They go home to-day paid no doubt 40 per cent, or 30 per cent. higher rates, but with less money in their pockets than they did atithe lower rate. Certainly they have done two days' work less in thel week, and have thus that much more time loafing about, damaging them morally, physically and intellectually and in other ways, and this iq the natural outcome. In addition to this, legislative enactments, increases of taxation, municipal extravagance, all have added their quota to the cost, and the result is that we are in that sort of position that we cannot compete, and the men who are selling against us, and whom we are complaining of are selling at the old prices we ought to have. That is not the popular explanation of this difficulty. It only affects remotely and indirectly the question of tariff reform, but it is a question of life and death for us,and the sooner we recognize it as a Matter that wants setting right the sooner we shall resume the position we used to hold. (Hear, hear.) We conquered the world in the iron and coal trades by the selling of cheap material and by strenuous work from Monday morning to Saturday night. Can we maintain it by selling at 25 per cent. higher or 40 per cent. higher than we did before, and by working four days a week ? • That is a problem that has to be solved. If we get protection and tariff reform and so on. a large proportion of the benefit will go to the working man, but the other ques- tion is the more important to my mind, and it is absolutely put away and forgotten on the platform and by the Press. (Hear, hear). The motion was carried.</p>
        <p>WORK IN THE ENGINEERING AND COLLIERY DEPARTMENT. _</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Mr. W. PETER RYLANDS, seconded by Mr. PEARSON, the officers of the company at the ironworks and collieries were thanked for their services during the past year. Mr. HERBERT BLECKLY, responding, said: On behalf of the iron department I beg to thank you, and I am proud to be able to respond for so excellent a staff. Our record in the engineering department this year is similar to last, only, in consequence of increased com- petition, it has been maintained with greater difficulty. We have, however, been fully employed making the most modern type of blast furnaces, calcining kilns, coke and ore storage bins, and erecting colliery plant, 1 including a large installation in connection with Semet Solvey coke ovens: also supplying iron and steel works appliances, girders, bridges, and roofs of every descrip- tion. A new feature in our business, from which we expect an accession of orders, is the establishment of a</p>
        <p>good connection in South Africa for mining plant. Our manufacture of railway wheels and axles has again increased, and we are busy. The 2,000 tons wheel boss- ing press, referred to last year, is being made, and the rest of the plant in progress so, if all goes well, we expect to be able to meet the railway companies' new regulations when they are in force. We have also just entered upon another new undertaking, from which we confidently anticipate the best results,in the application of electricity to driving the engineering department, where it is especially suitable, enabling us to dispense with old arrangements, and suhstitute a system of the most modern and advantageous character. I should like to add that the whole plant and machinery of the ironworks has been efficiently and economically main- tained.</p>
        <p>RE-ELECTION OF A DIRECTOR AND AUDITORS.</p>
        <p>On the motion of the CHA.IRMA.N, seconded by Air. BECK. Mr. G. F. Pearson was re-elected a director of the company. Messrs. Blease and Sons were, on the motion of Mr J. PAUL RYLANDS, seconded by Mr. RICHARDSON, re- appointed auditors for the ensuing year at a salary of £2BO.</p>
        <p>The Chairman was thanked for prasiding, on the pro- position of Mr. W. H. BLECKLY, seconded by Mr. J. J. BLECKLY, and supported by Mr. BRANCKER. The CHAIRMAN, replying, said: It may interest you to know that when we started business in 1875 our out- put of coal was 309,000 tons. At present it is over three times that quantity. Our output of iron and sttel has more than doubled- Therefore, there is a good de more to do now, and it requires the hard work of our managers to look after this, and bring us up ta a high state of efficiency. One thing you may rely upon is that, whilst I am largely interested myself and appreciate the dividends quite as much as anybody else, I shall not lose sight of the fact that we ought to keep plenty of money in hand to meet the calls of either our coal or iron managers for any appliances which may lead to the efficiency of the two concerns. (Elear, hear.) It is better to be able to work at low prices, and to adopt those means of efficiency, rather than to pay high divi- dends for a few years, and after that be compelled to go to the bankers or debenture holders for money. (Hear, hear.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>SALL RIGHTS RESERVEDI.</head>
        <p>SALL RIGHTS RESERVEDI.</p>
        <p>SOCIAL SERMONETTES FOR WORKING MEN.</p>
        <p>BY JOHN TAYLOR. Author of "Search Lights on Llni, Labour and Social I.)robleins."</p>
        <p>THE LABOURER'S GOLDEN AGE</p>
        <p>"The fifteenth century, and the first quarter of the sixteenth century, were the golden age of the English labourer' if we are to interpret the wages which he earned by the cost of the necessaries of life. At no time were wages, relatively speaking, so high, and at no time was foodso cheap."—PROFESSOR THOROLD ROGERS.</p>
        <p>Professor Rogers, in his "Six Centuries of Work and Wages," gives us some striking object lessons on the earning power of labour, and the cost of living. He has ransacked rent rolls, and official records, and ancient documents from all parts of the kingdom, and he not only tells us the actual wages that were paid to all kinds of workmen, but he quotes the market price of bread, and ales and beef, ani all the necessaries of life.</p>
        <p>By studying these documents carefully he is able to tell how much money a workman could earn, and he can reckon up the cost of living, and he can tell how much a prudent labourer would be able to save out of his earniags. Looking at these questions all round, he comes to the conclusion that, so far as food and wages are concerned, the fifteenth century workman was well, off, and, in some revs:lts, h 3 was better off than the men of our own day. I.—WAGES WERE KEPT UP ALL THROUGH THE CENTURY.</p>
        <p>The population of England was smill, and the wars and insurrections, and plagues, and pestilences prevented it from growing, so the demtnd for laboar exceeded the supply. In the previous century wages had been as low as threepence per day for agrlcultural labourers, and fourpence for artisans and skilled workmen. In 1415 a number of masons were at work on York Cathedral, and their wages were eight guineas a year each. So the money value of their wages would work out at nearly sixpence for each working day. The Statute of Labourers, which was intended to keep wages down, and make labour cheap was a failure, because the law of supply and demand is stronger than kings and Parliaments.</p>
        <p>1450 many skilled workmen were employed at Oxford, and the records of that ancient city show that superior masons earned eightpence per day all the year round. The ordinary masons and builders earned six- pence per day for nine months, and fivepence per day for three winter months.</p>
        <p>The agricultural labourers earned fourpence per day, and during the harvest month they had .sixpence per day and sundry perquisites. The work was not very hard, and the hours were not long, and they were paid for seven days every week, though they did no work on Sundays. They had eighteen days' holiday in each year, and they got their full wages all the time. Christmas was their great time for merrymaking, and they had a spree that lasteda whole week. They had three days at Easterand three at Whitsuntide, and they had several more after harvest.</p>
        <p>Then they had the protection of their gilds, or trade unions, that saved them from oppression and persocu- tion. These guilds lent money without interest to enable members to carry on their business. They gave money to apprentice poor children, to some trade, or to relieve a brother in time of sickness, or to pension his widow when he died.</p>
        <p>Now it is clear from the facts and flgor33 1., have quoted that the fifteenth century workman never had much money in his pocket. The agricultural labourer had to work all the week to earn two and fourpence, and the skilled artisan could only earn three and six- pence a week. And yet in some respects, these men were better off than the workman of to-day who is earning thirty shillings a week. You must remember that money is not everything. You cannot eat it. You cannot drink it. You cannot wear it,and it would make a very uncomfortable pillow. Money is of no earthly use to you except as a medium of exchange, and you can only settle its value by find- ing out what people are willing to give you for it. lI.—FOOD WAS ABUNDANT AND CHEAP.</p>
        <p>So the labourer had a double advantage over his fore- fathers—he was getting more money, and the purchas- ing power of his money was enormously increased. I have shown you how the supply of labour was kept down by wars and pestilences, but the great plague did not carry off all our sheep and cattle. Our flocks and herdf multiplied faster than our people, and the supply of food was greater than the demand. Thefifteenth century workman bad hardly anything to pay for rent, or rates, or taxes. His house was small, but it was large enough for his wants, and he had the use of the common land, where he could gather his fire- wood, or feed his cow, or his poultry, orhis pigs, without paying rent to anybody. He was never out of work. He could always earn full wages, and food was so cheap and abundant that heconl. earn three or four times as much as it cost to keep himr Beef was sold in those days at three pounds fof a penny, and it was as wholesome and tender as the bee you are giving tenpence a pound for to-day. The artisan of the fifteenth century who earned sixpence per day could buy eighteen pounds of beef with one day's labour. Our modern workman would have to give three times as much labour to produce the same results. Butter was sold at a halfpenny a pound, so one day's labour would earn a dozen pounds of butter. The workman of our On day would have to work half a week to buy as much in our markets.</p>
        <p>Cheese NS: as three pounds for a penny. Eggs were twenty for a penny. Fowls were three 'halfpence each. The man who earned sixpence per day could take home two fowls, and twenty eggs, and six pounds of cheese as the fruits of his day's labour. Where is the man who could stock his larder at that rate by working twice as long now? You could buy a goose or a pig for fourpence, and pigeons were sold at three for a penny. Ale cost only a halfpenny a gallon. What fine old times the thirsty topers had in those days. One day's work would earn twelve gallons of home brewed ale. Everybody drank ale in the fifteenth century, for they had no tea, or coffee, or cocoa, or teetotallers. But the ale they drankfts tested and examined by the "ale-taster," and if it was not in prime condition the end was knocked out of the barrel, and the beer was poured into the road, and the unlucky brewer was put into the stocks or the ducking stool as a warning to others. It is a thousand pities we cannot treat some of our modern brewers after this fashion. The Assize of Bread and Ale was a great protection to the medixeal workmen. It guaranteed the quality and the price of his food and drink, and it visited with severe penalties the rogues who tried to rob him. Now if good honest beer were sold at a half-penny a gallon, the artisan who earned sixpence per day !could buy twelve gallons of it by one day's work. It would take nearly a week's work to buy as much tied-house slops and arsenic in these days. Bread was so cheap and abundant that employers of labour frequently gave it away to their workpeople, without reckoning it as part of their wages. In 1450 wheat and oatmeal sold at five shillings per quarter, and malt was three shillings and elevenpence per quarter. Now I have shown you that food was remarkably cheap all through the fifteenth century, and therefore the cost of living was very low. At 891 borne Priory, in Hampshire, they gave board and lodgings to men for twopence per day, and to iwomen for three halfpence ProTessor Rogers tells us that in 1467 two girls were hired to work at fourpence each per day, if they found their own food and lodgings. If they lived with their employer he would charge them twopence each per day for the cost of their keep. The cost of clothing was much higher than that of food. A yard of course linen shirting was sixpence</p>
        <p>sad that meant a whole day's work to the artisan, and a day and a half to the agricultural labourer. A yard of woollen cloth cost eighteenpence which was three days work for one man and more that :our for another. You need not wonder that men wore leather breeches and coats so long as cattle were so cheap and textile fabrics were so dear. lII.—WHAT ARE THE LESSONS OF 3IY STORY? Well, in the first place it teachers us that low wages do not necessarily &amp;pen poverty. In the hundred and twenty five years beginning with Henry IV. and ending , with Henry VIII. no working man ever earned five shillings a week. It was a time of unrest,that included the Wars of the Roses, and several foreign campaigns; and yet hardly anybody died of hunger, and pinching poverty was unknown. The artisan who earned sixpence per day could buy as much of some kinds of food with his sixpence as you could buy with six shillings now. He could live on twopence and save the other fourpence Whore is the working man to-day who can earn three times the cost of his keep and save tivothirds of his wages? In the second place, it shows us that a nation of food producers has a better chance of enjoying life than a nation that manufactures what it cannot eat. In the fifteenth century seventyfive per cent, of the workers lived by their labour on the land. They were plough- ing, and planting, and sowing, and rearing sheep and cattle and poultry. If pad times came, and they could not sell their produce, they could live on it, and they could manage as well without money as with it, so long as they had their flocks, and herds and land. We are producing calico, and woollen cloth 04 boots, and shoes, and iron, and hardware. When traae is slack, and we cannot sell these goods, we must hoard them, or part with them at slaughter prices, because we cannot eat them.</p>
        <p>In the third place, it is just as necessary to keep our expenses down as it is to keep our wages up. What is the use of agitating and clamouring for more wages, so long as we vote for bigger armaments,and more ships of war, and reckless extravagance in our national and municipal affairs? Whatever sends up the cost of living does as much harm to the working man as a correspond- ing reduction in his wages: Unfortunately, the average workman does not see it, so he gets plucked and plun- dered every day. The men of the fifteenth century were wiser. They had not much coming in, but they took care of the bit they had got, and if they thought the King and his tax-gatherers were trying to take too much out of them they could get up a rebellion or a civil war on the shortest notice, any time. How they learned to look after themselves so well I must tell /on some other day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>WAGES IN THE IRON TRADE. IMPORTANT MEETING IN BIRMINGHAM. Scheme of-Reorganizatioa. SPECIAL REPORT.</head>
        <p>WAGES IN THE IRON TRADE. IMPORTANT MEETING IN BIRMINGHAM. Scheme of-Reorganizatioa. SPECIAL REPORT.</p>
        <p>Hatters of considerable interest to the Lancashire iron-making district were dealt with on Thursday by the Standing Committee of the Midland Iron and Steel Wages Board which met at the Grand Hotel Birmingham under the presidency of Sir Benjamin Hingley. The bi-monthly ascertainment of prices by the accountants for July and August showed an average selling price for the eleven selected firms of £6 4s. 1153 d. compared with £6 7s. 2•29 d. two months ago. There was a total fall in the output from these firms fr0m26,863 tons to 25,292 tons,spread with tolerable evenness over bars, angles and tees, plates and sheets. The only exception was hoops, which have increased from 4654 tons to 4731 tons. The most serious falling prices:affect sheets—from £7 2s 0•16 d. to £6 Us. 1147 d, and hoop strip and miscellan- eons from £6 4s. 1-71 d. to £6 Os. 6.28 d. In accord ance with the resolution of July 31st 1902 which suspended the operation of the sliding scale. wages are continued at Bs., 6d. per ton. The most noticeable circumstances about this return is that while the Northern selling price has increased by 10•26 d. the Midland price as fallen 2s. 2•76 d. This increases the disparity between the two districts, which has caused so much trouble. The scale if strictly followed in the Midlands would give only 7s. 91—The employers therefore add, as a kind of bonus, a further 9d. This wage will continue until Saturday, December 3rd.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>SLIDING SCALE.</head>
        <p>THE SLIDING SCALE.</p>
        <p>There was a long-Cii-s-ci;siOn-Teiai;e to the men's notice to terminate the sliding scale. At first some heat was developed, the employers being under the impression that the men had set themselves to destroy the Board, an inference most earnestly deprecated by the operatives, who spoke appreciatively of its work in the past. Strong complaint was, however, made of the way in which its authority was disregarded by many works and a strong appeal was made to strengthen the Board. At present, as Mr. Jones, for the employers, informed our representative, only 26 out of about 60 workers are pledged members; others, particularly in Warrington, Shropshire, and the out- lying districts generally, either follow the wages settlements without in any way acknowledging allegi- ance, or make an atmual contribution of £lO or some such amount t•-) the Board. This arrangement was strongly complained of by the operatives, who stated that the employers are to an increasing extent making private arrangements with their men. Eventually, a resolution was carried declaring that " The notice given by the operatives to discontinue the sliding scale will not interfere with the general work of the Board, and the present arrangements for fixing the rate of wages will continue until the first week in February. In the meantime an inquiry will be made by the Standing Committee, who will after- wards hold a special meeting to receive the replies." A letter signed by the employerz and operative mem- bers of the Standing Committee will be sent to the whole of the employers engaged in the iron and steel trade of South Staffordshire Lancashire, Shrop- shire and the Midlands asking (1' Are you in favour of the continuance of the Midland Iron and Steel Wages Board? (2) if favourable will you agree to become subscribing members conforming to and observing its rules and regulations? (3)the operative s2ction to make similar enquiries with respect to ironworkers. Subsequently, it is understood, in the event of the enquiry showing a spirit of loyalty to the Board, a Special Committee will be formed to outline a scheme for the new sliding-scale which it is agreed must replace the old one. The " Iron- workers' Journal " for October contains a lengthy editorial from the pen of Mr. James Co; the general secretary of the Association on the crisis. He says "The attempt of Sir Benjamin Hingley (Chairman of the Midland Wages Board) to steal a march upon the Midland paddlers by his endeavour to take sixpence per ton off them on evidence which he refused to produce and which was entirely refuted by the expenditure of a penny postage stamp has landed the Midland district into an industrial discussion little anticipated by the employer section a the Board. Whatever our personal desires may be the 'whole question of Midland extras has to be faced and the sliding scale absolutely abolished. After mentioning the fact that only three works were opposed to giving notice to terminate the sliding scale the writer says 'There is very little doubt that even these would vote differently had they to take the vote over again, but there is the fact that without any pressure or prompting from the Society or Board officials we have an almost unanimous vote to terminate the Midland sliding scale.' The question has been asked through the columns of the Press : What is the object of the workmen in giving this notice? Let me say at once that this vote of the workmen does not reflect upon the principle of sliding scale arrangements or the excellent influence of the Midland Wages Board. It may be just possible that the Midland Wages Board will soon end in decent burial, but that will only be because of the indifference of the employers, and their repeated violation of the principles and rules and not from any want of faith on the part of the workmen them- selves. I am now going to make what may appear an exaggerated statement of fact but I make it as a present to those capitalistic papers who think their employers are all honour and the workmen the reverse. Taking the history of the last twenty years with which I have been closely connected with the Association and Midland Wages Board, there has never been a single case where workmen have rebelled against the Board's decision, while on the other hand I can produce many instances where employers have left the Board and refused to subscribe to it because of decisions which their own fellow employers have given against them." . One word as to the object of the workmen in ter- minating the sliding scale. Their sole object is to obtain a reality foga sham. In all sliding scale armng- ments there must be an ascertainment of prices such as , really represent the actual fluctuations in the trade. Under the present scale this necessity is not secured. The difference in policy between the employers and the workmen is this :—They (the employers) are constantly endeavouring by a re-shutfling of the firms from which the prices are taken to make the selling price fit an imaginary premium of is. 6d. whilst we are contending for a real solid representative basis of the selling price and then adjust the necessary premium which will place us on an equal footing with other districts." "Surely we may claim that our object is a laudable one and cannot be gainsaid. Of one thing lam certain. No scale will be acceptable which does not embrace a larger representation of the trade, and which does not confine itself to the Midland districts only. The ques. tion of expense may be raised in securing a wider audit. This is a matter entirely for the employers to decide. We as workmen are prepared to pay all the expenses of a satisfactory audit and the only difficulty rests with the large number of employers who secure all the bene- fits of a sliding scale arrangement but refuse to subscribe their necessary portion of the expenditure."</p>
        <p>PROVISIONS dini DEARER,—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
