      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <p>McDOUGALL'S Paterit SELF—RAISING FLOUR ONMETAED</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>, WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>, WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>Her Majesty's Favourite Flower.— Queen Alexandra's love for the violet is well known, but few people are acquainted with the fact that over two hundred feet of glass houses are entirely devoted to the cultivation of these, her Majesty's favourite flowers, at Sandringham. The Queen who possesses an extensive knowledge of the flora of her native and adopted countries, takes the keenest interest in the gardens of her Norfolk home,and no changvs are ever made without first consulting her Majesty. Old-World Gardens.—There has been a noticeable tendency of late to reinstate the old-fashioned garden and buxom dahlias, sturdy marigolds, sunflowers, holly- hocks, variegated phlox, and other old-fashioned flowers are once more to be found in the gardens of all our great country seats. Miss Alice Rothschild has always been a lover of old-fashioned flowers, and pos- sesses a wonderful collection of them at her villa at Grasse in the Riviera, where she employs upwards of seventy gardeners. Lady Jersey is another of our great ladies famous for her love of old-fashioned gardens. Those at Osterley are exceedingly beautiful, and are noted for their rec. rambler roses, and masses of rhododendrons. The AdopLion of a Colour.—Nowadays it is the fashion for the smart Society woman to select the one colour that best suits her, and to wear it on every possible occasion. This fashion of selecting one colour and remaining faithful to it, was set by Queen Alexandra, heliotrope being her Majesty's chosen hue. Princess Henry of Hess, long ago selected pale blue, and when not garbed in this colour, she weft, white, with masses of turquoises. Miss Muriel Wilson has adopted yellow, and Lady Ulrica Baring has chosen red. Regarding ornaments, almost every Society woman has her favourite gem. The Duchess of Marlborough for example is seldom seen without some of her priceless pearls or Lady Archibald Campbell without some of her white pectens, set in silver. These latter, which are very rare and curious, were brought back by the Duke of Argyll years ago, from a cruise in West Indian waters. High necked instead of Discollete gowns.—The time when a high necked gown for evening wear was regarded as hopelessly dowdy, has happily gone by. Formerly the woman who appeared in the stalls at a theatre in a frock that was not strictly decollete was as much the subject of remark as the man in morning dress, but of late years both men and women have claimed a greater latitude as regards evening attire. The fashionable dressmakers resisted the change as long as they could, but since the high necked evening gown now figures in every Society woman's wardrobe, they have had to recognise this change of fashion. It is certainly one making for the general convenience,and our dressmakers are now just as skilful in turning out smart high-necked frocks as are the modistes of Paris and Vienna. Dressing for Evening Festivities.—The woman who would look her best at an evening function, should put everything down to the minutest detail, ready for the toilet, some hours before,and the actual dressing should be accomplished at full spe9d.Dawdling is almistake,and no new modes in hairdressing should ever be attempted on such an occasion. The gown too, unless of the most airy fairy persuasion, should always be worn beforehand if only for a hour, so as to become accustomed to it. In cold weather the dressing room should be warmed, and the chill taken off every garment. Nothing is so fatal to good looks as cold feet and hunger. A light, but nourishing tea should always be partaken of when an evening festivity is in view. Where Hairpins come from.—There are said to be something like 60,000,000 of pins used in this country every day, and though most people have read accounts of their manufacture, none can say where they all go to. So likewise with hairpins. Where do they all go to? But that they want constantly replenishing is good for the little village of Painswick in Gloucestershire, where we are told is to be found the greatest manufac- tory of hairpins in the world. The work of transform- ing miles of wire into finished pins is of course all done by automatic machines, but the industry employs fully 300 hands, most of whom are women and girls. The indispensable wrist Bag.—Reticules or small wrist bags, are still as necessary as ever, and it seems doubt- ful if we shall ever emerge from the present pocketless state of things. It is true that the winter coats are mostly provided with pockets, but as a rule they are more make-believe than useful and do not do away with the necessary handbag. These therefore still remain an appropriate and useftl little present for any woman, as, though she may already possess several, they will not be too many, it being desirable if possible that the bag should harmonise in colour with the dress worn. Sour Milk and Brown Bread.—Scientists of recognised authority have recently favoured us with some useful hints concerning the merits of brown bread, and "skim" or sour milk. No one would have thought that sour milk was good for the health, but not only is this the case but it is said also to free the human body of active agents which bring about its decay, and thus it conduces to longevity. The lactic acid which it contains, is des- tructive to the bacillus which exist in millions in the human system, and it is pointed out that the peasantry in Ireland, Denmark, and other countries, who consume large quantities of "skim milk," are all remarkably long-lived people. As regards brown bread, the opinion given by Professor Attwater at the British Association, was rather against the general idea that it is more nutritious than white bread. It would be so if digested, but, as the human system rejects the properties which would make it superior, Professor Attwater seemed to think that people thrive better upon white bread than upon brown, owing to its more digestible quality. This would seem to be the 'common experience, as if people obtained the greater satisfaction from brown bread, they would eat it, yet it is seldom seen even on the tables of the labouring or agricultural classes. India and our Colonies.—Few of us realise bow much of our beautiful clothing and rich ornaments, are fur- nished by India and our colonies. To take the latter lirst,—we get from Australia glorious opals, and pearls beyond price; from India and South Africa diomonds ; from Burma, pigeon's blood rubies and other stones as fine; from Ceylon, sapphires, cat's eyes, moonstones; and from India the golden and rose pink topaz,amethysts and fine cornelians. Then in the matter of dress, our furs—such as sable, ermine, seal ow., are trapped in far away Canada, or in the still more Arctic North Western Territories once known as the Hudson Bay Territory; and the West Coast of'Africa supplies the casut chouc from the indiarubber tree, an indispensable item when waterproofs are concerned. Apart from Ireland which gives us our loveliest laces; Malta helps us with the delicate thread work it sends. From Ceylon also comes</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <p>some pretty lace, and Cyprus provides the fashionable washinelace which has ornamented our summer linen gowns. We have ostrich feathers from South Africa., shawls from Cashmere;silks from Hong Kong, embroid- eries from India, wools from Australia, and indeed the listslwould bea long one if we were to attempt to enumerate half we get in the way of dress from India and our Colonies..</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>BETTING RAID AT CREWE. Landlord away at Warrington.</head>
        <p>BETTING RAID AT CREWE. Landlord away at Warrington.</p>
        <p>After a sitting lasting all day on Tuesday, the charge against Frank Vickers, licencee of the' Engine Hotel, Crewe, for keeping his house open for betting purposes was resumed on Wednesday morning, when the defence was proceeded with. It was alleged that the licencee was out of town on the two occasions the police visito the house. The Mayor: Then one side is committing perjury, and we shall sift the matter to the bottom. The licencee gave evidence, and said that he was having his holidays during the time the officer in plain clothes visited his house. He was at Warrington on the first occasion and at Alderley on the second. He was perfectly certain as to the dates, as he had special per- mits granted to fish in private waters. During the hearing of the case a telegram was despatched to the owner of the fishing waters at Al derley, i and he replied that defendant fished n his waters, but he had forgotten the dates. Mr. J. B. Wood, of Warrington, stated that he was with Vickers on the two dates on which the police alleged they visited the Engine Hotel, and produced a private memorandum book showing records of their catches. The magistrates scrutinised the pocket-book and ordered it not to be given back to the witness. Dr. Hodgson, one of the magistrates, said the case had assumed very serious and extraordinary proportions Ot4ler witnesses were called to testify that 'Vickers discbluraged betting. He was a popular athlete, and that accounted for the large number of sporting papers found when the police raided the premises. The defendant was fined .£5O and costs, and ordered to pay costs on two other summonses. The magistrates expressed the opinion that pr oceedings for perjury ought to be instituted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>MAKING OF A BUSINESS MAN.</head>
        <p>THE MAKING OF A BUSINESS MAN.</p>
        <p>"It can be stated with confidence," says an American physician of high reputation, "that nobody ever has perfect health unless he has perfect digestion. In fact, the degree of his health may be measured by the state of his digestion, just as the mercury in a thermometer shows the amount of heat in the air. It is also true that .indigestion, often accompanied by constipation, is the starting point of many organic diseases—freqaently, but not always, of consumption itself." So much for a clear-headed doctor of remarkable ability. Now we will listen to a retired man of business —Mr. James Howgate, of 39, Town-street, Chapel Allerton, Leeds. "I can never be too grateful," says Mr. Howgate in his letter, dated February Ist, 1904, "for the good Mother Seigel's Syrup has done me, and feel that I must describe it, for the benefit of others. It is a long time now since I first began to take it. I had been nine months under the doctor, yet was so weak that I could hardly raise my arm, when Mother Seigel's Syrup restored me to health in a few weeks. It happened this way. Mv illness began with loss of appetite, brought on, I believe, by overwork. Everything late caused me pain. I was quite unable to digest,and becameiso weak from want of nourishment that I was incapable of working. Sickness now set in, and every morsel I swallowed was rejected; even beef tea would not remain with me. To add to my misery, there was a constant feeling of suffocation,and at times I could hardly get myibreath.At hat I had to take to my bed, and for nine months I lay there scarcely able to move, and without prospect of recovery. A doctor attended me daily, but was unable to do me any good. "It. was while I lay in this dreadful condition that I was advised to try Mother Seigel's Curative Syrup, and I bless the day that I accepted that advice. After taking a few doses, the vomiting ceased and I began to mend? The load on my chest seemed to be lifted, and once'more food nourished me. By the time I had used three bottles of the Syrup I was on my feet again rapidly gaining strength, And in a few weeks I was as well as ever I had been in my life." There is no more subtle foe to vitality than indiges- tion. It saps one's energy as nothing else will, and leaves its victim weak in body and mind, utterly unfit for the ordinary duties of life. In restoring Mr. How gate to health (as it has done in countless similar cases), it may be claimed for Mother Seiael's Syrup that it has beer the making of a man of business.</p>
        <p>THE ASSAULT ON A WIDNES PUBLICAV.—HEAVY FINE. At Widnes, on Monday, Joseph Bright, a young man of no fixed abode, was charged with assaulting W. H. Thornton, liceneee of the Queen's Arms, and also with refusing to quit the said premises on April 26th last.— Mr. Picton, of the firm pf Messrs. Riley, Picton, and Kelly, of St. Helens and Liverpool, represented the licencee.—Prosecutor stated that on the morning of the day in question two other men were followed by prisoner into the Queen's Arms. The men ordered two pints of beer, but prisoner was refused, owing to his condition. He was asked to leave, whereupon he struck the landlord violently in the chest and behind the ear, knocking him into the road,followinethis up by wanting to fight. Police C:mstable Moores was sent for,and pri- soner decamped.—The Mayor, after a strong reprimand, Imposed fines of 40s. and costs in each case, or two months in default.</p>
        <p>I FAILURES IN ENGLAND AND WALES.—According to 1" Kemp's Mercantile Gazette," the total number of I failures recorded in England and Wales during the week ending Friday, Sep.23rd, was 161, viz.:—New Bankruptcy Proceedings published in the London Gazette 83; Deeds of Arrangement registered, 78. The respective numbers in the corresponding week of last year were: 'Bankruptcies, 69 ; • Deeds of Arrangement, 70—Total 1139; being a net increase in Bankruptcies of 248, and a net increase in Deeds of Arrangement in 1904, to date of 466.</p>
      </div>
