      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, October 1, 1904.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 1, 1904.</p>
        <p>(Copyright.)</p>
        <p>Tack Gordon could not resist her childish charms.</p>
        <p>"Mademoiselle Rossini," he stammered out, in a low voice that sounded strangely unlike his own.</p>
        <p>She stopped and looked at him, with the star- light shining down on her small white face; but she did not speak. He stood transfaud by her loveliness, and could not utter a word. Captain Gordon, who had never lost his presence of mind even before a duchess, was now tongue-tied and cswkward before a slip of a girl, whose sad, wist- ful eves were lifted to his own.</p>
        <p>"Oh, forgive me!" he muttered at last; "for- give me—but—but—" In a moment Millie saw that he was the man who bad dropped the rosebud at her feet—the handsomest man she had ever seen. _ _</p>
        <p>"But what ? " she asked in a shy, half-frightened voice, a rosy tint stealing over her face, and the long lashes of her downcast eyes casting a shadow over her cheeks.</p>
        <p>, please forgive me; I must speak to you, continued Jack. She did not answer him. It was a strange wooing; a handsome man; a lovely girl; with myriads of twinkling winter stars shining down upon them as they stood all alone in that silent night. Jack could not tell why this slim girl had so fascinated him; no such feelings had ever stirred him before. Her small band was gently laid in his, her lovely face was very near his own. "Millie," he whispered, 'I love you." The tiny hand trembled in his; he could feel her heart throbbing in union with his own. This gave him courage. Millie did not shrink away from him now; she was no high-born lady, and this rapid wooing did not offend her. Her whole nature seemed to leap up in response to his; her lips had lost tho cultivated smile, her eyes the sad wistful look, as he gently raised her face. "Is it strange that a man should love a face like this? " he said. She was so guileless, so innocent herself, she believed in the man whose eyes had met hers—so softly, so tenderly. Her heart beat wildly as she met Jack's passionate look. It was grnwing late; the narrow street was deserted, the two were alone—all alone. Jack Gordon threw his arm round her. His blood was on fire, his bead whirled round.</p>
        <p>"Do you think you could love me, Millie ? " The child trusted him implicitly; her lovely face grew like a blush-rose, her eyes shone brightly as the stars above her.</p>
        <p>"Yes, I do love you," she answered very softly. And Jack, gathering her closer to him, passionitely kissed her rosy lips. The Exeterites seemed suddenly to have forgotten all their scruples about theatres, circuses, and such-like frivolous and wicked amusements. Night after night they went, and the departure of the circus was daily postponed. e Night after night Captain Gordon could ba seen among the throng. Punctually at the hour of nine, five minutes before Millie came on, he was always in his seat; and later, when the old cathedral town grew silent and still, a pair walked up and down the quiet street, lost in the oblivion of love, forgetful of sleep, forgetful of everything save themselves. It was only the old, old story, told so often, gaining sweetness each time it was told. Millie Rossini was a waif and stray, cast forth into the world by an old grandfather who had gone to the workhouse. Being no longer able to keep her, he had answered an advertisement in a paper for a young girl to ride in a circus—a comfortable home offered—and she was sent off to join the great troupe when littl more than a child; cast on the rough waters of ITfe, with nothing to guard her from evil but the shield of her own innocence, and the loving kindness of her heavenly Father.</p>
        <p>She had no recollections of home; out of the dim caverns of memory she could just dimly remember something of a decrepid old man who was very kind to her, but this was all. Jack Gordon was the first man she had ever loved, and she loved him with such an overwhelming love that it swallowed up every other feeling. They met again and again. Love cannot be measured by time; it springs up like a mushroom in the night—like the wind, none know whence it cometh or whither it goeth. Love to Millie was a thing pure and sacred. To Jack it was quite different; a man's love always is. Of course, he loved Millie passionately; he felt he could not live without her. Even to look at her, to watch her sea-shell colour vary, to note each play of her facile mouth, to meet the full glow of her eyes— it all thrilled him with a pleasure he had never known before.</p>
        <p>She was homeless, friendless; surely it could be no wrong to take her from her wretched life, from all the hardships she had to endure, and to strew her path with roses, to give her every luxury she could wish for, to lavish on her the whole love and fidelity of his soul. Marriage, after all, was but a form, a ceremony; what did it matter if they both really loved? "We have known each other some time now, darling "—it was only a fortnight. "Do you really love me, Milly ? "</p>
        <p>"0 Jack, you know I do!" she said, turning and looking at him for a. moment fixedly. "They have been very happy—these hours together—haven't they, my child ? " She did not speak, but bending down kissed his hand, which .wr!s clasping hers.</p>
        <p>, "And what shall we do when this pleasant time is over, Millie? " "When this pleasant time is over ? " she asked with a trembling voice.</p>
        <p>"I mean wlisen the circus—when you—have to leave Exeter; when you have to leave me and— and say good-bye, darling." • "0 Jack, what do you mean—to say good-bye 2" The words broke from her in a hushed voice. The face he looked down on wore a deathly pallor, the blue eyes were pitiful to behold. "To say good- bye, Jack ? " she whispered again, half to herself, as though in a dream. She grasped his arm tightly to keep herself from falling; her breath came in little faint gasps, and she shook from head to foot.</p>
        <p>The child's ghastly face, the terrified look in her eyes sent every resolution to do what was right to the winds. He could not marry her, but—bit he could not leave her.</p>
        <p>"Do you really love me, Millie, darling 2" he aged.</p>
        <p>Her tiny bands crept into his, and she looked up into his face and tried to speak, but a great lump rose up in her throat, and she burst into a flood of tears. Jack took her tenderly in his strong, manly arms, and the rapid throbbing of tb- heart that beat against his was the only answer; but it was enough. "I love you, my child—so much that it would be worse than death to lose you." The sudden reaction from fear to hope made her shiver, but a bright blush swept across her face. "I cannot marry you, Millie." Jack spoke out honestly and bravely; he wou'A not deceive her. It eased his conscience, although he knew how be was wronging her. _ _</p>
        <p>Millie, young, innocent eild, knew nothing of marriage or of any social laws; such ceremonies were nothing to her. "We shall always—always be togtther, Jack; you will never leave me? " she asked, looking up into his face without a blush.</p>
        <p>A great throb of compunction shot through the man's heart; her thorough innocence . of evil pleaded so strongly for her that he almost resolved to give her up even at this eleventh hour; to tear himself away. from his enchantress; .to put hundreds of miles of British soil between them. He looked at her, and the exceeding beauty of her face conquered him again with a love that was ready to overleap all; he could not give her up. He turnecirhis head away from her; he could not bear to look into her pure, trusting eyes while he spoke. "Millie, will von come away with me to- morrow—away with me alone to some far country where no one will know us, where we need nevo part again 2, "Go away with you? 0 Jack! you know i would go to the end of the world with you." Jack tenderly laid his hand on her head and said: "Y your own accord. Our me, darling, c good-night, child. " f Jack Gordon walked home that evening with strangely tumultuous thou ' thoughts surginc, through his brain. -What was this terrible thing lie was going e rKliasss t m to do? The most cowardly action that a man could possibly conupit—to take advantage of a girl's ignorance of evil to lead her astray. She was only a waif, but with a nature as pure as gold; he knew that if she had understood what she was going to do—if she had known right froM wrong—she would have done what was right. But, poor guileless infant, she did not know. For a moment a wave of repentance stole over him ; his better nature rose up in his manly breast, and he conquered. His should not be the hand to drag her across the great wide sea that divides the pure from the imnure—a sea so wide that ones tha other side there no hope or returning. Jack Gordon pictured to himself how Millie would miss him, how her little heart would grieve; he fancied he could see her rosy cheek (crowing paler</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>One's thoughts at present are naturally directed toward.; tailormade costumes, for there is a sharpness in the early morning and late evening air which makes one appreciate the good qualities of this kind, of dress. Mid- day the sun at tithes shines as fiercely and strongly as in August, and a dainty blouse or light costumc is all that is requisite with, perchance, the addition of a feather fluffy boa, as the wind is not altogether free from an autumn chilliness, even when the sun is mcst powerful. Besides we all know that with the fall of the lqaf the summer is over, and it is well to b 6 provided with warmer garments. This is not a year when one can give vent to the grumble which has of late seasons been annual in regard to the wretched weather and the consequently unused dainty toilettes</p>
        <p>, 0 !I , r / I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>TAILOR-MADE GOWN.</head>
        <p>A TAILOR-MADE GOWN.</p>
        <p>for there have been weeks of delicious summer weather during which the freshness was taken from white gowns and the colours faded in floral hats. Therefore, the contemplation of</p>
        <p>cloth costumes is as pleasant as it i; welcome and necessary, for the eye becomes weary after a time of fussy diaphanous gowns and turns with satisfactiOn to the more sober but also more solid charms of the tailor-made dress.</p>
        <p>A charming sample is depicted in my first sketch, a pleasant shade of . a warm brown cloth being employed in its constructibri, the smart little coat showing • strappings in an original and fanciful design. These are out- lined by a narrow piping of orange silk for a mixture of orange and yellow with brown is one of the most favoured jiist now. A waist- band of the brightly hued silk finishes the waist, while the hat is trimmed with a silk ruche in which the same colour is employed, the hat itself being of brown Silky felt matching the colour of the cloth used for the dress. The skirt is gauged round the hips in the manner which is so becoming to the slender type of figure, the centre front being left quite plain as it requires really a thin woman to stand the skirt which is gauged or gathered all the way round. The line of the shoulders is long and the fulness of the sleeves is caught into a cuff. Tiny gold buttons give a touch of brightness at intervals on the coatee and the whole is a happy mingling of the .prettiest and latest autumn styles. I have seen the same colours em- ployed very successfully in all varieties of hats and toques. The hat in this instance is in the low crowned, wide brimmed, be-ruched mode so much in vogue.</p>
        <p>A very charming style of dress is set forth in my second illustration peculiarly well suited to the willowy figure which is the desire of all votaries of fashion. Height and a rounded slenderness are absolutely the necessary characteristics for those who wish to clothe themselves in the height of fashion. This pretty dress is made of very soli silk and is adorned with numerous gaugings on both skirt and bodice, the out- lines of the figure, however, being carefully followed and defined. Buttons are used as a trimming for they are applied to wide bands of ribbon passing down the front and across the shoulders. For a young girl the fashion is most becoming as it hides any hint of lingering angularity of outline and is also clinging in its folds. Soft silks are nowadays quite inexpensive and are very popular for they make _ up into very charming afternoon and evening dresses of all sorts and stand a great deal of wear. They do . not require much trimming as if too muck elaborated they lose their cachet. In a soft crimson a dress of this kind is very becoming for winter after- nom or evening wear as it reflects a warm glow on the complexion of the wearer and yet emphasises the clear whiteness of throat and hands. It is quite an art which requires careful study ,to dress in not only one's most becoming colour but in others which may at times seem to prove even - so-</p>
        <p>A USEFUL EAT.</p>
        <p>We turn, now that the days are shorter and colder, to a trimmer and more substantial style of headgear and felt is once more in demand. A pretty and yet simple felt hat is shown in my last sketch trimmed only by a smart bow of mirois velvet and a clasp. The shape is one' which, although it is not unlike those which have made their mark in former seasons, differs from its forerunners in many points. The curve of the brim is quite new, together with the shape of the crown. Felt, both smooth and rough surfaced, will be much worn all this winter and also both hats and toques of feathers laid flat and smooth, while marabout feathers are also made up so as to imitate closely various furs in all the most fashionable colours and. shapes. These feather hats and toques require little trim- ming—a twist of velvet caught by an ornamental buckle or a bird placed at. one side or in front with the wings spread out flat on the crown.</p>
        <p>I saw a very pretty idea expressed most successfully by which the blemishes of an undoubtedly passé blouse were cleverly con- cealed. This was brought about by a thin cream lace bolero finished with a flounce of lace at the lower edge and at the elbows. A *liberal amount of very narrow black velvet ribbon was tastefully interthreaded in an effective manner, and made still more effective by numerous small cut steel buckles which glistened and sparkled enticingly. COQUETTE.</p>
        <p>ow* )4E, , /16T „</p>
        <p>A CHARMING STYLE.</p>
