      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>WINTER PASSION.</head>
        <p>A WINTER PASSION.</p>
        <p>131' MRS. HANS BLACKWOOD, Author of "Meg Wallace," "Too late for Regret,'</p>
        <p>"Ilia Plighted Troth," &amp;o.</p>
        <p>"Lord Charles Singer's Circus"! It was a very exciting bill of -fare for a dull cathedral town at Christmas time, in which mundane amusement was believed to be the certain , road to perdition. ; "Startling and Unparalleled Feats of Equestrian- ism.</p>
        <p>"Nero, the Eccentric Elephant. Performing Lions, Donkeys and Dogs. Monsieur Bianchi and his Wonderful Troupe of Dancing Horses. Mademoiselle Millie Rossini, from the Wild West, on her famous horse Bonnibel !</p>
        <p>"Doors open at 6.30. "Reserved seats, 2s. 3d. Entrance, ls."</p>
        <p>"It might be rather amusing," thought Jack Gordon, a captain in the Royal Horse Artillery, whose battery had been quartered for the dreary Winter months at Topsham Barracks, near Exeter. He glanced at his watch. "Six o'clock," he said to himself half-aloud. "Just time enough to dine comfortably, and look in about the middle of the performance. I'll certainly go • anything is better than the monotonous dullness of this hole."</p>
        <p>His brother officers were away on leave, and he found his own society intolerable; and as most of the neighbourhood were away, Jack Gordon had not a very cheery time. Just then for the modest sum of two shillings for the upper ten and one shilling for the so-called "caterpillars of creation," the largest and most marvellous circus in the world could be seen; and this was a temptation too great even for the most proper . .</p>
        <p>Exeierites to resist._ _ i Long before the doors were opened crowds of , impatient milliner-girls, bank clerks, bakers and ' grocers, soldiers, sailors, tinkers, tailors, with ! doubtless here and there a ploughboy, and probably a stray thief, ,waited outside. It was a motley ! crowd indeed. Fifty yards from the huge tent could be heard the shrieking fifes and wheezy flutes, the cracked violins, the jingling triangles, ; the big drum, played with unceasing energy by an '• extremely red-faced man, as round as the drum , he hammered at.</p>
        <p>"Nero," the performing elephant—a huge beast With long, flapping ears and lengthy trunk—having floundered into the middle of the ring, plunged and waded through his performance with wonderful intelligence; next came the performing lions, donkeys, dancing horses and dogs; all did their accomplishments with equal skill and precision, while Tippo, the clown, made his imbecile jokes, calling forth roars of laughter from the rough and highly appreciative audience. After trying to make the spectators believe he was positively made of indiarubber, turning sundry somersaults and putting himself into the most grotesque and impossible attitudes, he finally perched himself on a large tub in the middle of the sawdust, and said:</p>
        <p>"My lords, ladies and gentlemen, look here If you wanted to get up to the moon and had no ladder long enough to reach there, how would you do it? "</p>
        <p>A dead silence. Bakers, grocers, sailors, soldiers, down to the ploughboy and thief, considerably impressed by the problem, regarded each other inquiringly. Tippo looked at them with a twinkle in his eye, while he sat and twirled his thumbs. "Give it up ? " he asked of a rosy-faced country bumpkin in his Sunday clothes, who sat opposite w, illidoweisdyopen eyes and gaping mouth. he drawled out, with a certain amount of shyness on hearing his own voice in public. "And so do all the other stupids!" Tippo answered, with a chuckle, bringing down the house.</p>
        <p>It was at this moment that Jack Gordon strolled quietly into a seat, as far away from the crowd as possible. He might have found a better seat; it was too near the stables to be' pleasant; the fumes of lamp-oil, musty hay, and horses, creeping through the crevices almost suffocated him, but he was too indolent to move from the objectionable neighbourhood, where he had seated himself in a comfortable chair.</p>
        <p>The orchestra, after two or three deafening, discordant crashes, struck up b monotonous, but not altogether unmelodious air. The ring gate Dpened, and Mademoiselle Millie Rossini came in on her beautiful horse Bonnibel. - -</p>
        <p>A. burst of applause broke from the audience when they saw her. She was scarcely more than a child; a slight figure in a perfectly-fitting dark- blue habit, a face beautiful and pure is an angel's; Large, innocent blue eyes, with a sad, wistful look in them, contrasting strangely with the smile that played around her soft, cherub-like lips. Her face was fair as the purest lily, with a glowing peach tint on her cheeks; hair long and waving, that looked as though it had caught the sun's kisses and kept them entangled in its meshes, hung down to her slender, supple waist. Bonnibel was a very good-looking horse, deep girth, clean limbs, with splendid shoulders, bossed with sinew and muscle, which quivered with restrained power beneath the silky, supple hide; a small, compact head, with just a touch of fire in his restless eyes. Millie Rossini was a decided success. As she went round and round a,burst of applause and admiration broke from the audience, the peach glow deepened on her cheek, and a smile stole across her half-parted lips; but still the same sad, wistful look lingered in her eyes. Jack Gordon sat with his eyes fixed on the girl's face; it had roused him from his indolence and fairly fascinated him. She jumped lightly down from her steed and bowed and kissed her tiny bands to the audience she had so enchanted. As she was tripping out of the ring Captain Gordon leant eagerly forward, and taking a lovely Marechal Niel rosebud out of his coat, he threw it down at her feet. She looked up'to see where the flower had come from; her eyes met those, of Jack Gordon looking down on her admiringly. She stooped hind picked up the rosebud, and pushing it hastily between the folds of her bodice, hurried away. • Captain Gordon strolled home leisurely after the performance was over, smoking a cigarette through the now silent streets, with his thoughts ever and again going back to that lovely child-face that seemed to have bewitched him.</p>
        <p>He was an eligible Fail that all the fair Devonians had lost no opportunities of paying homage to • his writing-t Able was strewn with invitations, trom a stately Christmas dinner-party in the county, to an old maid's tea in a small town villa in Exeter. He was young, and fairly well-off now, with the prospect of a fine property to come to him at the death of an uncle. He had a well. grown figure, uniting muscular power and grace of proportion in such rare degree that it might have served as a model for an athlete. His frank, handsome face, full of pleasant light, had never • failed to prepossess anyone who looked upon it; a fair moustache that matched the crest of crisp curls above his well-shaped brow. He was too indolent to flirt, too fond of independence to marry pi, experiencing a strange sort of void in his heart.</p>
        <p>He had never really been in love. But what was that he felt thrilling through his veins now, as he walked home in that quiet winter night? Was he really caught at last in Cupid's net? The idea was too ludicrous, and he laughed aloud. Bah The girl was nothing to him; only a little street Arab sprung from the lowest depths—a girl who gained her living by amusing a grovelling, vulgar mass of humanity. Oh, no; she was nothing to him. But what a figure she had! so graceful, so perfected, with such soft curves and supple bends; and what a lovely face it was! with those glorious sun-tipped tresses waving over her pure white brow and what sad, but lovely eyes! Jack Gordon thought he had never seen such eyes before.</p>
        <p>Captain Gordon was irresistibly drawn to the circus night after night; he -could not keep away. He always took the same chair, as far away from the motley crowd as he could possibly get; there be sat spellbound, and watched the fairy child in her flying rounds. Was it only fancy? or did the blue eyes that had haunted him night and day perpetually look his way _ - - -</p>
        <p>On the fifth evening, his soft hat well drawn down over his eyes, he waited in the cold and darkness near the back entrance of the giant tent; he felt his heart throbbing fast against his waistcoat, when a slim little figure in a long cloak rushed past him and slightly touched him as she went. That silent, gentle touch thrilled through every rein: he started forward and laid a hand gently on tne girl s snouluer. one seetnea to annum away like a fzightened bird, without once looking at him, and hurried on as if afraid to wait. But</p>
        <p>,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <p>and. paler, her eyes more sad and weary. Then the thought came to him, better far that she should suffer than sin. This decided him; there was only one thing left for him to do; he must go away without a word, without seeing her again; he dared not trust himself to say good-bye. The next morning he. found himself in the up express, with an aching pain stabbing at his heart —such a pain as he had never felt in his life before.</p>
        <p>Millie had spent a restless night; sleep would not come to her. Her brain was all excitement; the morrow was to bring her happiness, freedom. She was up early, and had put together her very slender wardrobe; everything she had barely filled a small tin box. Jack Gordon was to come for her early in the morning; she went to the window of her small room time after time to look down the narrow street, but no signs of him were to be seen. She watched and waited with that tearless agony that is the hardest of all. What had become of him? She could neither eat nor drink; all within her had grown numb. It was the last day of the circus at Exeter. Before the sun rose again in the heavens the huge caravan would be far away. All through the day Millie moved about in a sort of dream, she had not eaten a crumb. When she had gone through a long rehearsal, and was back once more in her small room, she wrote a little letter to Jack; such a scratchy little letter, for she had only just been taught to write between two ruled lines. The circus was crowded the last night of the season. The cracked violins, the big drum, the shrill fifes, the wheezy flutes—all were doing their best with lir:tiring energy. Nero, the elephant, floundered about with more animation than usual. Tippo, the clown, joked more recklessly and grinned almost to his ears. At nine o'clock the gate flew open, and in cantered Mademoiselle Millie Rossini on Bonnibel. She instantly scanned the building with a piteous, heartbroken glance, that rested on a familiar seat, but instead of seeing Captain Gordon, his chair was filled to over- flowing by the portly figure of a well-to-do Exeter butcher. Her face grew white as death, and a shudder ran through her whole frame; she pressed her hand down hard on her heart, as if in acknow- ledgment of her enthusiastic reception, but really trying to press down the pain. The fifes and drums, flutes, violins and triangles sounded louder and louder; Bonnibel flew madly round and round. Then came the "final scene; the whole house rose in a thunder of applause. In an instant there was a dead silence. Near to the entrance of the ring was gathered a horror- stricken group. On the ground, her long waving lair falling around, her blue eyes wide open, she tried for a moment to lift herself on one elbow, and then sank back with a moan of pain. She lay there still, white, most beautiful, with peace at last. upon her sad, sweet face.</p>
        <p>Tippo, the clown, pallid through his paint, bent down and, unfastening the child's dark-blue habit, pout his hand tenderly on her heart; but all was still; never again would it rise and fall with the bound of Bonnibel's stride; those sweet blue eves would never more look on anything this side the grave. A week had gone by. and Jack Pordon had returhed to Topsham. He could not live without his childish love, and had made up his mind to marry her, no matter what the world would say of him.</p>
        <p>But Lord Charles Singer's Circus had left, and only the fragile form of Millie Rossini was left 1., hind, sleeping peacefully under the green grass in Exeter old cemetery, saved by death from siti— saved—snd at rest.</p>
        <p>[TDB END.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>_ Saturday witnessed the conclusion of the successful main in connection with the Warrington and District League. The following are the scores :</p>
        <p>THIRD ROUND.</p>
        <p>J. T. Boardman, 3 A. Scott, 1 J. Lockett, 2 J. W. Worsley, 1 Joseph Weston, 2 J. Ditchfleld, 3 F. Millar, 3 l5 W Swanton, 1. S. Davies, 5 l5 T. Rigby, 2 F. T. Jefferson, sc l5 J. Wood, 5 M. Tobin, 9 l5 H. Perry, 5 . lO J. Swanton, 1.. l5 J. Roberts,l l3 H. Daintith, Sc. W. Jones (4), a bye. FOURTH ROUND.</p>
