      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>EVENTS OF THE WEER.</head>
        <p>EVENTS OF THE WEER.</p>
        <p>ARCHBISHOP OF CANTERBURY IN A RAILWAY</p>
        <p>ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>The journey of the Archbishop of Canter- 'bury through Canada and the States has been within an ace of finishing in dire disaster. Nothing could have been greater than the success of the tour in Canada, after which the Primate went on to the United States as the guest of Mr. Pierpont Morgan. At the end of last week he was .on his way to Washington to visit President Roosevelt in Mr. Pierpont Morgan's special train, accompanied by Mrs. Randall David- son, the great financier, the Vicar of Windsor, and the rest of his party, when the 'Special ran into a goods engine which had accidentally got shunted on to the main line. The accident occurred near ' 'East Brookfield, Massachusetts, and owing to a slight fog the danger signals were not Seen. Moreover, owing to the rails being !lamp the pneumatic brakes did not act. So was practically at a speed of sixty miles -an hour that the collision took place. The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>PIERPONT MORGAN.</head>
        <p>PIERPONT MORGAN.</p>
        <p>ARCHBISHOP OF CANTERBURY. engine which caused the trouble was corn! pletely demolished, and its driver baaly injured, while the engine of the special was thrown off the line and the tender crumpled Up to scrap iron. But, marvellous to relate, not a single carriage of the train was thrown off the line or damaged beyond the breaking of the windows in some cases, nor was any- one hurt beyond a severe shaking. Some of the party were at breakfast, whilst others Were in their sleeping quarters, and, though Pale and shaken, both the Archbishop of Canterbury and Mr. Pierpont Morgan appeared perfectly cool and calm. Three hours later the special proceeded on its Journey with a new engine, Washington being safely reached. Strangely enough, on leaving Oyster Bay the President himself narrowly escaped a railway accident.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>SERIOUS ILLNESS OF LADY CURZON.</head>
        <p>SERIOUS ILLNESS OF LADY CURZON.</p>
        <p>The sympathy of the whole British Empire is with Lord Curzon in his time of sorrow and anxiety, and on every side hopes for the recovery of the Vicereine of India Will find echo. Suddenly and unexpectedly Lady Curzon w struck down, just as she was on the eve of starting with her husband to resume those • Imperial duties which for the last five years they have dis- charged so well in India. The ,dvance lug- ;age had ac- ,ually been dispatched to Walmer Sta- tion en route for India last Tuesday, an d had to be coun- termanded 011</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>LADY CURZO</head>
        <p>LADY CURZO</p>
        <p>Wednesday, when Lady .13nrzon became suddenly ill. She was at Walmer Castle, where, in the home of the Lord Wardens of the Cinque Ports, they have Spent most of their vacation, when she was -sd suddenly taken ill, and on Wednesday things took such a serious turn that Dr. Champneys was called down from London, and Sir Thomas Barlow summoned from abroad. The latter caught the Boulogne boat and was conveyed to Walmer by special train. Acute peritonitis had been diagnosed, and on Saturday a successful operation was Performed as the last resource of medical science, and it gave relief and enabled Lady Curzon to rally. At the time of writing she is. still, however, critically ill. Lady Cur- zon was a daughter of Mr. Leiter, the well-known American millionaire, who died last June, and his death was a great shock to his daughter. She has made Lord Curzon a spiel-I:lid wife, and seconded him most ably 'in India; and in this way she has in a few Years become one of the leading and best- known ladies in the Empire. Lady Curzon has. no son, but three daughters, of whom the youngest was born only last March. Sir Thomas 'Barlow states that the peritonitis has been considerably localised. This is a satis- factory feature. The matron of Deal Hospi- tal is in charge of the nursing arrangements. Lord Curzon's brother, the Hon. Frank Cur- zon, is remaining at the Castle. Lotd Curzon is much worn by anidety, but he bears his grief with fortitude, and finds time to attend Personally to his official dispatches, several of "Which have reached him during the week. Many telegrams of sympathy and inquiry continue to be received, many coming from India. The King is a frequent inquirer, and at his own request his Majesty is kept in- formed of Lady Curzon's condition. At a late hour on Sunday evening a message was re- ceived at the Castle from Balmoral.</p>
        <p>.0, a, g. t• • • r _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>IS LONDON GLOOMY ?</head>
        <p>IS LONDON GLOOMY ?</p>
        <p>Mr. John Burns, the famous M.P. for Lattersea, is ever the ' champion of London. It •is not very long since he was challenging Mr. Harold Begbie on the subject, and now he has his coat off again to tackle Mr. Van Wyck, the Tammany ex-Mayor of New York, Who has been speaking deridingly and con- temptuously of our metropolis. This repre- sentative of the Yankee capital asserts that London is dull, possessing no amusements beyond its theatres and places for eating and drinking, while in a contrasting panegyrie he extols New York, where, he says, there is po poverty. Mr. Burns points out that his Impressions. of both capitals betray little real knowledge of the facts of the case, those of</p>
        <p>tendon being entirely superficial, whilst those aof New York are purposely glossed over. To begin with, Mr. Van Wyck makes the ridiculous statement that we possess no beautiful parks in London, such as exist in varis and Vienna, regardless of the to 6 that are under the auspices of the L.C.C., in addition to royal parks and City Corporation Properties. And, in passing, Mr. Burns slily Points out that, whereas the L.C.C. opened sixty-six parks in fifteen years, in four years ..,Tarnmany only opened one small one. Mr. urns goes on to Ideal with London's amuse- tuents, showing how nightly half a million 'People spend the evening in various places, on Sundays the parks are liberally ‘acovided with bands, and. in conclusion. lin</p>
        <p>DEATH OF NUL WALTER mmnnuc</p>
        <p>By the death of Mr. Walter Severn, president of the Dudley Art Gallery Society, another distinguished artist has been re- moved. The deceased artist was born in Rome in 1830 and was a son of Mr. J. Severn, at one time British Consul in Rome.</p>
        <p>He was edu- cated at West- minster School and began life in the Civil Ser- vice, but be- came known as an amateur artist and made a reputation as a water - colour landscape painter. He also made a vigorous effort to resusi• tate the craft of art needlework THE LATE MR. WALTER and embroidery, SEVERN. for which he earned medals at South Kensington, and re- ceived much encouragement from .Mr. Ruskin. With his friend, Charles Eastlake, he also started the art furniture which has now become so popular. Mr. Severn was the author of some publications which he person- ally- illustrated.</p>
        <p>:FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>produces irrefutable testimony and some striking figures as to the state of poverty and bad housing in Mr. Van Wyck's much vaunted New York. London, after all, is Rt.e,: not as bad as it is painted. "Let us hear no OOTBALGIIRS will be glad to know that the world- more from Mr. Van Wyck about London's F famed manufacturers, Geo. G. BUISEIET &amp; Co., of London, gloom and America's distributed wealth," tarn out Balls, which for quality, uniformity, and durability exclaimed Mr. Burns. "No poverty in New I are unique. These balls will be distinguished. by the appear- York! York ! How dare he say so, when America . well-known tade mark. Ask heti Sports Dealer for G. 0. B. a Co.'s Price List. If no pro. boasts a thousand millionaires, at the same awe thereon of theij time possessing a million tramps? And this curable, apply to 36 &amp;38 Queen Victoria at.. London tip Paetory —Peckham, S.R. (63). appeared in the 1900 Socialist Campaign Book: 'With an army of tramps, estimated at several hundred thousand, with another million that finds itself slowly but surely pushed down among the perennially unem- ployed, with the workers who have employ- ment losing nearly one-third of their labour time, the American labouring class will soon be forced to recognise its proletarian character.' Turning to his well-stocked library of facts and figures, Mr. Burns proved up to the hilt the absurdity of the statement that there is no poverty in New York. Such a suggestion, he said, on the part of an ex-Mayor was the coolest piece of Yankee bluff he had ever heard. How could Mr. Van Wyck reconcile his statement with yesterday's telegram from New York which says there is no cessation of the carnival of crime there. Since August I there had been 24 homicides, 253 burglaries, 68 robberies, and 95 felonious assaults. "If that is the usual pastime of a prosperous, gay, and festive people, all I can say is that I hope London will never become so beastly rich."</p>
