      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>_ . • . . • • • •• ' , " TIT_ „ _ • __ A. . – • –</head>
        <p>_ . • . . • • • •• ' , " TIT_ „ _ • __ A. . - • -</p>
        <p>MOB LAW IN VENICE.</p>
        <p>In most of the industrial centres of Italy during the last few weeks mob law has prevailed and amongst the many remarkable scenes witnessed were those at Venice, where for forty-eight hours the canals were without gondolas. Never before in the history of the city had there been such a triumph over authority. The trouble began by preconcer- ted arrangement on Sunday evening, when crowds paraded the streets and, putting out the lights, plunged the city into com- plete darkness. There was no pretence at opppsition by the police, and the mob had affairs all their own way. Emboldened by their success, the mob paraded before the city hall, and, after hooting the police, dared them to come out. The police replied by firing a couple of revolver shots into the air—a proceeding at which the crowd jeered.</p>
        <p>, ----"" 1 ;111 • , ~,•i. Nil• ~. LI, (471,,,- 0 ii .1....,1.. ti ~ i. . i -.„ --., ....... ~.),) ~ •,--- .....„, ~.---- • I J .... , ~ 11 f • 1 4,-titic.,, 4 ...i) - .. 7.... , ....- .4•14.„ 4 , . r -#7° • ~:,...k. ti-,</p>
