      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>VENETIAN SCENE</head>
        <p>A VENETIAN SCENE</p>
        <p>stoppage of the steamers and gondolas on the Grand Canal soon followed. Travel- lers could obtain no conveyance to the railway station, and had to remain over the day. A few people who had gondolas of their own attempted to approach the station but were prevented. Travellers arriving in the city could find no conveyances and had to trudge to their hotels on foot, and do the best they could with their luggage. Then, after five days' mob law, the strike was declared off, the people returned to their work without a murmur, and within a few hours the entire country resumed its ,wonted habits of industry.</p>
        <p>M. DE PLEHVE'S SUCCESSOR.</p>
        <p>It takes a bold man to succeed M. de Plehve and his predecessors as. Minister of the Interior in Russia, and the post has not been in .great demand amongst Russian statesmen and courtiers. . But the requisite brave man has been found in Prince- Sviato- i3olk-Mirski, and his appointment has been unanimously hailed_ by all sections of the</p>
        <p>Press and the</p>
        <p>public,_ who _re:-</p>
        <p>gsard the seled-</p>
        <p>iion of such a</p>
        <p>leading Liberal</p>
        <p>and Well known advocate of milder methods</p>
        <p>as a promise of</p>
        <p>reform and more reasonable ad-</p>
        <p>cal step -towards it. Again, Prince Mirski Vir a young man and full ,of energy, which win be required if he is to make any impression on Russian bureaucracy and the iron tra- ditions of the department of the Interior. Born in 1859; the new Minister started life as a soldier, and it is only within the last few years that his value as an administrator has been found out. Educated in the Corps of Pages, in 1875 he, was given a commission in the Hussars, and two years later he was specially placed at the disposal of the Com- mander of the Caucasian Army and took an active part in the Russo-Turkish war from the Asiatic side. He distinguished himself greatly and won the Order, of. Anne, which corresponds- to our V.C., while in 1881 he was appointed a lieutenant-colonel on the staff. From 1886 to 1890 he was Chief of the Staff to the 3rd Grenadier Division. Five years later Prince Mirski was made Governor of Peusa, and two years after was • transferred to Chaterinoslav, and there. it was that his administrative talents Were discovered. In 1900 the .Tsar made him Assistant Minister of the Interior, and in 19(32 he was entrusted with the: Governorship of the North-Western Provinces, where be won golden opinions. His new office is the one in which many a Russian statesman has failed, and it will be a great .day for Russia if Prince Mirski can administer it with his past success.</p>
        <p>p ministration. With the birth of the Tsare- ' vitch a mOre generous regime was promised, and this is PRINCE MIRSKI. • looked upon as the first practi-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>SALFORD v. LEEDS.</head>
        <p>SALFORD v. LEEDS.</p>
        <p>Despite many other attractions in Manchester, fully 7,000 spectators turned out to witness this match on the Weaste ground. Salford lost the services of Bedford, who had to leave the field through an injury to his knee. Soon after his departure Preston the Salford half, scored a smart try, dodging past almost the whole of the Leeds back division. Half-time score—Salford, 1 try (3 points) ; Leeds, nil. In the second half Leeds were able to keep up a strong attack, which culminated in Evans, after several attempts, scoring the equalising try at the corner. The game became more closely contested after this, and it looked as if it would end in a draw, when two tries were added in quid* succession by Birch and Llewellyn. None of the tries were converted. Result :—Leeds, 3trles (9 points); Salford, I try (3 points.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>HUNSLET v. OLDHAM.</head>
        <p>HUNSLET v. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>Acrowd of some 6,000 persons assembled at Parkside to watch the Hunslet v. Oldham match which had an additional spiciness from the fact that up to Saturday Oldham were an unbeaten team. S. Lees and Cash were noticeable absentees from the Oldham three-quarter line. At half-time Hunslet were leading by a goal to nil. A couple of misfortunes overtook Oldham in the second half. Ferguson who had been playing full back was compelled to leave the game in consequence of an injury to his head, sustained in a tackle, while Owens, who had gone three-quarters, vice Thomas, was sent off the field for striking Davies. Considering this handicap Oldham played up well, but were over-run in the for- wards. Shortly afterwards the visitors were again penalised for packing four deep, and Walter Goldthorpe placed a second goal. After this Everson had two good openings, but he failed to take them. W. Goldthorpe however, placed a third goal at a later stage, as the result of a penalty, and the end therefore came with Hunslet in front by !3 goals (6 points) to Oldham nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>ITCHING, IRRITATING, ECZEMA. A Local Preacher and Zam-Buk</head>
        <p>ITCHING, IRRITATING, ECZEMA. A Local Preacher and Zam-Buk</p>
        <p>It is hard to imagine a more irritable, agonising, and distressing condition than that of the sufferer from eczema. This is a disease of the epidermis or outer layer of the skin, which does not mature, and allows the watery portion of the blood to exude, forming scabs or incrustations, which, on being removed, leave a red, angry, irritable, itching surface. Wonderfully soothing and curative is the application of Zam-Buk spread on a fresh piece of lint night and morning, as the following testimony of Mr. John Millett Harris, a well-known local preacher living at Lyng. near Norwich, goes to prove. Mr. Harris, who is prominently identified with the Primitive Methodist body, said to a "Norwich Mer- cury" reporter :—"Two years ago eczema made its appearance, breaking out in a small spot on my arm. At first it was only slight, but it gradually spread almost all over my body. The irritation became something fearful. There were raw places on my body which seemed as though they were constantly being burned by fire. I would have given any money to have been free front it and'able to keep at work. As for trying to get rid of it I was attended by two clOctors, tried liquid medicine and any amount of remedies, including zinc and other ointments,but nothing seemed to give me any relief. I got terribly weak, and even the food I took appeared to do me but little good. At last a circular came by post describing Zam- Buk, the new herbal balm. I sent for some and applied it at once. Directly it was rubbed on the sores I felt relieved. At the same time, I may add, I took a couple of bile beans which regulated my bowels and doubtless helped the outward applications of Zam-Buk Both were worth infinitely more than they had cost me. Wherever I applied Zam-Buk the sores healed wonderfully, the rawness went at once the eCZeratt patches lost their 'fire,' and I very soon felt all right. I have lately been up at four o'clock every morning stone-raising and have quite recovered my old strength. I attribute this to Zam-Buk, aided by bile beans, and have given some of the ointment to many people, all of whom state they have derived great benefit ham using it. My father here gets bed sores, and ap- plications of Zam-Buk take the soreness away directly. A neighbour's child was also benefited, and I might just say that I will willingly answer any questions that any- one likes to address to me. I quite believe that such cases as mine should be published for the public good." Zam-Buk has proved invaluable for cuts. bruises,burns scalds, eczema, chafing, itch, sores, cramp, stiff joints, raw chins, blackheads, chilblains, ulcers, festering sores sore feet, weak ankles, chapped hands, ringworms, chill, colds, sprains, strains, stiffness, bad legs, blind and bleeding piles, swollen knees, inflamed patches, boils, abscesses, pimples, scalp irritation, poisoned wounds, dog bites cat scratches, rheumatism, neuralgia, sciatica lumbago:toothache, and for all deceased, injured, and irritated conditions of the skin. Price is. 10-d, per box, or 2s. 9d., for family size (2s. 9d. box contains nearly 4 times the is. lod.) Of all chemists, or post free, for same price, from the Zam-Buk Co., 4, Red Cross Street, B.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>WESLEYAN MINISTERS' VOTING POWERS.</head>
