      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>First Division.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S FINE VICTORY OVER BRADFORD.</p>
        <p>The visit of the well-known Bradford team attracted a crowd of about 6,000 to the Wilderspool enclosure on Saturday afternoon. An exciting match, full of incident, was witnessed, but Bradford were thoroughly outplayed, and Warrington achieved a splendid victory by 13 points to nil. Mr. W. W. Taylor, of Hunslet, was the referee, and the teams lined up as follows : Warrington.—Hallam, back; Fish, Isherwood, Dicken- son, and Hockenhall, three-quarter backs; Davies and Brooks, half backs; Thomas, Cook, Belton, Jolley, Boardman, Naylor, Morrison, and Shugars, forwards. Bradford.—Gunn, back; Dunbavin, Eagers, Mosby, and Dechan, three-quarter backs; Marsden and Surman, half backs • Laidlaw, Feather, Smales, Turner, Francis, Hartley, dreenwood, and Hutt, forwards. Touch judges, Messrs. G. Robinson, Runcorn, and A. Critchley, Widnes. Thomas set the ball in motion, the Warringtonians having t, face the breeze in the first half. The home team made a rush into the visitors' 25, but a free kick brought relief. A weak kick by Isherwood put Hutt in possession, and he landed the ball to Hallam, who replied nicely. Mosby, from a free kick, tried at goal for Brad- ford, the ball just going outside. Soon afterwards, the Bradford backs got going, and Dechan obtained posses- sion but was brought down by Fish. Hallam marked finely from a kick by Eagers with a host of opponents around him. Dunbavin got away, and, kicking over Hallam's head, the ball rolled over the Warrington line, Isherwood knocking it dead. Davies tried to break through, but was well held. Fish, picking up the ball, nicely put a well-judged touch-kick, placing the home team on the attack. A free kick, taken by Laidlaw, landed the ball near the Warrington goal, Dickenson replying at centre. Davies secured the ball from a scrimmage and transferred to Fish, who made a fine run, but was pulled up when near the line. A few minutes afterwards Brooks dashed in between the posts, amid great enthusiasm, and Fish landed an easy goal. Following this reverse, Bradford had a turn of attacking, and twice Mosby had futile shots at goal from penalties. Marsden next opened out the game, but Warrington easily held the opposition in check. IHalf-time score :—Warrington 1 goal 1 try (5 points), IBradford nil. I Laidlaw restarted, and BradfXrd made strong play in the opening stages, but Dickenson eventually led the lway in what proved a permanent aggression. Belton held the ball when a score appeared a certainty for Fish but from a scrummage a minute later Brooks dashed over for his second try. Fish failed at goal. Laidlaw and Morrison were ordered off for foul work, Surman made a brilliant effort, and passing to Dunbavin the latter was pushed into touch at the corner. Naylor got away from the scrimmage and put in a nice kick. Fish tried at goal from a penalty, but the ball fell short. The IBradford backs tried another passing bout, but Isher- 'wood frustrated the effort. A pass by Isherwood put Fish in possession, and after evading many opponents, he scored in brilliant style, and also kicked a goal. Dechan, receiving From Mosby was pushed in touch at the corner; A dribble by the Warrington forwards was pluckily stopped by Gunn when a score seemed imminent. The final result was :—Warrington 13 points,Bradford nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>WIDNES v. RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>WIDNES v. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Played at Widnes, before 3,ooospectators. Teams : Widnes—Tilley, back ; Hughes, Taylor, Peacock, and Barber, three-quarter backs ; Bennett and Kennedy, half backs; G. Aspey, S. Aspey, Cockram, Curtis, Lynch, Leyshon, McCabe and Webster forwards. Runcorn—Houghton, back; 'Jones, Warder, Butter- worth, and Turton, three-quarter backs; Richardson and Faulkner, half backs; Walker, C. Houghton, A. Richardson, Higgins, Dobson, Dixon, Tomlinson, and Padbury, forwards. Referee, Mr. J. Priestley, Salford. Touch judges, Messrs. C. Simm, Wigan and F. Little, St. Helens. Aspey started for Widnes, and a great deal of ex- change kicking was indulged in, Houghton repeatedly finding touch and putting his side onthe attack. Hough- ton attempted to place a goal from the half-way line, but the ball fell short. Just before the interval Tilley opened the scoring by placing a goal and at half-time Widnes led by 1 goal (2 points) to Runcorn nil. The second half opened in favour of Runcorn, and from a scrummage on the Widnes line the visitors looked like scoring, but Hughes intercepted a pass meant for Butter- worth, and kicked up the field. When the play was at the centre, three of the home forwards. Curtis, Webster, and McCabe, started a passing movement, and after they had passed and re-passed, Taylor got possession and scored a try near the comer. Tilley added two more points by kicking a goal. Shortly afterwards Taylor scored I second try. No goal resulted. Result : Widnes, 2 goals and 2 tries (10 points), Runcorn, nil. BATLEY v. WIGAN: THE REFEREE ASSAULTE D</p>
        <p>scores were :—Hull 1 try (3 points), Wakefield Trinity 1 goal (2 points). About five minutes before the call of time Wakefield were awarded a free kick and Meason landed a goal, and thus placed Wakefield a point in front. ; Jackson then made a good effort to score, but was pushed into touch, and then Hull were awarded a free kick, but Jackson sent the leather wide. Bennett kicked to Goodfellow, who dropped a goal, thus winning the game for his side by a single point. Result :—Hull 1 goal and 1 try (5 points), Wakefield Trinity 2 goals (4 points). HULL KINGSTON ROVERS v. SWINTON.</p>
        <p>This match, which was played on the Craven-street ground, provided the 5,000 spectators with a lively and interesting game. Half-time saw the Rovers with the substantial lead of 8 points to nil. After the resump- tion it was soon evident that the Rovers were outplaying their opponents. Barry was especially to the fore, an through his efforts Phipps was literally put over the line in front of the posts, where Starks ought to have added the goal points. It was about three-quarter time before Swinton opened their account with a fine goal kicked by Molyneux consequent upon the Rovers being penalised for offside play. Result: —Hull Kingston Rovers 1 goal 3 tries (11 points), Swinton 1 goal (2 points). BROUGHTON RANGERS v. ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Only a poor attendance assembled at Broughton,where the home team met St. Helens.The game was of a very one-sided character. Harris was the first to cross the line, whilst Sam James ended up a brilliant bit of in- dividual play by scoring a clever try near the corner. Worthington took the place, and landed a goal. This proved to be the extent of the scoring in the first half the home team crossing over with a lead of 8 points to nil. The second portion was of a more one-sided char- acter than the first. After the first few minutes the visitors were continually defending, Harry (2) and Harris each recording capital tries, while Worthington improved upon one of them. The St. Helens forwards eventually came away in a body,and from some scrambl- ling play on the home line one of the visiting forwards dropped on the ball and scored, and Traynor kicked a goal. Towards the close the Rangers came again, and Worthington ran in a smart try, the same player land- ing a goal. Result :—Broughton Rangers $ goals 6 tries (24 points), St. Helens 1 goal 1 try(s points.;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Don't Boil</head>
        <p>Don't Boil</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS SENSATION.</p>
        <p>THE CASE OF SARAH JONES.</p>
        <p>Quantity of Arsenic Found.</p>
        <p>THE VERDICT OF THE CORONER'S JURY.</p>
        <p>The interest which has been aroused in the mysterious death by poisoning of Sarah Ann Jones, 13 years of age, the adopted child of Joseph and Ellen Burndred, of 38, South John-street, St. Helens, developed a further stage on Wednesday, when the county coroner (Mr. 5, Brighouse) resumed his investigation into the circumstances surrounding the deaths of the girl and the infant, Joseph Burndced. C)aief-Constable Wood appeared on behalf of the police; Mr. C. P. Kelly represented the prisoners; Mr. E. W. Swift attended on behalf of the Prescot Board of Guar- dians, who had boarded-out the girl Jones to lin Burn- dreds ; and Mr. James Wilson (Wigan) watched the case on behalf of the Royal London .Friendly Society and. the British Workmen's and General Insurance Com- pany. The Coroner, addressing the jury, said he proposed to first deal with the circumstances attending the death of Joseph Burndred, and, after calling certain evidence, ,he would ask them to return a formal verdict in that ; case in accordance with the medical certificate of Dr. Jackson, who attended Joseph Burndred in his last illness. They would hear from the analysis that no ' traces of arsenic had been found in that body, and con- sequently there was no necessity to proceed further, so far as that body was concerned. Then with respect to the death of Sarah Ann Jones, they would hear the result of he analysis, and although the police had in custody Joseph Burndred and his wife Ellen, he (the coroner) had had the opportunity of looking through the evidence that the police proposed to put before them, and his opinion was there was no evidence against Joseph Burndred, the husband. He told them at that stage, so that the whole thing narrowed itself down to the simple issue—Did Sarah Jones die from arsenical poisoning if so, was there sufficient evidence to justif y , them in sending Ellen Burndred for trial for having caused her death? That wasa very simple matter, and it was a plain issue for them to decide on the evidence.</p>
        <p>THE SECOND EXHUMATION. JURY'S FINDINGS.</p>
        <p>The Coroner intimated that he would first deal with the case of the child Joseph, three months old. Detective Inspector Strong and Mr. Joseph Ilesford, cemetery registrar, then repeated their evidence given at the formal opening of the inquiry as to the exhuma- tion of the remains; and Dr. Joseph Unsworth, North- road, St. Helens, deposed that the coffin was well preserved. All that was left of the body were some bones lying on some wood shavings. Around what had been the middle of the body was a cloth binder, inside which were organic remains, and the whole were removed for analysis just as they were found. Mr. Herbert E. Davies, analytical chemist, of Liver- pool gave evidence as to his attendance at the exhuma- tion and removal of the remains of the boy, Joseph Burndred. They were removed to his laboratory at Liverpool. The body was completely disintegrated. He subjected the woodwork of the coffin, a portion of the shavings at the bottom of the coffin,and the remains to a very careful analysis, and applied various tests for the discovery of arsenic or other poison, but he could not find any traces, except of zinc and copper, which had nothing to do with the body. They were due to the corrosion of brass pins, two of which he found in the coffin.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: Are you positive that no arsenic can possibly have been administered in this case? Witness: I cannot go further than say that in the body as sent to me there was no arsenic. The Coroner: Is it 199saible • for arsenic to be administered to a child of this age in sufficient quanti- ties to cause death and still no traces of arsenic to be found?</p>
        <p>Witness: Yes. Cases are on record where people have died from the effects of arsenical poisoning where the whole of the arsenic had disappeared from the body. The Coroner: This child was aged three months. Would it take a large quantity to bring about a fatal result?</p>
        <p>Witness: No ; a very small quantity would be sufficient.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: Such a quantity as to leave no traces behind.</p>
        <p>Witness: That of course is impossible to say; but if there had been any appreciable quantity in the body I think the methods of analysis would have found it.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: We will take it, then, that you found no traces of arsenic in the body? Witnes : None whatever. ,</p>
        <p>Dr. Jackson then gave evidence as to his attendance on the child during August-and September, 1900, and on its death he gave a certificate that death was due to " debility fronibirth."</p>
        <p>The Coroner said that was all the evidence he would call in that case, and asked the jury if they were satis- fied to return a verdiot in accordance with the certifi- cate given by Dr. Jackion. After a brief consultation,the Foreman (Mr. T. Shaw) said the jury, returned a verdict of Death from debility."</p>
        <p>THE CASE OF SARAH JONES. THE ANALYST'S FINDINGS.</p>
        <p>The case of Sarah Ann Jones was then taken up by the coroner,who said he would proceed with the evidence at once.</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Hesford, cemetery registrar, repeated his evidence as to the exhumation of the body. Mr. H. E. Davies, M.A., of the firm of Messrs. Edward Davies, and Son, analytical and consulting chemists, 28, Chapel-street, Liverpool, was then called. He deposed that he had subjected the contents of six jars received from Inspector Strong to analysis. The jars contained stomach, liver, kidneys, intestines, lungs, and brains. He found traces of arsenical trioxide in the liver, as well as traces of zinc. He also found traces of arsenic in the stomach, intestines, kidneys, lungs, and brain. In a case such as that, where the arsenic was disseminated throughout the body, it followed that the arsenic had been introduced some time *before death, and much of it would be removed by vomiting and purging, and in estimating the quantity found in the body .that only represented a por- tion of the original amount, which must have been a large and fatal quantity.—ln answer to questions by the coroner, witness said it depended upon the quantity of arsenic taken whether it was found in all the organs, as in that case. In his experience it was not usual to find it so widely distributed throughout the body.—ln continuing his evidence, Mr. Davies said he had received from Inspector Strong bottles of eye- vash and other things which had been found at the house of the Burndreds, and also stock bottles of medi- cine from Dr. Bellew's surgery, from which the medicine supplied to the girl Jones had been made up. He had analysed the contents of all, and found no traces of arsenic in any of them. At this point Mr. Davies handed to the coroner some small tubes containing arsenic, which had been extracted from the girl's remains, and these were handed to the jury for their inspection. In answer to the coroner, witness said a large and fatal dose of arsenic must aye been administered to the girl, but it was impossible to say whether it was one large dose or a number of small doses administered at different times. He was of the opinion that it had been given in small doses, as it had got disseminated right through the body. Dr. Unsworth, of North-road, St. Helens, deposed that he had made a post-mortem examination of the body, which was well developed and well nourished, and there were no external signs of injury or disease. He proceeded to describe the condition of the various organs, which he !said were all healthy, and there were no signs of peritonitis. The Coroner: After your post-mortem examination and the analysis of Mr. Davies, what is your opinion as to the cause of death?</p>
        <p>Witness: The child died from arsenical poisoning. The Coroner: Can there be any doubt about it? Witness: I have no doubt about it whatever.</p>
        <p>In answer to further questbns, Dr. Unsworth said in , cases of gastritis the symptoms were practically the same as those in arsenical pois.ning. A medical man, ' in the absence of suspicious circumstances,might easily be deceived as to whether a case was one of gastritis or arsenical poisoning. Dr. Bellew, of Cotharn-street, St. Helens, was the next witness. He said he was called to see the girl Jones at Burndred's house in South John-street on the 29th July last. She was seated on a sofa in the house,and seemed to be suffering from whatJ3e then thought was a case of gastritis. She had been vomiting into a bucket, which had some greenish stuff in it. He made some inquiries, and Mrs. Burndred told him that the girl was suffering from diarrhcea. He ordered' her to be fed on nothing but barley and water, or brandy or whisky and water. He also made up a bottle of medicine for her, and ordered poultices for her. The symptoms were the same practically as those which, prevailed up to her 'death, and on the girl's death he gave her a certificate that the cause of death was " gastritis exhaustion." At that time there was an epidemic of gastritis in St. Helens, and he agreed with Dr. Unsworth that the symptoms of gastritis were similar to those of arsenical poisoning. He was present at the post-mortem examina- tion, and had heard the evidence of Mr. Davies, the analyst. The Coroner: After what you haveheard of the post- mortem and analysis, and what you saw of the girl, what is your opinion now as to the cause of the girl's death? Witness: lam sure this child has died from arsenical poisoning. In answer to further questions, Dr. Bellew said that before he was called to see the girl Jones the Burndreis were strangers to him. When the child had died Mrs. Burndred came and paid the account-24s.—without Ibeing asked to do so. I reply to Mr. Kelly, he said he attended the girl</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>PERFECT LEMON JELLY.</head>
        <p>A PERFECT LEMON JELLY.</p>
        <p>For the exquisite flavour of Ripe Lemons there are no Table Jellies to equal Eiffel Tower Lemon Beautifully clear and they always set.</p>
        <p>daily for eight days. Mrs. Burndred was there every time, and always appeared to be sympathetic and affec- tionate towards her. Mrs. Burndred had told tin: that although tha girl was an adopted child she took as much interest in it as if it was her own.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>MEDICAL OFFICER'S SUSPICIONS.</head>
        <p>THE MEDICAL OFFICER'S SUSPICIONS.</p>
        <p>Dr. O'Keefe, of Park-road, St. Helens was then called. He stated that he was deputy-medical officer for Parr and Sutton for the Prescot Board of Guardians. He was well acquainted with the Burndreds, and had known them for many years. He had attended three of their children—Sarah, four months, who died on the 21st November, 1895; Albert, aged two months, who died on the 31st July, 1897 : and Emily Emma, aged two weeks, who died on the Bth April, 1899. The children all suffered from gastritis and convulsions, although in such cases affecting children a doctor had to accept the statement of the mother as to the symptoms. In the case of the last child Mrs.Burndred's demeanour excited his suspicion. When he gave her the certificate of death, he expressed sympathy with her, but she seemed to treat it unnaturally. She laughed over the matter, and said the child would be much better off. He thought that was unmotherly and unnatural, and it aroused his suspicions. In June, 1900, he was called in, to see the child Joseph. There did not appear to be anything wrong with it, and he did not give any medi- cine for it. Mrs. Burndred did not appear to act as a mother should do; she seemed to be desirous that it should die. Shortly afterwards he was again called to see it, and he again found it apparently quite well, and would not give any medicine for it. He was again called in later on, and he did not see anything then that led him to suppose that the child was likely to die. Mrs. Burndred, however, said that the child was going to die,and on asking her why she said that, she replied that it would not take anything. He asked for some milk, which Mrs. Burncired obtained after some hesita- tion, and the child took that all right. He said to her," Give it milk, and the child will take it." He called on the following day, when Mrs. Burndred said she thought the child would have died during the night but witness told her there was absolutely nothing wrong with it. She said, I have reared a good number of children, and all have died suddenly. You will find me at your place to-morrow morning or else to-night for a certificate of death." He told her that if it died he would not give her a certificate—he would have al post-mortem examination, and see what had caused its I death. He qualified that statement by saying that it might help them to save children's lives in the future. The child was quite well on the next day, and witness was not again called in. On hearing of the girl's death he saw Dr.Bellew and spoke to him about the symptoms. In answer to the Coroner, he said that was not a sudden suspicion in his mind. He had spoken to two members of the police force about the deaths in the Burndred family a long time ago. He was of opinion, after what he knew and heard that day, that the girl had died from arsenical poisoning, the result of con- tinuons small doses.</p>
        <p>The Coroner asked if it was true that white arsenic was a product of works in St. Helens, and Dr. O'Keefe said it was used in large quantities in some of the works. He knew that people could get it and take it home, and did so to poison obeetles, cockroaches, and vermin.</p>
        <p>Joseph Cooper, relieving officer for the Prescot Union, produced a report made by a former (and now deceased) officer as o the circumstances under which the girl Sarah Ann Jones became chargeable to the Prescot Guardians, and gave evidence that the girl Jones was boarded out to the Burndreds, after inquiries, in August, 1901. Ellen Burndred had made application for a girl to live with her as a companion for her daughter Sophia. The agreement was signed by Mr. Burndred. One of the conditions was that the Burndreds would receive 3s. per week for bringing up the child, and 10s. per quarter for her clothing, or £9 16s. per year. Witness visited the house monthly, and did not notice anything of a suspicious character. If the child had required medical attention Mrs. Burndred would have been entitled to receive it from the union. On the Ist April last the child was taken back to the workhouse, but on the following day Mrs. Burndred asked to have the girl re- turned. She said they had all been upset. So far as witness could judge, she seemed to have an affection for the child. The girl was afterwards adopted by the Burndreds without any payment from the guardians, and she left the workhouse on the 2nd June.</p>
        <p>The Coroner said that, looking- atthe matter from a money standpoint; the Burndreds were £9 16s, a year worse off by adopting her. Witness said that was so, but if the paymeuts had been kept up they would have been discontinued in a couple of months owing to the girl's age. In answer to ques- tions, he added that when Mrs. Barndred reported the death she said the child had had some cabbage, which had started the colic, and she never recovered. He said she could have had medical attendance for the girl free, and she replied that she would not let Dr. O'Keefe over her doorstep again. John Lloyd, 212, Blackbrook-road, Haydock, an agent of the Royal London Friendly Society, said he had known the Burndred's for about six years, and he called at their house for premiums on behalf of his society. The girl Sarah Ann Jones was insured on the 2nd Sep- tember. 1901. The policy was for 2d per week, and £2O 12s. was paid at her death. Witness was not sure that Mr. Burndred was present when the contract was made. In answer to a juror, witness said that no other person outside the Burndreds had the girl Jones insured in his society. George Stead, agent of the British Workmen's and General Assurance Company, Limited, spoke to having known the Burndreds for eight, or nine years. On the Ist February last he aecep?3d an insurance in the case of the girl Jones at a premmtn of id. a week. The Coroner pointed out that in the proposal forra it was stated that the girl was not insured in any other society, which was untrue, as the girl was insured in another society. In answer to a question, witness admitted filling in answers to some of the questions after the document had been signed, and the Coroner said that was a very wrong thing to do. It was a dangerous practice. He had known people to be sent to prison for that kind of thing. The Coroner also called attention to the signa- tures of Ellen Burndred and Sarah Ann Jones, and said they appeared to be in the same haildwriting.—Witness said that both Mrs. Burndred and the girl Jones signed the document themselves.</p>
        <p>John;Kiii-o-n-,--5-7-,—li'e.Wck-road, superintendent of the British Workmen's and Genoral Assurance Company, said that Mrs. Burndred was paid a 135., less 15s. arrears, on the death of the girl Jones. PRISONERS' DAUGHTER CALLED.</p>
        <p>Sophia Burndred, daughter of the prisoners, was then called. She said she was 20 'years of age. She spoke as to the girl Jones coining to live with them, and said she thought her illness started after Jones had eaten some cold cabbage for dinner. She was for some time very jocular. She started vomiting one day, and about the third day after that Dr. Bellew was called in. For three or four days before her death she slept in a room upstairs with her mother. Her mother used to give her barley and water, and milk and soda water. Her mother always attended her and gave her those things. She never saw her father attend to her in that way, her mother having full control and looking after her. They were on good terms with the girl Jones, and witness was very fond of her. In reply to Chief-Constable Wood, witness said she could not remember if anyone else partook of the cold cabbage besides the girl Jnoes. Her mother told her something about the insurance money. She told witness that she had told her father that she had only got £lO for insurance, and if her father asked her (witness) about it she was to say that they had only got £lO. In reply to Mr. Kelly, witness said that, as far as she could see, her mother was always kind to the girl Jones, and treated her affectionately as if she were her own child.</p>
        <p>The inquiry was then adjourned until Thursday. A VERDICT OF "WILFUL MURDER" AGAINST</p>
        <p>MRS. BURND RED.</p>
        <p>On Thursday the inquest was resumed. The first witness heard was John Henry M'lnteggert, who said he was engaged to Miss Sophia Burndred and had lived at the Burndreds' house. There was no one in the house during the girl's illness except himself and Mrs. and Miss Bumdred. The woman took care of Jones, preparing what food she had. The witness had some of the cabbage of which she had eaten before she became ill, and it made him poorly too. Several travelling drapers were called, and they stated that Mrs. Barndred was indebted to them. Mr. Kelly, who represented the prisoner, urged that this evidence was not relevant to the issue.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: Here is a woman who is hand up and owes money all round. Surely that gives motive ; and then by the death of this girl she draws £3O. I should have thought it was most material to the issue. Mr. Kelly replied that it seemed far fetched. The Coroner said he would tell the jury that this woman had every inducement to do wrong. Mr. Kelly said the reference that a person would com- mit murder because she owed £2 to a travelling draper was far fetched.</p>
        <p>The Coroner: If you are going to talk like that we will look at the annals of crime. People have taken lives for less than £2. How many are murdered for less than ss. Their lives are taken away on the smallest pretext, and sometimes without any.___</p>
        <p>- An insurance agent named Josepti Blagg, who visited the Burndreds' house when the girl Jones was ill, said Mrs. Burndred asked him what he thought about the girl, and he said there was something unnatural about her that he could not understand. He did not know why he made that remark.</p>
        <p>Margaret Daniels gave evidence that on the day the girl died she saw her in bed, and she was unconscious. She said to the female prisoner that the girl was suffer- ing very much, andishe replied that she was dying as fast as she could, and would not be there by dinner time.</p>
        <p>Thomas Wilson gave evidence of seeing Mrs. Burndred attempt to commit suicide by throwing herself into a , deep pit on the morning after the body of the girl ' Jones was exhumed. When asked why she had jumped , in she said she was troubled. Inspector Strong, who arrested the prisoners, said Mrs. Burndred, in reply to the charge of murder, ; answered, "It is not correct," and the male prisoner said "I have nothing to say; I know nothing." 1 Mr. Kelly intimated that he proposed to call the ' prisoners. 1 The Coroner said that as far as Joseph Burndred was</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>SORE HANDS</head>
        <p>SORE HANDS</p>
        <p>Itching, Burning Palms, Painful Finger Ends, Shapeless Nails.</p>
