      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>ONE NIGHT TREATMENT.</head>
        <p>ONE NIGHT TREATMENT.</p>
        <p>Soak the hands or feet on retiring in a strong, hot, creamy lather of Cuticura Soap. Dry and anoint freely with Cuti- cura Ointment, the great skin cure and purest of emollients. Wear on the hands during the night old, loose gloves, and bandage the feet lightly in old, soft cotton or linen. For red, rough and chapped hands, dry, fissured, itching, feverish palms, with brittle, shapeless nails and painful finger ends, or sore, tender, inflamed or itching feet, this treatment is simply wonderful, fre- quently curing in a single application. Complete local and constitutional treatment for every humour of the skin, scalp and blood, with loss of hair, may now be had for 4s. 9cl. Bathe with hot water and Cuticura Soap, to cleanse the surface of crusts and scales, and soften the thickened cuticle. Dry, without hard rubbing, and apply Cuti- cura Ointment freely, to allay itching, Irritation and inflammation, and soothe and heal, and lastly, take the Cuticura Resolvent Pills to cool and cleanse the blood. This treatment affords instant relief, permits rest and sleep in the severest forms of Eczema and other Itching, burning and scaly humours, and points to a speedy, permanent and economical cure of torturing, disfigur- ing humours, from pimples to scrofula, from infancy to age, when all other remedies and the best physicians fail. Sold throughout the world. Cuticura Resolvent, 2s. 6.1. (in form of Chocolate Coated Pills, Is. lid. per vial of 60), Ointment, 2s. 6d., Soap. Is. Depots London, 27 Charter.. house Sq.; Paris, 5 Rue de la Pais; Boston. 137 Colum- bus Ave. Potter Drug &amp; Chem. Corp.. Sole Proprietors. ' KirSend for "How to Cure Every Humour."</p>
        <p>concerned, he would tell the jury there was no oriaence against him.</p>
        <p>-Mr. Kelly then called the female prisoner, who said she always liked the dead girl, and cared for her well. They were all heart-broken when she was sent back to the workhouse, and asked for her to come back. When she was ill the neighbours were asked to see her, and had free access to her. When the girl died she missed her, and that was the trouble she referred to when Wilson asked her why she jumped into the water. The Coroner said be did not like the idea of cross- examining Mrs. Burndrecl, and would refrain from doing so. He then summed up at some length, observing at the outset that there was no evidence against Joseph Burudred. He complimented Dr. O'Keefe upon having had the courage of mentioning his suspicions to the police. But for him this inquiry would never have been made. Dealing with the question of motive, he pointed out that Mrs. Burndred was in arrear on all her policies. Sarah Ann Jones living was a great encumberance to somebody, but Sarah Ann Jones dead was a very useful asset to somebody. it was not likely that the girl had taken the poison herself, and who was the most likely person to administer it?</p>
        <p>Th jury, after half an hour's consideration returned a verdict that Sarah Ann Jones died from arsenical poisoning, and that there was sufficient evidence to send the prisoner Ellen Burndred for trial on the charge of wilful murder.</p>
        <p>Mr. Burndred was thereupon committed for trial on the coroner's warrant. She and her husband will be brought before the magistrates on Tuesday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>EVENING CLASSES.—These classes, which have been arranged by the Runcorn Rural Education Committee, commenced on Monday evening. A fair number of students presented themselves and during the evening several representatives of the Local Education Com- mittee visited the classes and found everything going on satisfactorily. BAND OF HOPE.—A tea party and public meeting in connection with the Hill Cliffe and Stockton Heath United Band of Hope, were held in Hill Cliffe school- room on Monday evening, when many members and friends attended. The meeting was presided over by the Rev. J. S. Hughes (president of the society) who was supported on the platform by Rev. Joseph Manning (Warrington), Mr. A. H. Dunn and Mr. G. Whitfield (secretor)). The Chairman, in opening the meeting, extended a hearty welcome to all, and said he trusted the coming session would be even more successful than those previously held. A good programme had been provided, including recitations by May Vernon, Edith Cole, Mabel Spencer, Lily Hall, Harold Jones and Misa Annie Woods; songs by Elsie Whitfield, May Isherwood, Minnie Lightfoot, Masters Stanley Jones and T. Rich- mond, and also by Miss Kershaw and Miss Wilson, of Warrington. A dialogue was well given by Joseph and George A. Whitfield, and Mr. F. Sherbum contributed several 'Selections on the gramophone which were highly appreciated. During the evening an address was given by the Rev. Joseph Manning (the newly appointed Wesleyan minister) in the course of which he asked the children to pay strict attention to all that was taught in the Band of Hope and to endeavour to live up to its principles. In speaking to the adults he appealed to them to work individually and as members of Christian churches to help forward the temperance cause. On the proposition of Mr. Dunn, secondect by Mr.Brereton. a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to all who had contributed towards the success of the gathering. Mrs. Hughes, Misses Jones and Moreton and Master Whitfield acted as accom- panists. Through the kindness of a friend, apples were distributed amongst the children as they left the room.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>PIPE IN-STEAD OF DINNER.</head>
        <p>A PIPE IN-STEAD OF DINNER.</p>
        <p>"I can't stop to eat now. 111 have a pipe instead." That is how men ruip their digestions. Hurried meals, with too many pipes or the deadly cigarette, are the Bane of the Busy. "Continual smoking and irregular eating did me more harm than anything else," said the pier-master at Weston.</p>
        <p>"I was exposed to all weathers and took my meals when I could get them. Often I went without • food many hours, and returned home wet through. My con- stitution weakened and my digestion failed. A friend advised Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people, and before I had taken three boxes I felt much better. I continued them, and soon was quite well again. Being a heavy smoker, I thought my pipe caused the mischief "But that was not all. Recently, when I had a bad attack of sciatica, Dr. Williams' pink pills gave me immediate relief."</p>
        <p>The pier-master, Mr. John Carlsen, lives at Quarry- bank-terrace, Weston, near Runcorn. His wife had also an experience to relate of the Same medicine. "Some years ago," she said, "I met with a cycling accident. My right arm was much hurt. "For ever so long after, it remained troublesome, 1 and a touch of rheumatism made it very painful. Eventu- ally I tried Dr. Williams' pink pills, and I benefited very much." Mrs. Carlsen added that two young adies in the district who suffered from atmmia took a few boxes of Dr. Williams' pink pills upon her advice, and before long were cured. "We also know," said Mrs. , Carlsen, "a man who was inclined to be consumptive, and after a fair trial of Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale ' people, he speaks in the highest praise of them." j These are some of the many uses of the pills. They i cure by making blood. Anwinia, which is simply blood- ; lessness ; consumption, which often follows neglected amemia; and mere weakness and lack of energy, which , result from lack of sufficient blood, are cured by Dr. I• Williams' pink pills as the natural result of the new blood the pills give. Ailments arising from impure blood, the commonest of which are rheumatism, eczema, and skin diseases, scrofula, chronic erysipelas and the . like, are cured because Dr. Williams' pink pills purify and feed the blood; and disorders like fits, paralysis, I locomotor ataxy, sciatica, nervous headache and • neuralgia' are cured by them because they feed the nerves through the blood. They not only cure indigestion, but enable the stomach to get good out of the food ' eaten. They go post free for two and ninepence a box from Dr. Williams' medicine Co., Holborn-viaduct, j London; but can be had at all medicine shops, if the purchaser will resolutely refuse substitutes.</p>
      </div>
