      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR ' dI d t any grounds of complaint on his art = r{iehi,..%.'n'ede ia's been hit harde; back with p the. --.......---- -------. -,N,-.^........• result that the Rec. H. Dunnico has proved his state- ments to be practically correct, as regards Independent c..IOII,RUSPOND Labour Party funds. ''flie member of Parliament signs -- an agreement and gets fifty pounds as an instalment of Authors of letters intended for publication must in . the £2oo.Many trades unionists and members even of the all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor Independent Labour Party, agree with Mr.Dunnico that as a guarantee of good faith. it is unjust to extract a pledge under such conditions. Matter intended for publication must be written 071 We want independent men, to do right without fear. one side °nit/ of the paper and sent in, if possible, not, Wire-pulling never did any good either in religion or later than Thursday. ' politics and never will. In conclnsion I would advise -- Councillor Stott not to be so thin-skinned.—Yours, REPT:TINA-CAN.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>The Manchester Football League 1 and its Treatment of Members. Sir,—Two salsons ago, two young men from Latchford nanYmi Herbert Smith and Lewis Griffiths ; signed on to pity for Broadheath, and assisted them in a few matches during the season. Last year they did not play for the.,Club at all, in fact, they knew that Broadheath as a club had ceased to exist, and another , team with just a sprinkling of the Broadheath players had been formed at Altrincham, and called the Altrinc- ham Club. The field was close upon two miles away from the old one. The secretary, the committee, the ground, and the name were all chmaed. This season the two young men have signed on iorOughtrington Park. Herbert Smith has played in two matches, but both of the young men are now objected to by Altrincham and neither of them have been allowed to play flr the park since the 10th inst. I have written to Mr. Motht the league secl ()tarp who informs me "That both of them are on the retained list for Altrincham and that so far as the league is concerned, Altrincham and Broad- heath are the same." Can any of Yournumerous readers inform me under what football law, players may be bandied about from one team to another without'their knowledge or consent? Neither of the young fellows were consulted about the matter, nor did they know that Altrincham hat' any claim upon them. If this kind of thing is to be sanctioned by the league, young men had better be very careful about signing on for any team,or they ratty find that having signed on for Newton, they will be on the retained 'list for Sale next year, and the following year may require a transfer from NVarriagton. This is surely a sort of-White slavery and the fact will be an eye-opener to many, as it certainly is to me.—Yours faithfully,</p>
        <p>S. H. J.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Rev. H. Dunnico and the Labour</head>
        <p>The Rev. H. Dunnico and the Labour</p>
        <p>_ Party. Sir,—l had not intended to enter this controversy,but some observations which have been made on the character of the Rev. H.Dunnico prevent me from remaining silent. From the tone of virtuous indignation with which his opponents write, one wouldiimagme thathe was deliber- ately misrepresenting them and their parties; in short, that he wa.s lying in'the interests of the Liberal party. lam not disposed to write a lengthy letter upholding the position which the rev, gentleman occupies—he is quite capable of defending it 'himself—but I dothink that his utterances respecting the representation of the people, both muniaiFtlly and politically, are well under- stood and respected by those who know them. lam surprised that Councillor Stott should have been pleased to question Mr. Dunnico's sincerity because 1 was present at a meeting held at the Baptist Chapel, not many weeks ago, when he eulogised, in no measured terms, the rev. gentleman's social work in Warrington. Why, then, this Change of front? Has Mr. Dunnico's attitude towards the great social questions altered? I think not. Seeing, therefore,' that he still holds the same views and proclaims the same principles, there should be no necessity for Councillor Stott to suggest that he is now hostile to the interests of labour. If Councillor Stott or any other gentleman makes a per- sonal attack upon another public man, and questions the accuracy of his utterances in the Press and else- where, it would be more becoming, instead of insinuat- ing that he is 'dishonest' and 'untruthful,' and to term him unreliable," inconsistent' and, last but not least, "a paid agitator,' to accept a challenge and so test the truthfulness of such assertions. I have no quarrel with Councillor Stott, but I do•think that his refusal to meet Mr. Dunnico in open debate, after what has been said, is a matter of regret to some, at least, of his many friends. In promoting a reform of any kind the desire of every man should be to advance the public good, on the princi- ples of universal equity and the general interest of the community. We'Xre'too apt, now-a-days, to dwell upon the degeneracy and corruption of the world instead of realizing that life is just what we make it and it is very necessary that we ourselves, individually, as well as collectively, should determine—so far as in us lies—to change the order of things and to remodel the systems which have been allowed to enter our public life and for many years to debase it. We may be deluded into thinkin*, that, after all, the world is very common- place, cold and ungenial, and that it is losing its attractiveness. If that' s so, no Party which endeavours to establish itself with the intention of reorganising society can succeed if it does not include in its pro- gramme the common good of all classes,—the upper and lower as well as the middle—and.so help to mould them into one. lam anxious to learn all that I can of such a move- ment—a movement which will someday take hold of the people and compel them to give a patient and sympathetic ear to its tenets ; and so I have followed, from time to time, the correspondence in your columns and those of your contemporaries, but must confess that up to now, my anxiety has not been appeased. It is quite apparent that the Labour Repre- sentation movement is not, as at present constituted,the one I have been looking for. And one must admit, I think, that a party which in its limitations and narrow- ness prevents a man like Mr. John Burns from joining its ranks, as Mr. Dunnico has already pointed out, is not a desirable acquisition to the realms of practical politics. The desire for creating a new 'political party is making slaves of its representatives. You must do • this, you must not do that ";—no matter how good or bad the cause may sooner or later repel con- scientious and educated men from its ranks. There has been too much of that in the past. If we speak of in- dependence let us by all means be independent,and not be tied down to any system which takes !away our free- dom of thought and action. Doubtless,many wilhat once,say that the Conservative and Liberal Parties tie down their members in exactly thel same way. Unfortunately, that may be so. But allowing that, there is no necessity, surely, for another Party to be inaugurated on the same lines. The curse of to-day is unquestionably the fact that monopoly has not only secured the merchandise of the people but has also to a very large extent acquired the brains of some of its best representatives. No measure no matter how good it may be, can be expected now-a-days to have any reasonable amount of success unless it has its party backing. Instead of it being discussed and considered entirely on its own merits, the 'pros and oons' of its authors are reckoned of more importance. Party-feel- ing is the cause of such miserable and contemptible attempts at reform. And that is why it is necessary that, for the public good, men should be allowed to advance their own views, boldly and fearlessly, for free and open discussion, and that they should not be tied down to the beck and call of any party which by its promised advancements or monetary consider- ations has secure'd their slavery and support. And now, sir, I hope I have made myself sufficiently clear, and trust that I may be pardoned for obtruding myself in what, after all, has been a regrettable mis- understanding on the part of some of the friends of the Labour movement. lam satisfied, from what I know of Mr. Dunnico, that he has the well-being of the worker at heart and that he does sacrifice much on his behalf. I have tried to steer clear of giving offence to any I individual, and if I have spoken out against some aspects of the Labour Representation movement,l have done so purely and solely from a strictly fmoral and social standpoint and not with the intention of ad- vancing the claims of any other Partv.—Yours truly, EDMUNIS OSBORNE. 29, Lovely-lane, Warrington, September 26th, 190 t.</p>
        <p>Sir —ln looking through the correspondence column in last Saturday's 'Examiner " I find-there are letters from " Fairplay "and Councillor Stott. First, I will try and deal with" Fairplay " in his remarks about the benefit we have received from the Church schools. And by the way, it is evident that he wishes to pose as an extremely pious Christian. Now I assume he has got his education in one of these so-called Church schools, that have been the charitable gifts of Christians like unto himself. We will not argue the point here, but we know some of these schools are stolen property. How Christlike his letter begins! Why should he /advise Councillor Stott not to accept the challenge to meet in debate? And why should this extremely pious person be so desirous to cause a quarrel between two gentlemen, who no doubt respect each other? Novv if "Fairpla,y" knows anything i of the Rev. H. Dunnico he knows it s not his desire to cross swords with anyone who honestly states hi views on any subject. I have read many letters of the Rev. Mr. Carleton's, and have been benefited by many things he has said. Still I disagree with him in nine points out of ten, and who has been more out-spoken than the Rev. H. Dunnico against the Education Act? Might I not ask" Fairplay " how it is that the Rev. Mr. Carleton has not crossed swords with him. And if I was as' presumptuous as " Fairplay " I would say he dare not. But how silly and simple it would be tolsay so or to attribute that was the reason. I have been more or less connected with the Baptist Church for many years and was not aware that four-fifths of the con- grega,tion had had their education from these so called charitable Church schools. And I would like this assum- ing lover of fair play to give his authority for his statement. Ido not take it for granted by any means, I claim that he has no right to force his so-called charity on me against my will. I would ask" Fairplay " what induced him to strike a person who was having very hard blows hurled at him from several other sources? Was it to show the special mark of British love of fair- play? I would also remind him that Councillor Stott entered into this affray of his own free will, and went a considerable distance out of his way to do so."Fairplay's" remarks about passive resisters in their speeches and writings not having alluded to the fact that children need not have religious instruction is veritable bosh, and proves him to be willfully blind. With regard to Councillor Stott 's letter, I have read the letters both from himself and the Rev. H. Dunnico</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Rev. W. Dunnico's Reply to</head>
        <p>The Rev. W. Dunnico's Reply to</p>
        <p>Councillor Stott,</p>
        <p>Sir.—The letter of Councillor Stott which appeared in your issue of Saturday last calls for little comment. It is typical of the man and his party and merely proves that there is no political alchemy by which you can get golden conduct out of leaden instincts. One hardly knows whether to treat the reply with amusement or disgust. If it were at all possible to treat Councillor Stott as a serious politician then the latter treatment is what it deserves. One would imagine from the num- ber of letters " anonymous " and otherwise which appear from time to time in your columns that there were no other people in existence save those in mem- bership with the Independent Labour Party. Syl little boys with tin cans may and do make .a big noise but after all they are only little boys with tin cans. Such letters represent not the common sense of the majority but the deviations from common sense of a numerically small but factious minority and carry with them their own refutation. I shall now limit my attentions to Councillor Stott,not ,because he has proved himself more warthy of them than others, but because he has been put forward as the "competent authority "and "official representative" as well as the" goliath " of the local Socialist panty. If he has an overwhelming sense of his incompetency at the present moment, and I have no doubt he has, then he must blame his admiring colleagues who have so glaringly over-estimated his capabilities. The three questions I invited your correspondent to discuss were questions that he and other embers of the" party" had publicly criticised and pronounced to be inaccurate and I consider that theyare in honour bound to publicly disprove my statements or admit their inability to do so. The only reason given for declining my invitation is, "that I have enjoyed educational facilities he (Coun- cillor Stott) has not." But surely that is no crime. I started life as lowly if not more lowly than Councillor Stott, but instead of wasting my time at street corners, pandering to ignorant prejudices and seeking to instruct people upon matters I was entirely ignorant of, I sought to do what your correspondent ought to do viz. equip myself for the position I sought to occupy. Moreover I have gained I more knowledge in the Itniversity of *" experi- ence" than I ever gained in the university of "letters" and the doors of the former are open to all alike. Still are debates won by grandiloquent language or gramma- tical correctness? Ceitainly not. A debate is won by the man who can produce facts and if this" competent authority "is so "cocksure" that I am wrong why does he not come forth armed with his official informa- tion and demolish his opponent in the sight of an admiring crowd? Councillor Stott knows perfectly well that,he cannot do so. The truth of the matter lies here.liis bold bid for party capital and personal aggrandisemdent has proved a miserable failure and. he has not sufficient courage to admit it. Another thing, here is a party claiming to be superior to all others. Its members deem themselves competent to instruct the populace; they are seeking to capture not only our Town Council but the British House of Commons and yet they are unable to find a single man in a town like 'Warrington capable of repre- senting them in debate. A party utterly unable to defend and presumably ignorant of the very principles it exists to propagate ought ".shut up shop." If any local gentleman considered it his duty to attack either my church or myself r should not hesitate !to accept any challenge he might decide to issue,and I should not ask Dr. Clifford to come down and defend my assertions. Of course I had forgotton, that the criticsef a Christian church are one thing and the ethics of the Independent Labour party are another. There is one other thing I desire to mention, and then Councillor Stott may be dismissed. Councillor Stott with his customary insolence accuses me of being a " paid agitator." Now during the last two years or so I have addressed gatherings in various parts of the country and on various subjects. Moreover, I have delivered speeches or sermons in some 20 or 25 different churches, schools and halls in Warrington apart from my own, but I have not received one single penny in return for any service rendered. Your readers will estimate Councillor Stott and his "impudent" accusations at their true value. Before I close, Mr. Editor, I desire to single out one case under discussion, and this shall serve as a sample lof all the others. According to Councillor Stott, Will Crooks has been a bona-fide Labour Representation Com- mittee candidate all along, and a statement of mine to the contrary was as usual denounced as untrue. Now I have already proved and Councillor Stott has been compelled to admit it that Crooks did not sign the Labour Representation Committee constitution until a few weeks ago and upon signing it received a cheque for £5O with a promise of an additional £5O every quarter. If Crooks were a bona-fide candidate all along as your correspondent asserts, why did the com- mittee withhold the £5O from him although the other three candidates had received theirs? The point I wish to deal with now, however, is this. Not only were the Liberals officially represented at the meeting where Mr. Crooks was adopted candidate but his official nomination was signed by Liberals. Moreover, the Liberal organization subscribed the sum of £l3O to- wards his election expenses and has contributed £6O per annum tosiards his support since. Further many promi- nent Liberals are on the executive committee,the party did its utmost to secure his return and Mr. Crooks himself has publicly testified to this effect. Your readers may now decide whose statements are reliable and whose are not. The statement with respect to Clitheree is equally misleading but I will deal with that on some future occasion. I conclude Mr. Editor by reiterating what I have previously asserted that the negative policy of the Labour Representation Committfe is narrow, exclusive and detrimental to the best interests of labour in Iparticular and the community in general and the wel- fare of the country must not be sacrificed to the de- signs of a political coterie. The workingmen have more to gain by co-operation than hy isolation and it is extremists such as Kier Hardie and his followers who are;the real enemies of labour rather than those of us who are desirous of adopting a more reasonable policy. If the workingmen are wise they will take a more active interest in their unions and prevent them from being made the cat's-paw of men, who to use the words of Mr. R. Bell,M.P. "know little if .anything about the conditions of labour, who get a living by agitation and who retard rather than promote the cause we have at heart." Such words coming from such a man cannot be ignored. I have already expressed my own opinion about the Independent Labour Party and it is wildness itself compared with the following :—John Burns M.P., says "I denounce the arrant frauds that under the name of Independent Labour are going about the country doing everything to disintegrate Labour and Trades Unionism." Henry Broadhurst M.P., says" I ask you in the great and sacred cause for which I have lived my wholelife . . . to hound these creations from our midst." W. H. Massingham says: "The Independent Labour Party is without funds, without stability, with- out even a consistent programmeits leader Kier Hardie never a force in Trade Unionism has neither tact nor economic knowledge, neither possesses nor has ever practised a Parliamentary Policy." lihave many others opinions but these will suffice and I will conclude by giving a list of the Labour Members occupying seats in the House of Commons. Firstly those who have signed the constitution (purely nega- tive) and are in receipt of £2OO per annum in addition to the salary received from their Unions and other sources, and secondly those who have refused to sell their liberty for two hundred bits of gold and deserve the thanks of all honest men. Those who have signed the constitution : P J. K. Hardie elected 1892 X David T.Shackleton „ 1902 X Arthur Henderson „ 1903 William Crooks 1903 Aggregate Parliamentary life, 16 years Average parliamentary life, 4 years</p>
        <p>X Already 'C,iolated the constitution by taking part in the Devonport election. —Announced to appear on a Liberal platform. P Ploughing his lonely furrow. Those who have refused to sign the constitution. Thomas Burt elected 1874 Richard Bell I, 1900 John Burns „ 1892 J. Wilson • ~ 1885 W. Abraham(Mabon) , f 1885 C. Fenwick ~ • 1885 Henry Broadhurst I I 1880 J. P. Nanneth If 1900 George Harwood, M.A. , 1895 Aggregate Parliamentary life, 140 years Average Parliamentary life, 151 years I leave your readers to form their own conclusion, The list speaks for itself and gives the lie direct to the accusation that a man is the enemy. of labour because he ventures to criticise such a mistaken and suicidal policy.—Yours &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>HERBERT DUNNICO.</p>
