        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, October 1, 1904. GRAND LIBERAL BAZAAR. I, ASSOCIATED CHAMBERS OF. duty had, however, davolved upon him. He thanked Mr. Levenstein very much for what he had 'so kindly COMMERCE. said about the progressive policy (he thought he might call it so) of the concern—the progressive ideas in I regard to their manufactures and progress in the matter I of employment of which Mr. Levenstein had spoken so kindly, and which he might say the whole of the DELEGATES' VISIT TO WARRINGTON Board took a great interest, and he thought he might YESTERDAY, add, a great pride. (Hear, hear.) It was very kind of those representing the associated chambers of commerce to come there and the firm felt deeply honoured that so The firm of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons large a section of that body representing the whole Limited was honoured yesterday by the visit of a large commerce of the country should do them the honour of deputation from the Associated Chambers of Corn- paying them a visit that morning. Things said about merce of Great Britain—an association which is the soap trade varied so much. On the one hand, as he holding a special autumn meeting in Manchester. had often heard it put by his father in the old Idays— Sectional visits to places of interest and importance in next to the clergy—(laughter)—as cleanliness came !! the district had been arranged, and it speaks well for next to Godliness—and on the other hand he was sorry Ito say he had heard it classed with the manufacture of the popularity of. Messrs J. Crosfield and Sons I pills. (Laughter.) People said advertisements were Limited that the firm was an easy first in securing the nuisances and a trouble, like pill manufacturers were in greatest number of acceptances from members of this the daily press of the country. He was afraid they most important b)dy. would have to lbear with the advertisements of soap The deputation—about eighty in number—arrived makers for a very long time to come—so l