to that magnificent organisation and disciplined works of Messrs. Crosfield and Sons. (Applause). Their first duty was to express thanks for the hospitality which had been great and still greater had been the kindness of Messrs. Crosfield and Sons in throwing open to them their wonderful works. They were accustomed to admire the Germans but with regard to those premises there was nothing in Germany so self-contained. Even the band and choir were trained in the works and the band was good enough to go to London to compete for the Crystal Palace prize and he hoped they would get the first prize. (Applause.) Those who were not technically educated perhaps did not appreciate as much as one who had a fair knowledge oof it, the conditions of the place. He might be allowed to assure them that that place was as highly organised as well disciplined as any works he had ever seen. 'Those who were accustomed to see works must have been struck with the happiness of the workpeople. ' (Applause.) That was again due to Messrs. Crosfield's. It was marvellous what the firm had done not only to make the people do their ordinary work, but to raise their tone and character and spirit. They ' found in each place an arrangement made ' not only for the cleanliness of the workpeople but also for their social amusement. (Hear, hear.; The hall in which they were enjoying their repast was really an assembly room for the workpeople to read and discuss and even dances were arranged there. 'Con- sidering the liberal way in which the workpeople were treated ho was not surprised that workpeople were anxious to come there. The firm employed more than 2,000 hands but still the firm could not find employment for the numerous applications which were received from outside people. British manufacturers were always charged with doing their work by rule of thumb while the Germans,did their work scientifically,but there was not a more scientific works than the works they had seen that day. Use was made of everything made known by science. It had surprised him, he never ex 3 pected such a sight. He told his friend Dr. Markel he was only glad he was not his (the speaker's)competitor —(laughter)—because he had made use of every little saving that could be discovered by science. After seeing those works they would Is convinced that they were not so much behind in technical education or in science. It was largely legislation which interfered with the progress of their industries. In the name of Chambers of Commerce he thanked the firm for their hospitality and also their technical manager, Dr. Markel. (Applause.) Mr. A. H. CROSFIELD, in acknowledging the compli- ment, said he had tried to persuade his brother who had now the:most active control of the concern, or Dr. Markel, to reply to what Mr. Levenstein had said. The</p>
