      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>"The British Isles."</head>
        <p>"The British Isles."</p>
        <p>ARRANGEMENTS NOW COMPLETE,</p>
        <p>The hand-book of the grand Liberal Bazaar to be held in the Parr Hall, Warrington, on Wednesday, Thursday and Friday of next week, has now been published at the " Examiner" office and will be on sale in the streets on Mondafnext. It is a carefully compiled broguire of over one hundred pages with artistically designed cover, making altogether a souvenir that every Liberal in Warrington will be 1 proud to possess. The pages are interspersed with photographs of the officials and it contains a complete list of the stallholders and particulars of the many side-shows, concerts and entertainments. The history of local Liberalism is written up-to-date and should prove a valuable record of political Warfare and Liberal activity in the town. The price of the hand- book is threepence. From our front page announcements our 'readers will be able to glean full particulars of the bazaar's great attractions. They will see that on Wednesday, the first day, the opening ceremony will be performed by the Hon. Mrs., Emmott Barlow, that Sir J. T. Brunner Bart, M.P.' will take the chair and will be 'supported by Messrs. J. Emmott Barlow, M.P., Arthur H. Crosfield, and others. On Thursday, the ladies' I day, Mrs. Nicholson, who will be known to many of our I readers as a relative of Mr. Arthur H. Crosfield, and as wife of Mr. C. N. N:cholson, the Liberal candidate for Doncaster Division, will officiate, and Mrs. Ziba Armitage will take the chair on that occasion. On the third day, Mr. W. H. Lever will renew his acquaintance with Warrington and Mr. C. E. Parker, I the President of the Liberal Association, will act as chairman. The Liberal candidate for 'Kuntsford Division, Mr. A. J. King, will support him. There are too many other interesting features con- nected with the bazaar to mention here. We shall have to leave it to our readers to see for themselves the many interesting and pleasing features of the bazaar. We would, however, like to draw the attention of our many Irish readers to the fact that Mrs. Crosfield has been -very active in Dublin during the past week buying Irish manufactures for the Irish stall. This kindly act of Mrs. Crosfield's is further evidence of the active sympathy she, like other members of die Crosfield family, his for the cause of progress in the good old town of Warrington.</p>
        <p>[To THE EDITOR OF THE EXAILINER.] Sir,—l should like to draw your attention to the fact that the printers of the Liberal Bazaar poster have much to answer for. I, myself, got wet through yesterday in staying to read it. Casually stopping to take notice of the contents, I read on and on about the Bazaar until I reached the bottom of it and then realised that I had got a' soaking! They should nofbe allowed to put so much interesting matter on one bill. Further than that, two friends of mine refused to go back to work until they had read it, and so lost a quarter. lam told that there was an acrobatic troupe standing on each other's shoulders in one part of the town, busily engaged in devouring its many delightful and enticing particulars. I hope you will bear this letter in mind.—Thanking you 'in anticipation, Yours &amp;C.. ONE WHO'S GOING.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>MAYORALTY.</head>
        <p>THE MAYORALTY.</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN BOLTON ACCEPTS OFFICE FOR ANOTHER YEAR.</p>
        <p>Alderman William Bolton is destined to occupy the Mayoral chair for another year. On Monday afternoon a caucus meeting of the Town Council was held at the Town Hall, under the presidency of Alderman Francomb, and, on the motion of Alderman Pierpoint, seconded by Alderman Hutchinson, it was unanimously decided to invite Alderman Bolton to accept the office for a second term. His Worship intimated his willingness to again undertake the duties. Alderman Bolton has been a member of the Council continuously since 1882, and has taken a great interest in municipal work. On the formation of the Education Committee he was appointed chairman of the Sites and Buildings Sub-committee which has in hand the important work of providing additional school accommodation. At the same meeting it was decided to fill the alder- manic vacancies in November by the re-election of the retiring aldermen --Mr. William Pierpoint, for Whitecross Ward; Mr. James Fairclough, for Bewsey Ward; Mr. Thomas H. Sutton, for St. John's Ward; and Mr. Arthur Bennett for St. Austin's Ward.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>Having now had two practise games, the Heathen this afternoon open upon their series of matches. During the season most of the leading teams of the North will be encountered.</p>
