        <p>• 21. Western , 28 South Manchester.-- away Feb. 4 Alderley Edge • 11 Huyton home „ 18 Chester away , 25 Wrexham away Mar. 4 Timperley away „ 11 New Brighton away „ 18 The Royal „ 25 Alderley Edge away Apl. 1 West Derby away The following teams are selected to represent Stockton Heath in the first match against Bankfield (near West Derby). First team: R. G. Pierpoint, goal; E. E. Woods and L. Fairclough, backs; H. G. Hatton, E. Pierpoint and J. Richardson, halves; R. Fairclough, W. Richardson, R. Pierpoint, S. Baxter and C. H. Bond, forwards. Referee, Mr. Bellamy. Train leaves Central Station 1-50. Reserve, A. N. Ather. Second team: J. Gray, goal • Egerton Fairclough and P. B. Hawtin, backs; A. liodgkinson, Eric Fan- dough and Smith, halves; D. Skelton, R. Monks, W. Longdin, A. Ferguson and W. Howard, forwards. To be played at Grappenhall-road. Bully off 3 o'clock. The above two teams should enable the club to open its season with a dual victory, seeing that the Bankfield club was only formed this season. The principal business at the annual general meeting of the Hockey Association, which was held on Thurs- day evening in London under the chairmanship of Mr. E. H. Nash, arose from the proposition of Mr. H. J. Greening (hon. secretary of the Mid-Counties Hockey Association) to pay the travelling expenses of all players in international matches. In the past no ex- penses of any description have been pad, but the diffi- culty which was experienced last season in raising representative teams to visit Bath, Dublin, and Glas- gow on successive Saturdays, brought into prominence the matter of at least allowing the cost of travelling.— The motion was opposed by the Western Counties H.A., who considered that its adoption would form the thin end o f the wedge, but it was eventually carried.— H.R.H. the Prince of Wales was re-elected president of the Association. Mr. H. M. Tennett, the old Oxford captain, succeeds Mr. Frampton as hon. s4cretary.—The accounts showed a balance in hand of ,c 132 15s, 7d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-10-01_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>KUBELIK AT THE PARR HALL.</head>
        <p>KUBELIK AT THE PARR HALL.</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football. • (By " Ovalist.") Warrington played flue football last Saturday and completely annihilated the League Champions, Brad- ford, on the Wilderspool ground. The result--or at any rate the magnitude of the victory—was entirely unexpected, for the homesters were not at full strength and in the past two or three seasons have cut up rather badly against Park Avenue men. In football, however, as in fighting "You don't know what you can do till you try," and Warrington certainly tried hard enough to bring about the overthrow of the Champions. They made up for the absence of two or three regular players by an added dash and vigour which quite staggered the visitors. Five points were obtained in the first ten minutes, and from this pint Warrington never looked back or lost their command of the game. When the referee's whistle finally sounded Warrington had scored 2 goals, 3 tries (thirteen points) to nil.</p>
        <p>Kubelik! That name seems to possess a magic spell over the public at the present tinie. It was sufficient to induce at least one thousand residents of Warrington and district to brave the unpropitious elements of last evening for the purpose of seeing the possessor of it and forming their own judgment of a violinist of whose fame they have heard so much. And what kind of man is he? He steps on the platform with a quick and springing tread and makes a dignified bow to the audience. Standing before you is a young man of medium height and somewhat slender build, with a pleasing and open countenance and ,a huge shock of hair as a crowning feature. As he plays on his violin you are struck with the ease and facility with 'which he manipulates the instrument and produces the sweetest of notes that entrance the audience. It is art concealing art and ds the music grows upon the audience in its strained attention to catch every movement, the end of the piece comes all too quickly: Last night, Kubelik introduced his audience to some choice excerpts from the old masters. Of those of more recent date, perhaps the most interesting. was the "Bohemian Dance " of Alberto Randegger, junr. To this piece, Kubelik did full justice. There was a swing and rhythm which was infectious, and at times one almost heard the patter of the feet of the dancers. The audience cheered vociferously at the close and would not be denied an encore. Sitting next to the, writer was a musical critic who cheered not. "An over- estimated man" was his verdict. But the audience cheered on, and renewed their applause as Kubelik bowed his acknowledgments. "How many of them really understand music I wonder," said the critic in an undertone. The audience continued to demand an encore nevertheless. and were not satisfied until Kubelik complied with a final piece. The pianoforte accompaniments were played by Herr Ludwig Schwab to whose brilliant instrumentatiln a share of the success achieved must be generously attributed. Miss Katharine Goodson made her first appearance in War- rington as a pianist and created a very favourable</p>
        <p>Before the commencement of the game there was decidedly more confidence among the few Bradford supporters than in the minds of the Warrington enthusiasts. At the last minute it was announced that R. Bate, the reserve half back in place of Di Davies, was ill, and that both the half back positions would be filled by "novices." J. Davies, the new Welsh half, and Brooks were deputed to operate behind the scrummage, while Hockenhall was put on the wing. This arrangement allowed of some re- adjustnient if necessary, but a change was never required. The new half backs acquitted themselves like men and were in no way below the standard required for the Warrington senior team. Brookes even scored the two first tries, and altogether the achievement of these two players under such difficult conditions was remarkable. Neither showed any nervousness, though the opponents were last season's League champions. Needless to say they got many a cheer during the afternoon.</p>
        <p>Bradford were at full strength and confident in their ability to wipe out the Warringtonians once more, forgetful probably of the little affair at Hudders- field last April when Warrington showed what they could do. Consequently play started with a rattle in sporting parlance. In plain English neither side lost any time in getting to grips with the other. Bradford are as fine a body of men as any team now playing, and they looked powerful enough to eat the wire pullers." But the latter are wiry both by name and nature, and soon proved the quality of their" metal." Bradford had slightly the better of the opening stages, and had Mosby shown any goal-kicking ability they might have been two paints to the good very early on. Warrington cleared through Hallam and Fish, the latter of whom seemed particularly lively. After accounting nicely for the burly Dechan, he did a " turn " on his own by which the Bradford defence was almost pierced. Gunn thought he had his Fish nicely bottled but he was wrong, and the slippery one would have been over the line in another stride but for the arrival of reinforcements from the enemy's side. The crowd fairly gasped at the audacity of the left-winger's effort, but when from the nest scrummage young Brooks tipped the ball over the line and fell on it near the posts their pent-up excitement found full vent. Fish obliged with a goal, and a Bradford gent with a white hat who stood near me looked somewhat sick. Marsden made great efforts to get his three-quarters on the move after this and though he succeeded the passing was too much on orthodox lines to cause very serious trouble. On the other hand J. Davies twice tricked the opposing halves very cleverly and only Gunn's_smartness in capturing the ball saved his side from further reverses.</p>
        <p>On re-starting the visitors seemed to realise that they must get even at once or their goose was cooked so to speak. Marsden, Eagers and Dunbavin all got within speaking distance of the home line but the tackling of Dickenson and Hockenhall on the right was safe and sound. Warrington soon rushed the ball away, and then followed a fine round of passing by the home backs who made a lot of progress on the right and then transferred to the forwards who carried play right to the line. Again was th) fort captured before the defenders could realise what had happened. Brooks took a rapid transfer from his fellow-half and romped over, no goal following. Though eight points now separated the teams there was no slackening. Bradford again attacked hotly but fruitlessly. Play veered back to centre again and here Fish secured and surpassed himself by a corkscrew run through the thick of the Bradford men. He appeared certain to be tackled and the onlookers could hardly believe their eyes when Fish emerged clear from pursuit and planted the ball neatly between the posts. It was a superb try such as only " the irrepressible "could have scored —and a goal followed as a•matter of course. I think the tale ought to end here. Play proceeded for some minutes longer, but it was tame io comparison with that glorious moment,and therA was no doubt that the better team had won by a clear thirteen points and that the champions had sustained their first defeat.</p>
        <p>It is a customary thing for the supporters of the winning team to remark after a.match "What a fine game it has been ! " There were plenty of such expressions of opinion on Saturday, but they were justified whatever their source. It was a fine game, and both teams played fast, open football, such as the crowd loves to see. In fact there are many people who contend that Bradford played as good football as Warrington, but that assertion I beg to contradict. The visitors certainly played quite as open and attrac- tive a game as their conquerors, but the attractiveness was not allied to effectiveness, and in this respect Warrington undoubtedly played better football—as the score itself shows. Bradford relied too much on stereotyped methods, hand-to-hand passing across the field, which is rarely dangerous. Warrington, on the other hand, showed more ingenuity and resource. Their method of attack was never twice the same in successive onslaughts, with the result that they soon found a weakness in the visitors' line of defence. Brad- ford will not be champions this season by playing football of the orthodox type seen on Saturday. Perhaps they learnt something by the Wilderspool encounter.</p>
        <p>Speaking of individuals, Marsden, the visiting skipper, was easily the best man on the Bradford side. He strove hard to put a better complexion on the game, and if his centres had followed the same go-ahead tactics they might have done some good. Instead they tried the running round process against speedier opponents, with the inevitable result—thrown in touch! Both Eagers and Mosby are big enough to break through occasionally, and they ought to try. Gunn did good service in the rear, and rumour has it that he was too helpless with laughter at his outwitted club-mates to tackle Fish when the latter put the finishing touch on the afternoon's scoring. I have already referred to the Warrington half-backs and their excellent performance. Among other rear-men Fish was the shining light, and appeared in tip-top form, in fact I have not seen him so keen and active for some time. He was quite his old self—and that suffices. Isherwood and Dickenson did yeoman service in checking the Bradford inroads, but they have hardly yet got into the old combination. Hallam also showed improved form, hile the forwards to a man were heroes. They worried the breath out of their bulky opponents, and throughout had the best of matters. It was a great performance on their part. •</p>
        <p>Suspensions are getting common. Morrison and Laidlaw were sent off on Saturday, and Warrington have now three good men " strung up." Last season they never had a man reported ! I see that Morrison's sentence covers to-day and next Saturday, while Preston and Di. Davies are at liberty after to-day.</p>
        <p>Leeds, away, is the engagement for this afternoon, and Warrington will require to repeat last week's form to secure the spoils. They have certainly a severe task before them but there is both confidence and determination in the ranks. Leeds last week</p>
        <p>PROVISIONS ARE DEARER.—Advt.</p>
        <p>, impression. Her interpretation of the "Mare" , drubbed Salford at the Willows and are conse- from Tannhauser " (Wagner-Liszt) was in every quently on a pinnacle at present. They will be with- way adequate. her delicacy of touch and out Littlewood, full-back, however, as he is also attention to detail being marked features of among the convicted her playing. In response to the plaudits of the i audience, she repeatedly bowed her acknowledgments and at length complied with an encore which further delighted the assembly.—The only vocalist was Miss Gough, whose tuneful interpretation of "I know a lovely garden" (Guy d'Hardelot) and the French song Nymphes et Sylvains " (Bemberg) proved a pleasing 'Variation in the programme. The arrange- ments for the concert were in the hands of Messrs. R. H. Pearson, of Warrington, and Mr. J. P. Johnson of Kendal</p>
        <p>Warrington will play Hallam ; Fish, Isherwood, Dickenson and Hockenhall ; J. Davies and Brooks ; Boardman, Thomas, Jolley, Cook, Belton, Shugais, and Coleman.</p>
        <p>• J Davies and Brooks have again been selected and may be expected to try their hardest to fulfil the con- fidence reposed in them by the team committee. Brooks requires warning against one fault which was noticeable in his play last Saturday, and that is hold- ing the ball too long after getting away from the scrummage. He must take care to give the centres possession at once after he has escaped the attentions. of the opposing half backs.</p>
        <p>Harris is now out of Mr. Allison's care and progress- ing very satisfactorily at home. He is expected to turn out fo.r,the Runcorn match ,next week.</p>
        <p>The." A " team will be at home to Widnes Rangers and will give a trial to two new centres in the persons of Barnes and Berry, both local lads, the former being a son of the famous " Tommy " Barn es The Pull team will be Knight, full back; Savage, Barnes, Berry, and Kenyon, three-quarters; Jordan and Duckworth, half backs; Edmundson, Ashton, Lunt, Harmer, and Forster, forwards.</p>
        <p>Association Football. The following are the Warrington and District League tables up-to-date:</p>
        <p>FIRST DIVISION.</p>
        <p>Goals P. W. L. D. For Agst Pts. Stockton Heath Warrington Albion 1 1 0 0 1 0 2 Warrington St. Mary's 9 1 1 0 1 1 2 Earlestown Y.IVI.C.A. Thelwalll 2 0 1 0 1 2 1 , Orford Barracks Lymm Crosfields Latchford Britannia - 0 0 ~....</p>
        <p>- , - . SECOND DEVISION. _ Stockton Heath:Res Albion Res. Widnes St. Ambrose 3 1 1 1 4 3 3 Warrington Central St. Mary's Res 3 1 1 1 3 3 3 Christ Church Crostields Res Vulcan A.F.C. St. Barnabas 3 1 2 0 7 10 2 Warrington St. Paul's 2 0 1 1 5 8 1 Daresbury Penketh 2O 1 1 2 1 1 Widnes St. Paul's Saturday's matches resulted as follows :</p>
        <p>FIRST DIVISION Thelwall nil, Warrington St. Mary's 2. Latchford Britannia v. Warrington Albion, match not played.</p>
        <p>SECOND DIVISION, Christ Church 1, Widnes St. Ambrose nil. Stockton Heath Reserve 5, Warrington Coatral 1.. Penketh nil, Crosfield's Reserve 1. Albion Reserve 3, Warrington St. Paul's 3 St. Barnabas' 2, Vulcan 5 St. Mary's Reserve 2, Daresbury nil. To-day's fixtures are : Warrington Albion v. Earlestown Y.M.C.A., referee, T. Worrall. Stockton Heath v. Thelwall, referee, W. H. Crowther. Orford Barracks v. Latchford Britannia, referee, J. Mather.</p>
        <p>Daresbury v. St. Paul's, referee, F. Foulkes. Widnes St ,Anthrose v. Stockton Heath Reserves, referee, A. Bowyer. St. Barnabas v. Christ Church, referee, H. Singleton. Penketh v. St. Mary's Reserves, referee, J. Walker. Crosfleld's Reserves v. Vulcan, referee, O. King. Central v. Widnes St. Paul's, referee, J. A. Pendry.</p>
        <p>Cycling. About a dozen members of theW arrington Club had spin to Helsby on Saturday and several continued the journey to quaint old Chester. The afternoon was beautifully fine and all thoroughly enjoyed the outing.</p>
        <p>To-day witnesses the close of the season, and Knuts- ford will be the destination.</p>
        <p>The concluding run for 1904 in connection with the, Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Club took place on Saturday. The pretty L illage of Arley was visited, about a dozell. members turning out. Lighting up time to-night, 6.49.</p>
        <p>Water Polo. Warrington second sustained another reverse at the hands of the Chester first team on Monday. The match, was played at the City Baths, Warrington being repre- sented by the following :—S. Walker, E. Bayley; 3. Warren, C. Turner, Howard, J. Rathbone, Pemberton. In the first half, the visitors defended the deep end. Chester attacked with vigour, but for a time Walker kept his goal in safety. The bombardment continued, and at the interval Chester led by three goals to nil. On resuming Warrington became aggressive, but the forwards did not take full advantage of the opportuni- ties which presented themselves. At length Rathbone- sent in a successful shot, and as the home men could make no further headway the game ended in a victory for Chester by three goals to one.</p>
        <p>There was a large gathering of Grammar schoo students at the Warrington baths on Wednesday after noon, when the annual race for the swimming champion ship of the school took place. The distance covered was six lengths, and a keen contest resulted as follows' —1 Adams; 2, Whitehouse; 3, Turner; 4, Sutton. Adams is to be congratulated upon becoming the holder for the year of the championship belt. Mr. Barlow officiated as judge, and Mr. Carruthers and Mr. Young Easters at the school) were present.</p>
        <p>Angling. Mr. C. Higginbottom, a Warrington angler, was play- ing a perch he had hooked in the Boliin, near Lynam when a pike seized the perch and stuck to its prey so persistently that Mr. Higginbottom succeeded in landng both fish. -</p>
        <p>An open contest promoted by Mr. W. Maddock of the Boat House Inn, Agden Bridge was fished on Saturday last in the waters of the Bridgewater Canal. There were only eighty-two competitors so it cannot be said that the match was well patronised. However, fishing proved to be very good and there were some very even weights recorded. Messrs. Henry Kent, J. Wolfenden, W. Bodle and Alfred Bate acted as committee for the match. Result, Ist, J. Jolley, 11b. loz. Bdrs.; 2nd, J. Haynes, 14oz. 2drs.; 3rd, J. Speakman, 12ozs. ; 4th, J. Griffiths, lloz. 13drs.; sth, Henry Woods, 10oz. 12drs. ; 6th, .1. Shepherd, 10oz. lldrs. ; 7th, H. Moore, 10oz. 9drs.; Bth, C. Moore, 10oz. Bdrs. ; 9th, T. Armitage, 10oz. ldr. 10th, B. Dobson, 9oz. lldrs.; and 11th, J. Astill, 9oz• ldr. Anglers from Warrington practically captured all the prizes, though there were a good few competitors hailing from Broadheath, Salford and several other localities. Fifty-one competitors weighed their catches and the total weight of fish caught was exceedinglY satisfactory, the aggregate being 201 b. loz. 7drs.</p>
        <p>The members of the Latchford Angling Society fished a contest in the canal at Grappenhall on Saturday last, and in striking contrast to the above recorded but pont:, catches, only 14 contostants catching fish out of 3A engaged. Arrangements for the match were satisfac- torily undertaken by Messrs. Alf. Starkey, R. Gorton and T. Wright. Result :-Ist, A, Maddock, 10ozs. 9dr5.; 2nd, T. Plevin, Bozs. Tdrs, ; 3rd, W. W. Starkey, 60z5• 15drs. ; 4th, J. Jones; sth, W. H. Shakeshaft ; 6th, J, W. Starkey ; 7th, W. Maddock ; Bth, R. Gorton; fithp T. Caldwell; 10th, H. Thornton; 11th and 12th, a tie?. Messrs. A. Randles and G. Taylor ; 113th, J. Owen ana 14th, T. Chorley. Three prizes remained over, not being won. A special prize for the heaviest fish caught in the match was won by Mr. A. Maddock, the fish being a roach of 7ozs. 14drs. A special prize for the smallest fish caught in the match was divided between Messrs,' J. Jones and W. Maddock. The prizes were distributed on Monday evening last at the headquarters of the club, the Brook Inn, Latchford.</p>
        <p>ITEMS.—A fairly good number of local anglerS' ventured out last week end in search of sport amongst the course fish, but reports on the whole of individual fishing are only moderate. The old river at Lymm was productive of a few pike. Messrs. IVlounfield and,. Princep getting at least five. Other course fish did no' furnish much sport. A few odd catches of roach are reported from the Bridgewater Canal, though gudgeerl gave a fair amount of sport. The canal at Acton Bridge fished but very moderately; whilst the River Weaver yielded any amount of sport with small- fry,if the match fished at Barrow Lock was any criterion. The River Bolliia showed some improvement in sport, some roacil and trout being captured, whilst one angler is spoken 0 ,' as having been smashed up" on two successive days. The river continues to be very low. A few anglers report catching a few roach and trout from the river Gowy. From further afield, come two leviathan chub, respectively, 31bs. 4ozs. bOdrs. and 21bs. 3ozs. 9dr5., recorded by Mr. J. Rotherham from Llanymynech. Tle contest promoted by the District Protection Society aid of the Nursing Association will be fished to-daY the waters of the Bridgewater Canal at London Bridge: Several local clubs have not quite finished their mate' programme for the season. Warrington.—Printed and published by JOHN WALK% &amp; Co., at The Examiner General Printing Wor Bewf:sy-street, and published by them at the sanle place and also at The Examine Offices,Victoria-rouki. WIDNES: 32, George-street, ST. HELENS; 3, yjarke,.L.. street, EARLESTOWN; Church-street RUNCORN, °Cu Ist, 1904.</p>
