        <p>BILLIARDS. THE STROKES OF THE GAME. TiY 313.1.5C0 [COPYRIGIIT.] V. SlDE.—continued. The Effect of Side against the Nap of the Cloth. Though any player with any pretensions to good play is quite cognisant of the correct side for increasing or diminishing the angle of de. flection when playing with the nap of the cloth, it is surprising how many fairly good players (men who can make their thirties and forties and occasionally more) know that when playing against the nap, that is from the spot end of the table, side on the cue ball has just the reverse effect of what it has when playing from the baulk end with the nap, and that therefore many strokes from he spot end of the table must be played with what may be termed "reverse side," that is, the reverse side of what would be re- quired were the stroke played from the baulk end. DIAGRAM 14.</p>
