        <p>LOCAL NEWS. Appleton Rifle Club. The final competitions of the season were held at the Appleton Rifle Range on Saturday last, and resulted as follows :—Medal Handicap: Mr. A. Down, first prize of bronze medal ; Mr. F. J. Grocott, second prize. Open Sweepstakes: Mr. A. Down, first prize; Mr. C. H. Mann, second: Members' Stakes: Mr. C. H. Mann, first prize; Mr. A. Down, second. 4111 •"` di SUGAR KEEPS DEAL—Advt. niM niM Town Council. ottm. The monthly meeting of the Town Council will be held on Tuesday evening at six o'clock in. the Town Hall. Special resolutions will be moved by Councillor Stott in regard to the standing orders governing con- tracts, and by Councillor P. Peacock with regard to reduced fares on some of the tram routes.. 866151 mslo Death of Mrs. M. Whittle. MON 4tr ;imam oer,, The death occurred on Thursday of Mrs. Margaret Whittle, licencee of the "Marquis of Granby" Inn, Church-street. The deceased lady, who' was in her sixty-fourth year, had been landlady of the "Marquis of Granby" Inn for twenty-five years and the licencee for fourteen years—it being exactly fourteen years this week since her husband passed away. She had been ailing for some considerable time, the cause of death being cancer. The funeral will take place at the Cemetery to-morrow (Sunday). 7:4 rtead 111114 SUGAR is DEARER.—Advt.3II.= Ladles' Ambulance Class.! meetingiwas held at the Gymnasinm last evening at which it was decided to form an ambulance class for Warrington ladies, a number of whom have expressed a desire to be afforded the opportunity of becoming proficient in the art of nursing,- and in the rendering of first aid. Dr. Adams and. Dr. Smith have consented to take the classes, the first of which will be held at the Gymnasium next Friday week. All communications should be addressed to Councillor Hulse, Salisbury-street. ":'"H 466 M, ve.NB wrig 1111=7"flige. Choir Concert.l 14V00011 HVOOOII nampor,,,4ol The fourth annual choir concert in connection with Bold-street Wesleyan Church was held on Wednesday evening, and was well attended. An excellent pro- gramme was presented,the whole of the contributors being well received. Songs were given by Mrs. A Booth, Master H. Griffiths,Misses E. Dunn, H. Wilson,. Prince,Emily Taylor, Carrie Hastings, Millard, Mr. W. Warburton, Mr. J. G. Osborne, Mr. Page, and Mr. B. Jennings, those by Mrs. A. Booth and Miss Carrie Hastings being particularly pleasing. A quartette by Messrs. Clayton, Warburton, Page -and Osborne was encored, as were also the humorous items contri- buted by Mr. H. M. Ashton. The choir acquitted themselves admirably in the chorus "The miller's wooing" whilst the choir boys met with equal success in the part song "The choristers." Messrs. L. Richard- son and F. Hickman discharged the duties of accom- panists, Dr. D. Ferguson, presided. 'k,t,N 6465 ' SUGAR is DEARER.—Advt. FIRE GRATES of every description.—J. S. GAR ! LAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, I Bridge-street,Warringion.--Advt. age</p>
